      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>ONE PENNY</head>
        <p>ONE PENNY</p>
        <p>WORTH KNOWING</p>
        <p>That JOEIN WALK ER AND CO,, Printers, Bewsey-street. Warrington, can effectually assist you in any department of your business, having unrivalled facilities, can give you GOOD WORK, PROMPTLY, PUNCTUALLY, and at cheap rates.</p>
        <p>When next you have any</p>
        <p>PRINTING, BOOK-BINDING, OR LITHOGRAPHING consult us for prices &amp;o. Your inquiries will be cbeerfully responded to. ESTIMATES FREE. CORRESPONDENCE INVITED.</p>
        <p>ElEtI DIY, DE.C.Emi3Ert 7, 1900.</p>
        <p>The LIST OF APPLICATIONS 411 OPEN at Ten ELM., on</p>
        <p>MONDAY NEXT, the 10th inr „ and will CLOSE on or before WEDNESDAY NEXT, ' A 12th inst., at Three pm. THE WIDNES AND 11IINCORN BRIDGE COMP I.NY. Incorporated by the WI 'nes and '-;.uncorn Bridge Act, 1500.) SMARR CAPITA.L ..42£100,000 Divided into 10,000 Sb • .3 of £lO each. The directors invite application f•--..• 6,500 of the above shares at par, payable as follows On ... •.. . 21 per share. On allotment £1 per share. The remaining 2,8 per share In calls, not exceeding £3 a share, at intervals of not less than three months., Interest at 3 per cent. per annun. will be paid half-yearly, on the Ist January and Ist d, ly. da.riug canstruction on tbe amount from time to time • paid up on the shares, in accordance with and subject tt the provisions of Section</p>
        <p>49 of the Act incorporating the t:lompany. The Corporation of Widnes and th, Urban District Cornell of Runcorn have conditionally agr.,ed to subcscribe to 2.5C0 Ordinary Shares. and 1.010 Or mary Shares respectively (teeing toe remainder of the au lorised Share Capital) at par upon the terms and subject to the conditions reopen- tively contained in an agreemer. between the Corporation and this Company, dated the 3r. December, 1900, and an agreement between the District .'ounell and this Company, dated the 22ud November, 1900 LIIRECTOV.S. Sir John T. Brunner, Bart , ALP., Druids Cross, near Liver-</p>
        <p>pool (Chairman). Joseph Beecham, Esq.. S.P., St. ‘telens. H. Wade Deacon, Esq., J.P., Livt-pool. Holbrook Gaskell, ;fun., Esq., J.P. Clayton Lodge, Aigburth,</p>
        <p>Liverpool. H. Seton-Karr, 22, Sloane-gardens, London,</p>
        <p>S.W. H. Sutton TIMMiI, Esq.. J.P., Widnes. Major J. W. Waring. Widnes. ENGINEERS.—Jchn J Webster. Esq.. M. Inst. CE.. 39. Victoria-stre.t. Westminster; John. T. Wood, Esq., M.lnst.</p>
        <p>C. 14 3. Cook-etreet, Liverpool. BANICIIRS.—Parea Bank, Limitec , Liverpool, London and</p>
        <p>Branches SOLICITORS.—Messrs. Alsop. SU. 'seas. Harvey and Crooks, 14, Castle-street, Liverpool ; lacters. Oppenheim and Mal- kin St Helens and Liverpool. BROKHRS.—Messrs. Ashton, To, i,:ud Noble,?, Tithebarn-</p>
        <p>street, Liverporl. AUDITORS —3, W Davidson, Rf F.C.A., Liverpool; B Cookson. itsq., A.C.A. Liverplo: SHCRRTARY.—F. H. thables. OFFICES.-6, Castle-street, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>PROSPECT US. The Widnes and Runcorn Bridge Company has been incor- porated by Special Act of Parliam•at for the purpose of con- structing and working a Transmeeser Bridge over the River Mersey and the Manchester Ship eennal, so as to connect the towns of Widnes and 'Runcorn awe i the adjacent portions of Lancashire and Cheshire. The b Riga will he available for foot passengers, and also for aniares and vehicular traffic of every description. The population of Whines has i mreased from 6.893 in the year 1861 to about 32,000 at the p esent time, while its rate- able value has increased from Cs ,180 in the year 1871 to £150,723 in the year 1899. Widn, is the principal centre of the British Alkali Trade. and he,' also large Soap Works, Metal Rxtrac' ing works. Iron Fouheries, Ss° The population of Runcorn has increased from. 10,063 in the year 1861 to about 18.500 at the present time, while its rateable value has increased in the same interval Irons £25,993 to absut £56,310. Runcorn also possesses large Alkali and other works. and many of the workmen and others who are employed in Widnes terve their homes in Runcorn. Although the distanae between Whines anti Rencorn is only about 435 yards, there is no bridge connecting them which can be used by vehicles ; there is indeed To accommo- dation ter wheel traffic across the Mersey between the ferries at Liverpool and the bridge at Warrington, a distance of about 18 miles. The only means of communication for foot pas- sengers are the ancient Runcorn and Whines Ferry and the Foot Bridge, which runs alongside and forms part of the Runcorn Railway Bridge This foot bridge is inconveniently situated for the traffic on both sides ; it is aeout 80 feet above the River Mersey. and is approached on the Runcorn side partly by an incline and partly by a flight of 43 steps, and on the Widnes side by a steep incline; it is uncovered and exposed to the weather. Notwithstanding these incon- veniences and that the charge for crossing the foot bridge is twopence for a single journey, with day return tickets at threepence and weekly tickets at one shilling. the foot bridge is largely used. It is nrovided by the Company's act that from and after the construction and opening for public traffic of the transporter bridge and works thereby authorised, the maintenance and use ot the ferry shall tse discontinued. The necessity -for improved means of communication between Widnes and Runcorn has long been felt, but no new facilities have been afforded since the opening of the foet bridge in 1868, although the total population of the two towns has more than doubled in the interval. The proposed bridge will brave a single span of about 1,000 feet, one pier being constricted on the foreshore at the termination of Mersey-road, Whines, and the other at the commencement of Waterloo-road, Runcorn, on the south bank of the Manchester Ship Canal. It will, therefore. be a little to the eastward of the railway bridge of the London and North-Western Railway Company. and the underside will be about 32 feet above the high-water level of spring tides. The loreige itself will be e' ties ordinary suspension type, having steel cables stiffened by two longitudinel girders braced horizontally to withstand the greatest possible wind pressure. A frame on rollers will run on four lines of rails attached to the underside of the girders, and from this frame will be suspended, at a convenient height above high water mark, a transporter car, to be moved to and fro by electric or ce her mechanical power. The car, in which covered accommodation will be provided for passengers, will he about 65 feet long by 24 feet wide, and will be very conveniently available for passengers, carriages,carts, omnibuses, tramcars, traction engines, steam rollers and in fact wheel traffic of every description, as well as cyclists and animals. and as an inustration of its capacity it may be stated that it will be capable of accommodating at one time four two-horse farmers' waggons loaded with produce, and 303 passengers. The street forming the approach on each side of the river will be at the same level as the platform of the car, the maximum of comfort and convenience being thus secured. The car will travel at such a speed as to transport passengers from one side of the river to the other with greater &amp;el:oaten than would be possible over an ordinary roadway bridge. A bridge constructed and worked on the system proposed over the River Nervion. at Portemalette, near Bilbao, has been in successful operation for more than eight years, under conditions much less favourable than these which exist at Widnes. A similar bridge has been working satisfactorily over the River Seine, at Rouen. for about a year. Similar bridges have been authorised by Parliament during the last session for erection across the River Usk at Newport (Men ). and across the River Ribble below Preston. A model of the proposed structure can be inspected at the Office of Mr. J. T. Wood, C E., 3, Cook-street Liv The directors are of opinion that the bridge will command a:large ansi varied traffic, and will be used not only to supply purely local needs, but also as a means of come:turd- cation between Liverpool, St. Helens. and other L tneashire towns on the one hand, and the rich agricultural distric. of Cheshire on the other. as shown in the map accompanying the nrespeetne issue/ to the public. It will, he fact, form a new ,rnd important link betweee the two counties. The company's Act provides for the construction of a wharf at the end of the bridge on the Rencorn side alongside the Manchester Ship Canal, which will enable ocean traffic to and from Widnes to be dealt with without transhipment. A report as tothe probable traffic on the proposed bridge has been made by Mr. S. B. Cottrell, M. Inst., C.R., the manager of the Liverpool Overhead Railway, who concludes as follows :-- " I may sum up the effect of the above report by stating that in my opinion an immediate traffic from passengers, vehicles, and goods of over £12..e01 per annum may reasonably be expected, and that within a comparatively short time after the bridge has been opened that figure will be largely increased?' The engineers estimate that, the cost of maintaining and working the bridge apart from administcatiou will be substantially less than £2,001 per annum. No contract for construction or equipment of the bridge has been entered Into. but the directors have obtained es- timates from the engineers as to the probable total cost of the undertae ing. which lead them re the conclusion that the Company's share capital (£100,C00) and borrowing rowers (wilier are limited to £33,000) are amply sufficient,. The directors are. therefore, of opinion that the bridge, in addi- tion to being a great public convenience, will yield a good return as an investment' The Corporation of Whines. in exercise of a power con- ferred upon them by section 43 of the Company's Act, have agreed to subscribe for 2.5(0 ordinary shares (or £25000) at par, upon the terms of and subject t ) the conditions con- tained in an agreement dated the 3rd day of December n)o, and made between the Mayor, Aldermen. and Burgesses of the Borough of Whines of the one part and the Company of the nth,. part. The agreement provides for the nomination by the Corporation of two directors of the Company. The Urban District Council of Runcorn. in exercl.e of a similar power.have also agreed to subscribe for 1,000 ordinary shares (or £10,000) at par upon the terms of and subject ta the conditions contained in an stereement dated the 22nd day of November, 1900, and made between the District Council of the one part and the Company of the other part. The agreement provides for the nomination by the District Coun- cil of one director of the Company. No part of the capital now offered has ben or will be under- written. The Act of Parliament was obtained by and at the cost of a committee (of which all the directors are members), which has fixed the amount of the costs, charges and expenses for or in relation to the Act to be paid by the Com- pany to them at the sum of £lO,OOO. and this figure has been accepted and agreed to on behalf of the Company. Interest at the rate of 3 per cent, per annum, as authorized by Parliament, will be payable half-yearly out of cepieal on the amounts from time to time paid up on the shares of the Company during construction of the bridge. It is expected that the bridge will be completed within two years after the commencement of its erection. Copies of the Company's Act, of the agreements with the Corporation of Widnes and the Urban District Council of Runcorn, and of Mr. Cottrell's report may be inspected at the offices of the Company's solicitors. Where no allotment is made the deposit will be returned. If the amount allotted in any case is lees than that which was applied for, the balance of the deposit so far as is requisite will be applied towards the payment on allotment. It is intended to apply for a quotation on the Liverpool Stock Exchange. Prospectuses and forms of application may be obtained from the bankers, brokers golicitors, and secretary of the Company, and also at the 'Town Clerk's Office. Town Hall Widnes. and at the offices of the Urban District Council, Runcorn. Liverpool, Decerniser 7, 1900.</p>
