      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <p>Elastic Stockirigs Surgical Bandages. Abdo nv nal Belts. Buy them from the or makers in Liverpool. SIVIPTI-1 az SONS, 37, BRECK ROAD, LIVERPOOL We supply a better quality at: a lower price than any other firm in tze trade.</p>
        <p>Sybil was thankful, and said so with tears. The little place was as clean as a new pin, and the kind- ness of these poor people, who had never seen her an hour ago, went to her heart. Fanny Atkins lent her very best hat—which was somewhat too gorgeous for Sybil's taste; but beggars must not be choosers—and she set off with a quaintly-worded, still more quaintly-written letter of introduction to the lady superintendent of the restaurant.</p>
        <p>It was fortunately most improbable that she would be ltioked for by any who might seek her in this neighbourhood. Still, in the • nervous state to which she had been reduced she felt painfully self- conscious, fearing that every pair of eyes that looked at her might be those of an enemy. She was glad to reach her destination—a cheap little restaurant with stale cakes displayed in the window.</p>
        <p>"If only they are willing to try me," she wist- fully thought, and entered, not guessing how Destiny was moving towards her even here. Fanny Atkins's illness had come at a busy time of the year, and the lady superintendent of the restaurant in Fulham-road was glad to have a substitute so quickly supplied. - Sybil was told her duties, given a big, ruffled white apron to. tie over her frock, informed that her wages would be eight shillings a week and her noonday dinner, as well as a cup of tea and a slice of bread in the afternoon supplied free of cost. If she broke a dish she would be fined, if she damaged any article of food she would have to pay for it. The prospect was not alluring; but Sybil imagined herself breakfasting at the Atkins's on a plate of porridge, which would cost a halfpenny a day, and supping on a slice of brown bread and a glass of' milk. Thus she could live for a while, she told herself, and if there were good news of Hester she could even be contented.</p>
        <p>She tried to be friendly with the three other girls employed in the place, but they saw at a glance, and knew at the first word uttered by her sweet, soft voice, that she was not one of them- selves. Her gentleness was called "airs" ; and though Sybil did not understand this at once she was looked upon askance by her companions from the first hour of coming among them.</p>
        <p>She felt miserably -helples where Hester was concerned, for she was pledged to secrecy regarding everything that had taken place at the mysterious house by the river. Hester had exacted her promise that in no circumstances, unless her per- mission were given, should Sybil reveal the secrets of that strange night there and the flight that had followed. Her own name and Hester's name were to be uttered to no one save those already acquainted with their identity; and all that Sybil dared to do was to inform the police that her friend Mary Carter (whom she described) had disappeared while she, Sara Ward, had made a business call at a house in Craven-street.</p>
        <p>This she did on the night of her first day's employment in Fulham-road; but she dared suggest no reason for the disappearance. She dared not say that danger had been supposed to threaten her friend and herself lest she should be asked questions which, for the sake of Hester and her promise to Hester, could not be satisfactorily answered. Even as it was she had hard work to preserve her incogmto, and was obliged to mention the house in Thameston-street as having been her last place of residence. She explained her own sudden flitting from there by telling as much of the truth as she might, and saying she had been afraid to stay after her friend's disappearance, that the lodgings had) been paid for up to date, and the things both girls had left would more than pay for anything else that was owing. _ _</p>
        <p>She was reluctant to tell even as much as this about herself lest it should lead to Beauchamp Drake finding her again; but for Hester she was willing to run the risk. lays passed on, however, and she heard no tidings of her friend from the police. Hester might have been snatched up into the clouds for all that could be learnt of her after the moment when she and Sybil had said good-bye to each other at Mr. Robertson's door in Craven-street.</p>
        <p>Things did not go very smoothly at the restaurant in Fulham-road, for the superintendent had a temper, and not a girl in the little establish- ment but was more or less jealous of the open admiration and preference which customers often shewed for Sybil with her flower face and dainty ways. Sybil never complained or told her grievances to Fanny Atkins, who longed to .hear the gossip of the "shop." But one evening, when she had been in her new position for nearly a week, she was going away from work unusually depressed. Suddenly a cheery voice hailed her. It was Mr. Atkins, jogging homewards with his cab.</p>
        <p>"Hullo, missy!" he jovially cried. "I've me 'orse to change. Will you hey another ride ? " Sybil would have thanked him and declined the favour, lest he might be hailed by some passer-by and wish to earn an extra shilling. e. 71 raute. But he would not take "No ". for an answer, and presently she was shut up inside the "old growler," as the driver disrespectfully called the vehicle he drove. Somebody had left an evening paper inside, and this Sybil was requested to take upstairs when she went, as newsynners were luxuries not possessed every day by the Atkins family. The cabman whipped up his tired horse that "Miss Ward" might gain a good opinion of his driving, and Sybil looked dreamily out from the window at the lighted streets. As she gazed a hansom flashed by in the opposite direction—a smart, private hansom, with a liveried driver and a silk-cushioned interior brightly illumined by electricity. Involuntarily she uttered a faint cry, for the occupant was one who (though she had not expected to see him again) had seldom been wholly absent from her thoughts. "Mr. Churchill ! she exclaimed, half-aloud. And though the noise in the street was too great for him to have heard the words, and the only light upon her face—framed in the open window of the four-wheeled cab—was that of a street lamp, it seemed to her that her eyes and those frank, well-remembered brown ones had met for the fraction of a second.</p>
        <p>She was being rapidly driven in one direction, he in another. The Fulham-road was crowded with carriages, omnibuses and cabs going both ways. Even if he had seen her, even if he had cared to tell his man to turn and try to search for her vehicle in the throng of others, it must have been as impossible to find her as to discover a needle in the proverbial haystack. Yet her heart felt heavy with a sense of loss when the smart hansom had vanished like something in a dream. He had looked so prosperous in his evening clothes—just as she had seen him that first, last time—and no doubt he had been on his way to some grand dinner party, as oblivious of her insignificant existence as if thay had never met. So she told herself. Yet she wished that her hard won serenity had not been disturbed by the sight of him, and that sudden change she had fancied upon his face, which made her foolishly feel as if their eyes had really met and he had really cared. Of course, it was all her imagination, but it had seemed as if he were surprised, delighted; and almost alarmed, all at the same time. "It was only riity own feeling, which was so strong for just a moment that it appeared to reflect itself in his eyes, which probably did not see me at all, "the girl said to herself, as Mr. Atkins's four-wheeler rattled into the mews.</p>
        <p>She stepped out as he opened the door, holding the evening paper in her hand, and having thanked him for his kindness moved slowly upstairs. She did not guess that one of the many hansoms that went swiftly by in the wide street beyond the shadows of the dingy, narrow mews, held a man who searched for a vision that had eluded him.</p>
        <p>"Read the paper aloud, will you, Miss Ward?" pleaded Fanny Atkins, who was convalescent though still weak, and promoted to a sofa in the tiny, box-like sitting-room, which was dining-room as well. "Read the society news. Ido love to know what the great folks are doing." Sybil sat down in a chair by the table, which held a paraffin lamp. She began at the top of the column devoted to the movements of the "smart world," and having imparted to Fanny the latest news concerning a Duchess and a Countess or two arrived at a paragraph half-way down.</p>
        <p>" The in;irria-ge -of Miss aladys Wade to Mr. John Churchill,' " she read, and then straightway forgot that she was reading aloud. He was going to marry Gladys, then. Happy Gladys I Well, there was no great cause for surprise. She had wondered that night at dinner if he and Gladys cared for each other, though they had not talked a great deal together. Why should she care? Gladys Wade's life and Jack Churchill's life were far enough removed from hers. What was it to her that the two lives were to be joined in one?</p>
        <p>"What's the matter, Miss Ward?" inquired Fanny. "You seem as if you'd suddenly gone off in a dream, like. Or "—and she laughed at the evident absurdity of her owr suggestion—"as if you know some of the Quality, and had got a shock hAq.rin' somethino• unexnected about 'em." "How stupid of me," exclaimed Sybil, blushing to think how she had let her feelings be read by Fanny Atkins. "I have rather a headache "—which was true—" but I won't be so idiotic again. She</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
