      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>Iffir DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>The other day I noticed that a cloth hat, much tucked, was trimmed with a cluster ot ostrich tips, the ends of which rested on the hair. The ruffle is in pleaten white silk, bordered with fur. This is now the latest whisper of the fashion. A very chic set, consisting of hat, ruffle, and muff, are in silk of the lightest tone of beige, almost cream colour. The hat is tucked in "snail" fashion on the crown, a line of brown fur following the curves of the tucking. A cluster of beige feathers and two or three bright terracotta chrysanthrunums also adorn the headgear.</p>
        <p>I find there is a practice of omitting to call skirts and blouses by these names any longer, but corsages. Many independent bodices are to be worn out of doors with fur coats. These are lined with something very soft, and have no boning at all, ;,o that the effect is smartly supple, and in wear pet.fect comfort is secured. The discerning person selects panne as the ideal material for such bodices, and trims it with glace silk and frothiest point &amp;esprit. But cheaper, yet smart, models are made of the new English silks, which are so charmingly soft and are endowed with extra- ordinary vigour. The more the home dressmaker seeks for and finds these silks the better. They may be bought in self colours now; at first they were only striped, and as well as being useful for shirts they make good evening cloak and cape linings on account of their inexpensiveness.</p>
        <p>There aro, as we all know, many tasteful dresses for the young girls, but one that I think would he suitable is of dark blue cloth. A blue serge could be used, but this would have to be of a fine quality. The skirt is plain, but relieved by a three-inch band of blue and white striped silk put on in wide vandykes and bordered on either side with two rows of narrow blue velvet. The bodice ;is made with a blouse fitted on to a lining of striped silk, over which comes a short zouave of cloth bordered with narrow velvet and finished in ?front with a big rosette of the same, catching the two fronts of the zouave together; the sleeves are -slightly full and finished, as is the neck, with transparent lace bands. The waist is drawn into 49. folded band of striped silk, but for this a narrow velvet belt could be substituted.</p>
        <p>Being well- dressed this sev-son really lies in knowing what not to wear, and many are the citfalls into which the unwary may fall, unless forethought and discretion are employed. Too aavish use of gold ornamentation is one of the most apparent snares; to be elegant, the gold must be a subtle hint, nothing more. The pagoda sleeve, In season and out of season, is another stumbling- "block ; on outdoor gowns in winter it looks, to some people at least, incongruous. The mingling of tinselled net or chiffon with Inable fur on toques is equally incongtuous. Sable and velvet are the fitting accompani- ments to each other, with a little lace, ut the present fashion is only tawdry. We need to use our sense of the fitness of things most Tigorously in these days, when the arbiters of fashion seem to have lost it.</p>
        <p>The liking for hosiery to match the dress is increasing to such an extent that white silk stock- ing:. are being bought to be sent straightway to a -reliable dyer, to be turned into precisely the colour of the scrap of cloth that accompanies -them. Not contented with plain patterns, tiny inlets of lace to match what i worn on the gown -are appliqued on them, and the consequence is that these exotic productions are never sent to the ordinary laundress, but go to be cleaned. lam becoming more and more enamoured of a rdixture of sick and cashmere hosiery; inexpensive, pretty, and usually to be bought to match any material as :to colour.</p>
        <p>There is a tendency to trim the smaller fur .coats with revers, collars, and cuffs of silk or cloth, braided or embroidered; but, on the other iiand, cloth jackets are usually trimmed merely with braid, cord, or jet, either laid on the material itself or on corded silk. Still a few smart fur- trinlinati coats are to be discovered. One, for instance, is a double-breasted. Eton of brown cloth, with revers and cuffs of sable, a full cravat of .cream lace, and alining of amber brocade; while another is of deep powder-blue cloth, made •in the mates style with collar and revers of gold-braided pale blue cloth edged with ermine.</p>
        <p>your loving JEIINIII.</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
        <p>1119)OUGALL'S PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR 2:2IISTINE THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 7 1900</p>
