      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>PPER.</head>
        <p>PPER.</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT. n • a</p>
        <p>Present giving is as much cherished an institution in the Danish Royal Family, as in our own, and among the multitude of gifts which arrive at Sandringham, from all quarters of the globe, on the anniversary of the Princess of Wales' birthday, those from members of the Danish Royal House are always exceptionally numerous, beautiful, and costly. During the afternoon of the recent anniversary, the Princess's presents were, as usual, arranged in the billiard room, where Her Royal Highness, with the Prince, Princess Vietoria, Prince and Princess Charles of Denmark, and house party, inspected them, Later, the servants, both indoor and outdoor, and tenantry, were allowed to view the magnificent col- lection of gifts, a customary, gracious thought on the part of the Royal recipient. On the occasion of each visit paid by Prince George of Greece to this country, rumours fly round to the effect that his engagement to Princess Victoria of Wales is an accomplished fact. It is extremely unlikely that such statements contain a shadow of truth, but that the Princess, like the Duke of York, is much attached to the Queen of Greece, and her cousin Prince George, is an item patent to all. Queen Olga is the daughter of the Grand Duke Constantine, who was known in his day,as not only the tallest, but the handsomest man of the Romanoff family. Like her sister-in-law, the Princess of Wales' a terrible grief has overshadowed the latter years of the Queen's life, the loss of her elder daughter, the Grand Duchess Paul, a beautiful girl of twenty, proving indeed a crushing blow. After the death of the late Duke of Argylle, Inverary Castle was let to Mr. Lawson Johnson, the inventor of Bovril, who however' has lived but a Ebert time to en- joy his tenancy. Mr. Johnson had made a lifelong study of dietetics, but, although he gained great wealth, he failed to prolong his own life, and was not much more than sixty when be died the other day on board his yacht, in the Mediterranean. It is not unlikely that Sir Thomas Lipton will take Inverary, as the Duke and Duchess of Argylle, intend to reside at Roseneath Castle when in Scotland. This place has hitherto been let to Mr. W Hatton, but it is now undergoing extensive alterations for the Duke's own use. Princess Louise, who like so many members of the Queen's family, is subject to rheumatism, prefers a southern climate in the winter. According to present arrangements, the Duke and Duchess will spend Christmas with Her Majesty, at Osborne.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Baden-Powell, and her daughter have left for South Africa, to spend the festive season with the hero of Mafeking. The famous General is one of the indis- pensable men at the front,and there seems no likelihood of his early return. He is one of the few officers, who remain,whose names were most prominent in the early days of the war. Sir George White and Sir Redvers Buller have returned, so too has Lord Dundonald, the famnus cavalry leader, who was first into Ladysmith. There have also been rejoicings at Hatfield over the re- turn of Lord Cranborne, and Lord Charles Bentinck is among the more recent arrivals. The country is now locking forward to the coming of Lord Roberts, who is expected about the end of the year, and we may be sure he will have a welcome, such as few soldiers have re- ceived during the century which is fast hurrying to its close.</p>
        <p>An American woman recently advertised her services for "cheering the nervous and loceamne." There are few employments, new, or old, to which women do not aspire, and this is clearly the late3t idea in the develop- ment of evolutionary womanhood. Moreover it is perhaps not so absurd as it looks, There are times when we all want cheering up, when it is a great relief for someone with a fund of high spirits, to come in and entertain us with amusing anecdotes and reminiscences. It is not everyone, however, who has a friend who is naturally bright and cheerful, and able to visit us when we are worried and depressed, or perchance recovering from a tedious illness. Therefore this American woman comes forward, as she would no doubt say, to "fill a long felt want "—that of the professional conversa- tionalist who will entertain us when desirea at so much per hour. _ _ _</p>
        <p>igt Not only do Americans secure our best partis, and revolutionize our foot gear, but their "notions" have caught on with our calm manufacturers, and among the seasonable delicacies prepared for Yule-tide are cakes most delicately flavoured, and covered with the softest of icings, in which walnuts, or preserves, take the place of the traditional currants and raisins. In no branch of trade, however, has the country made greater strides, during this century, than in the manufacture of sweets, and it is gratifying to note that we export these—and in no inconsiderable quantities—to Pariq itself. There would seem to be quite a revolution in our ideas of what constitutes good taster in dress,leaders of fashion showing at the moment, an audacity in mingling fabrics and colors, which formerly would have been considered imeossible. Take for example the rage for tinsel, in conjunction with the heaviest materials, such as Scotch and Irish frieze, yet the effect when the combination is properly regulated is charming, and these dresses are so lightened and brightened thereby, that they are used as visiting toilettes and upon occasicns which demand a smart gown. Travelling dresses are preferably in plain cloth, this fabric lending itself well to the graceful folds which characterise the skirts of this season.</p>
        <p>OE So convenient is the ehirt,that it appears to be taking tbe place of the tightly-fitting bodice, or blouse, exeept when paying or receiving visits. The light and richly ornamental vests and waistcoats that give such a dressy touch to winter gowns, lose their freshness and shape, when worn under a heavy coat, and the silk or flannel shirt takes their place, and is far more comfortable. The coloring and designs of flannel and flannelettes resemble very closely those of silk and tweod mixture so that shirts may be had to harmonise perfectly with a tweed skirt and as the shirt becomes every week more elaborate the effect Is quite as good as that of a bodice. One of the conceits of the moment is the wearing of 'uncut and unpolished stones• men as well as women falling victims to the craze. The turquoise is first favourite and very beautiful it is, with its dMicate vein- ins' of coltur. The more uneven its shape and the bigger it is,the better. The turquoise in combination with gold, curiously wrought, forms long and very beautiful neck and other chains. Jade, lapie-lezuli, moonstones, sardoeyx, acquamarine, chrysoprase, and other old-fashioned stones, are all pressed into the ser- vice of this barbaric jewellery, sometimes as many as four or five different stones appearing in one chain. These uncut jewels, are greatly f.voured by Royal and Society wornen.the!Queen however showing a preference for black unpolished onyx or jet. Quite a novelty in jewellery is the setting of diamonds OD tiny silver wire. for tiaras and aigrettes. The effect is dazzling, for each separate stone emits sparkling flashes of many hues, with each movement of the wearer. In Paris the Eecptian Searabeus, and the beetle, made of bripht blur-green turquoise, and other stones, set in gold are much used as buckles of waistbands, or as broochos,and are very quaint and pretty, so are Hewers in gold and anan-el, which are also worn upen the waistband. The popular taste for tea-drinking steadily increases in this country, in spite of all the doctors say against it. Our average consumption is six rounds of tea in the year, or about five or six times as much as is con- sumed by any other people of Europe, or tin the United States. Only in Australia do the people drink more tea than we do, their requirements being as much as seven and a half pounds annually. It is singular how the popular taste varies in different countries. Thus, while we require six pounds of tea per head of population, in the United States they consume only one pound. But in ceffee, the proportion is the other way about, and while we drink less than ore pound of coffee in the year, the American nonsumss as much as tee pounds. Then the average American will drink only China tea, while our sunplies nearly all come now.from India and Ceylon. All fnrdical authorities agree, that tea or crffee, drink ing to rxr:ess is bad, but there is very little harm in the fragrant leaf, proviied it is not 14t to stand ton long. If the teapot is warmed and dry, the tea should be left in it for one minute before flouring in the boiling weer, when two or three minutes is sucfirient to extract the *eine, which 7a the restorative property of tea, all else beirg more or less injurious.</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>OLD FALSE TEETH BOUGHT.</head>
        <p>OLD FALSE TEETH BOUGHT.</p>
        <p>Many ladies an d gentlemen lsave by them7oll or disused • alse teeth, which might as well be turned into money. Messr, I?. D. and J. B Fraser, of Princes Street, Ipswich (established since 1833), bny old false teeth. If you send your teeth to them they will remit you by return post the utmost veins ;or, if preferred, they will make you the best offer, and holi the teeth over your reply. If reference</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Movements in the Civil Service.</p>
        <p>The following changes, promotions, &amp;c., have been officially notified at the headquarters of the respective departments in London ,—Customs, Mr J A Waterwort h, preventive officer (lower section), Hull, transferred to Liverpool and district. Excise: Mr A Harris, second class supervisor, Preston 2nd district, Lancaster, promoted to first class supervisor, Burnley district, Bolton. Mr W. Riordan, second class supervisor, Wigan Ist district, Warrington, transferred to Preston 2nd district, Lancaster; Mr T Baker, second class supervisor, chief office, transferred to Wigan Ist district, War- rington. Warehouses ! The following alterations in the official list of Customs and Excise Warehouses have been authorized, and will be included man early general order ;—(Customs) Liverpool 21, Gow's, Peter's-lane, privilege of manufacturing hop powder withdrawn; Liverpool, 13, Gow's, Finney's- lane, approved for the manufacture of hop powder.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>Personal.</head>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>We are sure the whole parish will learn with re- gret. says "St. James' Parish Magazine "that Mr. MacDonald will shortly terminate his work here at St.James'. He has been offered and has accepted the assistant-curacy of St. John the Evangelist, Sheffield, where our good wishes and prayers will follow him. He has been with us three years and a half, during which time his kindheartedness and modest self-effacement have been most conspicuous That he will keenly feel his severance from St. James' we can well believe, and we can only hope that many will have received blesßing and benefit from his ministrations. The journey between Sheffield and Warrington is neither long nor irk- some.and we can af4sure Mr. MacDonald that when- ever he makes it his friends in parish and con- gregation will be pleased to extend to him a hearty welcome.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Sale of Work at Latchford.</head>
        <p>Sale of Work at Latchford.</p>
        <p>The annual sale of work in connection with Christ Church, Latchford, was held in the Parish Room on Saturday afternoon, when there was a very good attendance of members of the congrega- tion and other friends.—The Vicar (the Rev F A Screeton) in opening the proceedings, said they were this year more hungrily expectant than usual and there was a fund at the bank whose appetite would take a lot of satisfying—the church enlarge- ment fund. Anything left over after paying ordin- ary expenses and providing for the various parochial associations, would go to the Church Enlargement Fund; and, when he looked on the parish and reflected that about 50 new houses had been built since he came amongst them and more were to be built, he though that if the church did not need enlarging it ought to need it. He was sure that if they all put their shoulders to the wheel they would raise sufficient money to enlarge the church. They felt very grate- ful to Mrs White for her presence and be would ask her to open the sale. (Hear, hear and applause. (In the motton of Mr John Fairclough a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mrs White for her ser- vices,and the sale was commenced. The stalls which were laden with a numb-r of useful and ornamental articles, were presided over by tne following ladies —Plain work stall, the Misses Smith, Mrs John Fairclough, and Mrs White; flower stall, Miss Buck, the Misses Bond, and Miss Waring ;toy stall, Mrs E Woods, Miss Atkinson and Mrs Pearce; fancy stall, Miss Massey, Mrs G Dakin, Mrs R T Fairclough, and Mrs Munro; refreshment stall, Mrs Fairclougb and Mrs Deacon. Musical entertainments weree given in the afternoon by Mr Edgar Clare and thf Misses Clare, whilst in the evening a number os progressive games were arranged by the Mists Fairclough.There was on view a large quilt worked by the members of the Latchford Girls' Society.</p>
      </div>
