      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>HARRIS'S, 28-30, BERRY STREET, LIVERPOOL.</head>
        <p>HARRIS'S, 28-30, BERRY STREET, LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>LICENSING MATTERS.</p>
        <p>The Mayor and Transfers to Widows.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Mondly, there were several applications made for transfers ot licenses. The May or presided, and there were also on the Bench, Mr Monks, Colonel Ridgwaydilderman Heaton, Council- lor Smethurst, and Messrs Birchall, Wright and Thorn- ton. The following transfers were granted.—Off-license 64, Bewsev-road, from Sarah J Steward to William Cleo- ton; Big Horse Shoe, from James Standish to Charlotte Standish; Royal Exchange, from William Bolton, to Jane R Bolton; off license,s4, Sc James-street, from Archibald Campbell, to sarah A Buckley; Goldin] Ball, from James Welsby to Martha Welsby; and off-license, Greenall- street, from Matilda P.yton to Mary A E Payton. The Mawr, while one of the applications was being made, said he did not think it desirable for widows to take over these houses. There weal three or four appli- cations by widows that morning. The Clerk : They are not all widows. Mr. Browne : May I say on behalf of the applicants that they are the widows of licencees, and, of course, the interests of their late husbands were in the houses.</p>
        <p>The Mayor : I can quite appreciate that. The only point is whether the responsible business of a licensed house should be in the hands of a woman. Mr Browne : Messrs. Greenall don't like to turn the winows away when the husband has been in the house.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: We shall not make any change this morning, I think ; but, still the practice is one we are not inclined to continue.</p>
        <p>Mr Monks: We don't want to deprive the widows cf the interest they have in the house.</p>
        <p>THE WINHARLEIGH CAFE.</p>
        <p>Mr R Evans applied on behalf of Mr W Roberts secre- tary of the Winmarleigh Cafe for a music and dancing licence, for the premises in Sankey-street. Mr Evans said that no intoxicants were consumed on premises,nor was it likely they ever would te. _</p>
        <p>The Bench ask;cl what was the hour in the morning that was applied for and the reply was " rwelve noon, to 4 a m."</p>
        <p>Mr Birchall Thret we limit licensed houses to two o'clock ?</p>
        <p>Mr Monks: Is not twelve o'clock late enough. Mr Birchall : If two o'clock is late enough for a licensed house, it ought to bi) late enough for a place where no intoxicAnts are sold.</p>
        <p>Eventuatly it was dided to:grant a mu3ic licence from twelve noon to thr,. a.tn</p>
        <p>THE MIDLAND HOTEL.</p>
        <p>Mr Samuel Wakefield, the tenant, applvd for a music and dancing licence for the Midland Hotel.</p>
        <p>SupPrinte-nrient Monaßhan said there wa's no objection on the part of the police.</p>
        <p>The Maymr : How many music licences have we got altrgethrr ? The Clerk: Forty-foar with the one just granted. Mr Browne: Mr. Wakefield only wants the licence for the purpose of dinners and suppers, if he has to give the party a large room, He does not intend to use the licence at any other time, The Clerk : Of course there is a rule that no pro- festionals ere engaged. Mr Browne (to applicant) t You don't wean to do that?</p>
        <p>Mr Wakefield: No sir. I want to feel safe if anyone is asked to sing a sons. Tbe application was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>(CoPYrigU)</head>
        <p>(CoPYrigU)</p>
        <p>THE HOUSE BY THE RIVER.</p>
        <p>BY MRS. C. N. WILLIAMSON, Author of "The Secret of the Pearls," "The Howe by the Lock," "Queen Sweetheart," "The B&amp;rnstormers," "Her Royal Highness," &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>CHAPTER XXV.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXV.</p>
        <p>GIIOgT3 FT;O3I TIIE PAST.</p>
        <p>As the door closed after him, Sybil drew a great breath that was almost a sob. She must act instantly, there was not a second to spare. - -</p>
        <p>Having found him out for the schemer that he was, she was certain that Drake would not go awaj leaving directions with the man and womai of the house that she was to be watched. He was talking at this moment with the woman in the passage, close to the front door. Sybil could hear a low murmur of voices, though she could not catch a word that was being said. But the man was not in the house. Sybil had seen him from the window, only a few moments ago, going out with a market-basket in his hand.</p>
        <p>There were two doors in the dining-room, where she had received Beauchamp Drake—one through which he had just passed, near which he still stood in the little hall-way outside whispering with the landlady, another which probably led to a kitchen. This second door Sybil flew to open.</p>
        <p>She had been right in her surmise. The adjoin- ing room was a small, dark kitchen. The girl darted in, closing the dining-room door behind her. Fortunately for her hopes the house was not one in a row, but stood alone in a little patch of ground. A door in the kitchen opened into the back-yard. Sybil opened it and looked out. A brick-paved path wound round to the front of the house. _ _</p>
        <p>On a nail near the door hung an old shawl, belonging, no doubt, to the mistress of the house. Rather than render herself at once conspicuous by venturing hatless into the street Sybil snatched the unattractive garment and wrapped it over her head. By-and-bye, when she could do so in safety, she would send it back to the owner, she thought.</p>
        <p>Now everything depended upon a chance. If she could pass through the gate and get out of sight before Beauchamp Drake finished his conversation with the landlady, there was good hope of escape— that is, if she did not come upon the man of the house returning with his basket. At all events, she must take the risk, and be thp,nkful that Drake had not given her credit for so prompt, so daring a step as this. With the shawl drawn tightly together, almost concealing her white, desperate little face, she ran along the path, and as she reached the gate, her trembling fingers fumbling with the latch, she turned to throw a swift glance over her shoulder at the front door. Thank Heaven it was still closed!</p>
        <p>She got the gate open, and, afraid to excite attention by running now, was taking the first quick steps away from th€ house when the landlord came round the corner, his basket filled with parcels. Swift as a swallow she wheeled about hoping he had not seen her. No more backward looks now. There was but one course open—to disappear before Beauchamp Drake left the house. Sybil flew on, her imagina- tion playing tricks with her nerves and causing her to start at fancied sounds of hurrying footsteps or a voice calling her name. It was a long way to the first turning in this direction, which she had been obliged to choose, but she came to it at last. As she hastened into the street which branched off she saw a four- wheeled cab leaving the door of a house at which a woman and a pile of luggage had just been deposited. Here was an opportunity to be thankful for in such a suburban region, where cabs are not always to be got in a moment. But Sybil had no money. She had only a little gold brooch, whose value at most could not exceed a few shillings, though to her it was dear, for years ago it had been given her by a kind old lady who had befriended her in a foreign pension,. Still, this was not a time to admit of sentimentality. She ran after the cab, and timidly held up the brooch, her face, under the shawl which hooded her head, bright with blushes.</p>
        <p>"Please," she ejaculated timidly, "could you let me drive with you a—a mile or two for this? I haven't any money, but I've a long distance to walk; and if I might go part of it with you it—it would be a great help." The cabman looked down at the little figure, half-swathed in the ugly shawl, and the surprise on his rubicund countenance changed to a smile of amusement. If she had been older he might have thought that she was a suspicious character to whom it would be wise to give a wide berth; but wrapped in the shawl, her ruffled, yellow hair fluttering about her forehead in little, soft rings, she seemed a child. And if she wore an eccentric costume, and wanted a ride in a hurry, it was nothing to him. "Bless you, my dear, you're welcome to go for nothing if you want to go my way," said he. "I'm out of the four-mile circle and I've got to get back. My horse wants changing, and you can drive to the mews in South Kensington without payin' me a penny, so be as South Kensington's any good to you. "Oh, yes," cried Sybil. "Then put on your brooch again, my dear, and get inside." Sybil lost no time in obeying. With hasty thanks she sprang into the cab, slammed the dilapidated door, and in another instant she was being rattled leisurely but surely out of the neigh- bourhood she feared.</p>
        <p>She could imagine what was likely to be happen- ing at the house she had left. Beauchamp Drake, after telling the landlady to keep a watchful eye upon her lodger until he should return at twelve, would go away, perhaps only to walk until it should be time to return for Sybil's answer. The woman would look into the dining-room when she had closed the front door after Mr. Drake, and, not finding the young lady there, would proceed upstairs to the bedroom at the top of the house where Sybil had slept last night.</p>
        <p>Not seeing her lodger there she would begin to be alarmed and would run from room to room. Soon she must discover that she had been tricked in the one short moment when she had not been on the alert, and she would probably send her husband after Mr. Drake, If the solicitor had not gone too far he would be overtaken and told the story of Sybil's flight. In that case the search for her would immediately begin. "Out of a situation, miss ? " asked the good- natured cabman, when he had brought her to the mews where he lived and where he was to change horses.</p>
        <p>"Yes," said Sybil, for she could not go again to Robertson's Agency.___</p>
        <p>"I suppose y-ou'a like pretty well to get another, eh ? "</p>
        <p>"I would indeed," sighed the girl. "Well, my eldest gell has just left 'or place. She was in a restaurant in the Fulham-road till to- day when she come down sudden-like with the intittenzy. She wants to take the work on again when she's fit for it, but it's like enough they've got no one in yet as she only sent word round this mornin'. - What do you think of callin' in at the place, sayin' that Miss Atkins sent you, and seem' if they'll take you on till she's ready to go back?" "Oh, you're very good," cried Sybil. "I'd ho thankful—if they'd have me."</p>
        <p>"I don't know about it's bein' so very good," said the man. "It's as much for ourselves as you, d'ye see, for we'd like you to promise you'd give up when Fanny wants to step in again. 'Tisn't everyone would do that." "I gladly would," replied Sybil. "I'd look for something else meanwhile." "You couldn't apply while you're done up in that there shawl, my dear. But I'll tell you what, if you're not atove borrowing a thing or two of my gell you'd be all right. Whatever has brought you into hard luck it's no fault of your own, I'll be bound. I'd bet anything I've got you're honest as the day." _</p>
        <p>Mrs. Atkins, the cabman's wife, appeared at a little window over the stables, and Sybil was then invited upstairs. She could not tell her story, but she made enough shy, stammering admissions to excite sympathy in the woman—who was as fat and good- natured as her husband—while even Miss Atkins, the invalid, dragged herself from a tiny adjoining room for a sight of the mysterious stranger with the lovely voice. "Where'll you live if you gets Fan's place, miss ? " asked Mrs. Atkins. "Excuse my question, but seem' as you tells us you ain't too well off I was thinkin' perhaps you might like to lodge with us, with no need to hurry about pay in advance or nonsense o' that sort, as you'd hev' to in most 'louses. not bein' known. When we tykes a fancy we tykes a fancy, and you'd be welcome as the day if you could put up with the tiny room next Fan's."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>proceeded, bravely : "'The marriage of Miss Gladys Wade to Mr. John Churchill is—is postponed until Thursday of next week on account of a death in the bride-elect's family. It was not long ago that the suicide of the Hon. Lancelot Wade, once so popular in society, shocked his old friends and acquaintances, to whom he had been almost 3 stranger for some years. Now his young daughter, who was in poor health at the time of her father's sudden death, has died of pneumonia at the historic family mansion of the Wades, known for the past three centuries as Carstairs House. She had been placed by Lady Carstairs (the widow of the Hon. Lancelot Wade's elder brother, as most people know) in a nursing home, discharged as cured of o. very Serious cough, and, suffering a relapse, was sent by her own request to Carstairs House, where ahe died ten days ago. The funeral, which is tO be strictly private, will take place to-morrow'" Sybil read on mechanically, though, after the first, the sentences lost all meaning for her. "You're as pale as a ghost, Miss Ward. MY word, you look as if you'd seen one anyhow ! " said Fanny Atkins. "I —I feel as if I had seen my own ghost," Sybil could not help stammering. "I suppose I'm more tired than I knew. I'M afraid I can't read any more." The marriage put off because of her death, and her funeral, which would be at some place called Carstairs House to-morrow! What did it all mean? Was she dreaming or was it real? What a strange+ strange mistake to have been made; or had some other girl been masquerading as Sybil Wade? That seemed impossible, for what inducement was there for anybody to borrow the name of one so poor, so friendless, so unfortunate as she? It was a veiled mystery; and the wish for the moment strongest in Sybil's mind was to find CarsteAra House, wherever it might be, and go to that funeral+.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>CHAPTER XXIIL " HESTER TO SYBIL."</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIIL " HESTER TO SYBIL."</p>
        <p>Early in the morning (or rather, early for a lasi man) of the day before that on which Sybil read aloud the astounding paragraph in the evening paper, Lord Carstairs had gone on urgent business to Beauchamp Drake's offices. He had done a riskY an7l dangerous thing, because between two fires be had been compelled to pass through one, and had chosen the one which it seemed to him blazed with less fury than the other. Twice Drake had written that he must see his client, but Carstairs had been determined to proceed in defiance of any advice that might be offered; and, as somehow there had been an inexplicable change in Drake's manner to him of late, he had not been in the mood either to listen to his friend's counsel or to make such confidences as would call it forth. He had wired in answer to Drake's letters that he would see hint as soon as he returned to town. Meanwhile, the notice of Sybil Wade's death went into the papers, and now, lest Drake should be seriously offended by his reticence, Lord Carstairs intended to tell just as much of his affairs as might be absolutely necessary; no more. There was no telling when people might be shocked—or pretend to be shocked —and he meant, now that the solicitor was altered in manner, to be very cautious in his revelations and explanations to Drake. Without Drake's help he would have got on but poorly in the past; but now things were apparently going his own way at last. Hei would presently pay Drake the sum agreed upon for "services received," and then he did not see why he should need the intimate aid of the solicitor again. Drake was out when he called; had beat summoned suddenly away on important business, it seemed. He was certain to return within half-so" hour. But if Lord Carstairs was in a hurry, would he prefer to see Mr. Anson (the senior partner). rather than wait? . _</p>
        <p>No, Lord Carstairs was in no great hurry, lie thanked the clerk. He would not trouble Mr. Anson, but would go into Mr. Drake's private office and read the papers till Mr. Drake came back. Two or three morning papers were given him te while away the time, but he was uncommonly rest- less, and soon tossed them all aside. Then he rose and strol ed from the table where he had sat down to Beau;hatup Drake's sleek. It was littered with papers, for Drake had not expected that all visitors would arrive during his absence. Only an intimate friend as well as client would have been shewn into the solicitor's private room; but Lord Carstairs had suggested it, and the clerk had not ventured to refuse. A slip of paper lay half out of an open envelope which had not yet been sealed, and the address, in Drake's handwriting, was to the advertising department of the Daily Magnet. "Hester to Sybil," he read on the protruding piece of letter paper, and quick as a flash he had it out of,the envelope. There were a few lines only, evidently intened to be inserted in the "personal" column of the d newspaper, and "Hester begged "Sybil," for the sake of the past, to communicate with her by letter or in person at a certain address. IBy Jove!" exclaimed Carstairs, half-aloud; then, with a quick glance over his shoulder at the door, to make sun- that he was still alone, he hurriedly slipped the papar back into its envelope. At first he wal bc...-;!dered as to the true meaning of the lines he had seen, lint, when he had paced uP and down for a moment or two, thinking the matter over, he began to tell liiin.solf that here lay to explanation of Beauchamp Drake's change of manner. The fellow was trying to play traitor,' He was slyly attempting to find Sybil Wade on hl own account. Such a contingency had not occurreu to Carstairs as possible before, but now that hie eyes were being opened he could well see the, temptation. Drake was in most of his secrets, anu knew why Sybil would be a prize to a man wh° was free to marry her. "The traitor!" Carstairs ejaculated between Ili° teeth. "I believe he's been playing a double ganie. for some time. I remember how he said to me ticT, he, like other men, had his price; and though u° alleged on the same occasion that he was satisill to b true to me he has, perhaps, changed his mil% since. Only two days ago there was that urgenu letter from him, asking if I'd found out anythini or done anything new. Not a word have I breathe to him lately about Hester Fra,yne, yet evidentil he's aware of her disappearance, or he wouldn't be putting this advertisement in the paper to trf and lure Sybil Wade within his reach. Some_.l2o`f he's had word of the girls, and he wrote me astgoe 'hose questions in the hope I'd give myself moll only a lucky chance I didn't. And, JoTei! There was that papar I found here the last time called on him, with writing upon it so like syrgb. Wade's name on the handkerchief I'd kept. made a plausible excuse, but I'll bet now it 7, her writing, and that through it he discover, something about her, perhaps even found her, , insolent, treacherous hound! But I'll punish 111,,w for this. Not at once. I must get hold of evidence first, for he's such a plausible beggar explain everything away. No, the time to round ° him hasn't come quite yet." of Flushed with rage, that combined something fear (for Lord Carstairs had placed himself alto_Si completely in _Beauchamp Drake's power), he fo7 himself to sit down at the table once more, ture_il over the papers lest Drake should come suddew: in. But he saw not a word on the printed Pagebq He was thinking as intently as he had ever thoug in all the reckless, wasted years of his life. of How could Drake have got those specimeeLre Sybil's handwriting in his possession if theft hers, as Carstairs hardly doubted now? envelope which had come addressed to "•L',.',10 Carstairs" in the, same handwriting, must been sent out by some agency for such. things. 'no to find out the right one, among the 01,-,*? "addressing agencies" which existed in Leeu,,vtill How to see Sybil and identify her if she were " in a place of the sort? tiflea Suddenly ADCarstairs sprang up with asuiru. _.!...aumation. An inspiration had come a Ile recalled once hearing Drake speak, 19Whia sneering laugh, of an insignificant c,ousin of olio who yearned to "squeeze into society" 6)44 named Robertson, who had an, addressing lig"</p>
        <p>"somewhere,,Ta,,si in, th:le.City.x'c'i.ec yrereate --it _, I, and while htetl:ll-'z'its Sstlitil" on hie feet the d,dleCr orl343eiliers'; expression,hi nikee niY bayliav ne and Beauchamp Drake Car:lC hastily into the Note. His eyes darted to the desk, and from the delk eta effort to the t h e a couldn'tfactlli ng gettuh a ttaotiahtie easy nsn lay: estaii:lll the Nvaiti n g visitor. Carstairs's suspicions renue,r",a, was pale and worried, but he controlled hiS ow— Irs'dofjuystougrost?up to go away. I was tired ct waiting," said he. ".But I'm glad you've c'°l3:l7, before I was off. How about all those urge!'" you know I've been in the country—but here , a to answer any questions you may want to Drake looked relieved. He had no great opini9ll of Lord Carstairs's talents as a diplomatist, and.ll.? did not believe he was enough of an actor to hl.c,l° his feelings if he had seen the paP 0 ' so careles;g: left on the desk. The clerk had had a lecture liq showing anyone into his privae room wiring u. absence, but if anything had happene would do no good. Drake was thankful tofeel taw, nothing had happened. Y? • ,:t( -, ..4.,, Clb be continued.) z .-. -b-- d the lect_i.ure</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Town Council was held In the Council Chamber :at the Town Hall, on Tuesday evening. The Mayor (Alderman Roberts) presided, and there were also present :—Aldermen Fairclough, Heaton, Shaw Green, Monks, Hutchinson, Bolton, Francomb, Councillors Davies, John Evans, W Brough, Forshaw, Rogers, James Evans, Burton, William Roberts, F R Roberts, John Roberts, Pierpoint, Ellison, A J Jolley, Malkin, Greening, Peacock, Hulse, Wilkinson, Bennett, Baird, Smethurst,Tinnion,Sampson,with J Lyon-Whittle (town clerk), S Wilson (deputy town clerk), J Fairhurst (borough treasurer), T Longdin (borough surveyor), J Sutcliffe (assistant borough surveyor), J Dees (sanitary engineer), W S Haddock (gas engineer), C _Madeley (librarian). The Resignation of Alderman Harrison. The MAYOR said he had received a letter from Messrs BB White and Son, on behalf of Mrs Joseph Harrison, acknowledging the copy of a resolution passed by the Council. Messrs White and Son thanked the Conncil on behalf of Mrs Harrison for the resolution in question.</p>
        <p>The By-Election in Whitecross Ward. The Mayor alluded to the return of Mr Walter Brough as one of the representatives of the Whitecross Ward in the Town Council. Mr Brough takes the place of Mr Monks, elected an Alderman of tre Borough. Watch Committee. The Lighting of Vehicles. . _</p>
        <p>The minutes of :the— Watch Committee contained a certificate of efficiency of the Borough Police Force. A Memorial having been received by the carriers and cart- ing agents in the town requesting the Committee to alter the bylaw with respect to lights on vehicles, so that it ehall apply only to vehicles " proceeding faster than a walking pace," it was resolved that the Town Clerk obtain information as to the practice in other towns, and it was further decided that four additional 'bus licenses,be granted to Mr Edward Connor, two to Ply for hire between Sankey Bridges and Sankey-street, and two between Bridge-street and Knutsford-road. The minutes were passed on the motion of the MAYOR, econded by the DEPUTY-MAYOR. Finance and Estate Committee.</p>
        <p>Alderman Salm GREEN moved the confirmation of this committee's minutes and proposed that the follow- ing resolution should be included in the minutes,namely : —" That the Mayor, the Chairman, the Doputy-Chair- man, Alderman Monks, and Councillor A. J. Jolley be appointed a sub-committee to settle the financial adjust- ment between the borough and the county." The motion was seconded by the DEpury-Meros and carried. Street Improvement and Lighting Committee.</p>
        <p>Revision of Burial Fees.</p>
        <p>Councillors J. Roberts and Smethurst up in</p>
        <p>Arms. Lively Discussion.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Cemetery Committee, held on the 16th November, it was resolved that the following tablf of fees be adopted and submitted to the Secretary o State :</p>
        <p>Minister. Sexton. d a d For every burial in the morning lO 0 3 0 afternoon 3 0 2 0 if ft f f „ „ of a pauper 1 8</p>
        <p>In moving the minutes of this committee, Councillor Joint EVANS said that since their last meeting the Ceme- tery Committes had had the matter of fees under con- sideration, and they had decided to recommend the pre- sent scale of fees, which would have to be sent to the Horne Secretary for his approval. The list of fees bad been very carefully considered, and it was considered fair and favourable, and one likely to be adopted by the Home Office. The Parish Clerks fees were abolished altogether, and the question of compensating them for this loss would be considered at an early date by the ornmittee. Councillor PEACOCK seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor JOHN ROBERTS said he understood that by the alteration in the fees a minister would receive 10s for morning funerals. They would well understand that as far as ministers were concerned, no matter what denomination they belonged to, they were not a class of men who were overpaid. If he were in order be would move an amendment to the effect that 20s be paid for every morning funeral. He contended that anyone who wanted to bury his friend in a morning could just as well afford to pay £1 as 10s. In the parish that he was con- nected with, it would make a great difference to the Vicar. At the present time his salary was not very large, and if he came to have these deductions made froa, his salary it would make it very much worse for him. Councillor SMETHURST rose to speak, but the Mayor interposed, and said that the Town Clerk informed him that the present fees would obtain for 15 years. That new scale was only for the performance of funerals by clergymen over Dissenters buried in the consecrated ground. For 15 years the present incumbents, vicars, and the owners of the office would retain the office on the present scale. Nonconformists would be required to bury for lOs at morning services. Councillor SmernunsT warmly protested against the name of the Nonconformists being brought Is. The MAYOR said be only gave th 9 information so that there would be no wreng impression in the minds of the Council. He was sorry if he had caused any offence by the introduction of the word Nonconformist. It would not affect Councillor Smethurst's vicar.</p>
        <p>Councillor SmirrannsT said his Vicar could not let any ground in his churchyard for burials, and the result was that he lost the whole of the amount he would have reaped from the ground.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: H-e loses nothing at all. You are entirely wrong in your impression. The present Vicar of St. Paul's will receive the present scale of fees for 15 years, or as long as he lives.</p>
        <p>COUTICiIIetT SMETHURST : I RBI speaking of the vicar and the vicar who will come after him. The MAYOR: I think that the vicar who comes after him can look after himself. (Hear, hear.) Councillor SMETHURST said he was prepared to second the amendment. They talked about fair wages ; was this fair to the Vicar? It affected him and would affect him, and be asked the Council to blush before voting for a thing like this. (Laughter.) The MAYOR: lam afraid you are asking an impos- sibility. (Renewed laughter.) COUUCiIfOrASMETHURST : lam sorry to hear it. Here they had in the parish of S. Paul's three men who were B.A.'s—passed their Oxford and Cambridge examina- tions—and now the Committee would reduce their fees. The Vicar received a salary of EBO a year, besides what was subscribed for him, and it was beyond all feeling to want to take off this paltry sum of lOs for a morning funeral. Men came and worked in the parish for £BO a year, and not only preached on Sundays, but a great many gave their salary to the poor people. He had great pleasure On seconding the amendment. There were no harder-working people than a great number of the clergymen in th 3 town. (Hear. hear.) He did not think it was right to take that sum away from persons who gave the whole of their time for the benefit of the people. They talked about people receiving money without earning it—(warmly)--what did the Cemetery Committee do? How much did they charge for lettering a gravestone, when they did not put a finger to it in any shape or form? They never lifted a hand, nor did a single stroke, and yet people who did work they gave them 10s, and sometimes, when it was wet, they had to pay 3s or 4s for a cab. The MAYOR : Don't you think the clients pay fo! that? (Laughter.) Councillor SMETHURST . Their clients hwe to pay for a great many things more. Councillor BENNETT : If the clergymen are under-paid let the parishioners contribute towards their stipend as the Noncomformist did. (Laughter.) It is quite news to me that Nonconformists are not paid in the same scale as clergymen,</p>
        <p>Alderman-SHAW-GREEN They are not paid at all. Councillor BENNETT : It is extremely unfair. Councillor JAMES EVANS said he would like to point out to Councillor Smethurst that if a Nonconformist that day was buried in the consecrated ground the Noncon- formist minister did not get the fee, but the Vicar did. Did he call that fair ? (Hear, hear). They had not been dealt fairly with for some time. The Cemetery Committee were not legislating for the vicars of parishes, but for the common good of the community. (Hear, hear). If they had to take circumscribed and narrow views of matters such as that he wanted to know where the legislating was going to end. The Cemetery Committee had considered the whele com- munity, and not a section of the community. They had been dealing with all denominations, arid it was the desire of the committee to do so. If the ministers of the Church were underpaid the remedy was with the national church itself. Councillor SMETHURST. Where do Nonconformists visit? Councillor J EVANS : lam not discussing that matter now. If you like to discuss that question at a different time and in a :different place I am prepared to do so. (Hear, hear.) Continuing, Councillor Evans said Councillor Smethurst seconded the amendment on the ground that the resolution affected the Vicar of lois parish. He the speaker) failed to see where the injus- tice came in. All the fees had to be submitted to the Government of the country, and if the Government said the charges were either too small or too large it rested with them to reject them. They had looked at the I scales ef charges of other places, and they had struck the happy medium. They had not taken the least nor the highest, for the simple reason that they were given to understand that their ecale of charges had to be satis- factory to the Government or they wouid reject them ; hence they had tried on hard lines of equity, fairness, and justice to mete out to the Council a fair and just list of charges. Councillor F B ROBERTS said it seemed to him that Councillor Smethurst was walking in gross darkness. The real object of the Act of Parliament was just this Up to now any Nonconformist minister burying in con- secrated ground had to hand over any fees to the clergy man of the Church of England and the object of the Act was that in future a Nonconformist minister interring in consecrated ground would be entitled to such fees as were orcided upon. With regard to the present clergy and their successors during 15 years no alteration would be made. They would be,entitled to the present fees. Councillor John ROBERTS ; That I can quite under- stand. It is those who follow who suffer.</p>
        <p>.• Councillor F R ROBERTS : They do not follow for 15 years. Any vested interest which the Clergy of the Church of Engiand had in the consecrated ground would have expired, and he til,ught very rightly. (Hear, hear.) Every right-Uunking man would agree that if a Nonconformist minister performed the ceromony he shouid be entitled to the fee,,. (Elear, hear.) Councillor SMETHURST : Our minister buried one last week and did not get the fees. (Laughter.) Councillor F. R. ROBERTS : That is his own look out. Councillor SMETHURST Somebody else got them, but he did net.</p>
        <p>Councillor F R ROBERTS pointed out that the Cemetery Committee,in recommending these fees,were not by any means unanimous. Three thought 103 was sufficient, whilst two thought 7s 6d was high enough. He under- stood that there was some correspondence in a local paper on the subject, but he never troubled himself with reading the letters. The people who wrote these letters did not know what they were talking about. Nothing could be more fair than the scale they submitted that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Improvement in Cockhedge-lane.</head>
        <p>Improvement in Cockhedge-lane.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held on the 15th November, it was re- solved that the best thanks of this Committee be ten- dered to Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons, Ltd., for the public improvement in Cockhedge-lane, which will be effected by the setting back of their premises ; and resolved tbat permission be given to tbe Warrington Equitable Industrial Co-operative Society, Limited to construct a catbead on premises proposed to be erected by them at the corner of Mill-lane and Lovely-lane, and to construct four cellar areas in the passage at the rear thereof, leading from Mill-lane to Selby-street, on con- dition that they pay an acknowledgment of lie per annum.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Councillor BENNETT, seconded by Councillor JAMES_EveNs,the minutes were agreed to.</p>
        <p>Health Committee. New Hospital Attendant.</p>
        <p>The Health Council recommended that the following members be appointed a sub-committee for che purposes of the housing of the Working Classes Act.—TheC hair man,tlao Deputy Chairman— Councillors Baird,Greening Plumpton and Wilkinson. It was resolved that in con- sequence of increasing infirmines, Mr William Bloom- field, the hospital attendant, be forthwith relieved from his duties, but that he be allowed to reside in the Lodge for one month from this date, and that his wages be continued to him for six months. The Committee can- not dispense with the service of Mr Bloomfield without expressing its satisfaction with the way in which he has discharged his onorous duties during a period of 23, years. It was resolved that Mr A E Hands be apppointed Hospital Attendant at 263 per week, with house, coals, gas, Szc. The Town Clerk reported that by an order of the Board of Agriculture Warrington would cease to be within a Swine Fever Infected Area on the 29th inst. Councillor MALKIN asked whether any provision would be made for Mr Bloomfield at the expiration of the six months alluded to. The MAYOR: This is one of the cases which shows the necessity of the formation of a superannuation scheme for Corporation emple7e3. It is extremely pain- ful to the various committees to have to practically dis charge well-tried and faithful servants when no longer fit to carry on their duties and to feel at the same time utterly incapable of adequately acknowledging the services we have received. Councillor MALKIN : It is very hard upon Corpora- tion servants who have grown grey in the service to be turned away in this manner. The MAYOR: We have had opportunities of dis- cussing this subject on previous occasions. It was one of the provisions of the last Corporation Bill which was bitterly opposed by a certain section who called them- selves the Ratepayers Association. It was a most un- just opposition and entirely without precedent. The minutes were then passed. Sanitary Works Committee. This committ:e resolved that notice be given to the Town Ball, Park,and Baths Committees of the intention Li the committee to give up possession of the stables at the Town Hall, about the Ist of January next, that the price of manure be increased to £5 103 per ton, and that the following discounts be allowed on payment of accounts on or before the 31st August viz :—Under 5 Eons 7s 6d per ton, 5 tons and upwards 103 per ton. Alderman MONKS in moving the adoption of the min- utes reierred to the increased price of manure and said it brought the price more nearly on a level with other manures placed on the market. The committee thought it was a reasonable price. Councillor JOHN EVANS seconded and the minutes were carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Paving and Sewerage Committee.</head>
        <p>Paving and Sewerage Committee.</p>
        <p>This Committee resolved that Mr. Alderman Heaton he appointed Chairman, and Mr. Councillor Tionion the D ,puty-Chairman for the ensuing year. The following tenders were accepted : Tender of Mr J 8 Garland for the supply of 200 cast-iron gulleys at 16i 10d each ; tender of Mr G Hoaord for the supply of scavengers' slops, at 63 lid each ; tender of Mr F Booth for the supply of scavengers' hats, at 28s per aczen. It was also resolved that Cloth Hall Yard be paved and that the footpath in Lovely-lane in front of Mr. .Barton's new houses be formed and tiled.</p>
        <p>The minutes were approved on the motion of Alder- man BEATON seconded by Councillor TINNION. Councillor BENNETT called the attention of the Council to the state of Eilearnere-street, and said it was in such a deplorable condition that he would be glad if the work could be proceeded with at once. Councillor JAMES Evaics said his attention had been repeatedly called to the way in which the Corporation did its work. It was a well known fact that as soon as a passage or footpath had been made it was pulled up again in order that gas or water mains might be laid. He knew of a footpath over 100 yards long which within nine days of its formation was pulled up again in order that some pipes might be laid. Be thought such a state of things could be remedied if the Cera• mittead involved would confer with one another. Be believed it was possible for the work to be on done and efficiently done. If the property owners were not paying a second time for the work to be done it was obvious that the ratepapers were (Hear, dear.)</p>
        <p>(Ude:man HEATON promised that Ellesmere-street wouid be paved at the earliest possible date. With regard to Councillor Evans's complaint, the Alderman Pointed out tlaat notices were always served upon the Gas and Water Committees showing the streets it was proposed to pave. These departments had plenty of time to lay their mains, and the Paving and Sewerage Committee did not intend to be kept back with their Work. The committee had a duty to perform, and if there was any blame it was not attached to the Paving and Sewerage Committee. The minutes were then passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
