      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>rtHE EXANIINEI, FRIDAY, DECEVIBER 7, 1900.</head>
        <p>rtHE EXANIINEI, FRIDAY, DECEVIBER 7, 1900.</p>
        <p>evening. They had to submit the fees to the rlme Secretary, and surely the present Government would not be unfavourable to the claims of the clergy. The TOWN CLERK said he had endeavourad to explain the objects of the Act of Parliament in the report which ! he had submitted to the Council, but, evidently, he had not succeeded. The primary object of the new Act was that no fees should be paid to ministers unless tor ser- vices rendend. The Corporation were required to sub- mit to the Secretary of State a new table of fees to be, paid to all ministers of religion for services rendered.' Only the present scale of fees payable to incumbents included fees other than for services rendered. There- fore, those fees must be excluded in making the new table. For instance, there was a fee of 113 21 charged for surplice dues; the existing incumbent would be entitled to the present fees for 15„years, or so long as he remained incumbent.</p>
        <p>In answer to Councillor FORSHAW, The MAYOR said the Act of Parliament was putting an end, after the life interest of the present clergy, for ever to a payment for services not rendered, and if the Coun- cil attempted by any amendment to maintain the fee on its present basis they would be attempting to defeat the object and intention of the Act. What the Council bad to consider was whether the fees submitted by the Ceme- tery Committee for services rendered—nnt for services previously included in the fees—were fair.</p>
        <p>Councillor Foßsnew : Do I understand that this is compulsory bn the part of the Committee to revise the fees ?</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Absolutely _compulsory.</p>
        <p>Alderman &amp;raw GREEN wished- to say a few words in a thoroughly kind and generous spirit. He should be sorry for anything to be introduced to give a flavour of ungenerosity and unkindness. The morning fees at pm- , ent were from £l. us 21 in the best sections of the cemetery, in the poorest section 14s 2d. There were certain graduations in the four scales from £1 11s 21 to 14s 21. The us 2d was for a fee called the surplice —for putting on the surplice. He did not know any better term to use; he used the words given to him. The fee ranged from £1 to 3s for the actual service at the graveside. That was the service the Act declared should in future be paid for, and none other. And, therefore the Cemetery Committee came to the Council with a proposition that for a morn- ing funeral 103 should be considered a reasonable and proper sum for all ministers of religion. There was no distinction drawn in the Act, which said "ministers of religion"—for all ministers performing the sacred office of interring the dead the sum of lOs should be paid in the morning, and in the afternoon the sum of 3s. That occurred to him to be a reasonable, proper and sufficient fee. He did not want to limit the services of the clergy, but they had in some cases a handsome en- dowment to help them. The point chiefly con- cerned the future. It did not apply to the lifetime of any incumbent unless he vacated his appointment, so that the Act would not affect any single holder during his lifetime in that borough. He had made enquiries as to the number of morning funerals, and he bad found that they would not exceed 30 per annum in the whole of the borough, and it therefore came down practically to an insignificant item and one that was not worth all the heat that Councillor Smethurst had put into his pro- position. The MAYOR: The reason why it is proposed to make the alteration is because the duty has been made incum- bent upon the Cemetery Committee by the Act of Par- liament.</p>
        <p>COUncillOr SMETHURST: I 5 this not a linal Act? The MAYOR: NO, air: Alderman EtUTCHINSON : But it is an Act of Parlia- ment we can apply as we think best.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR :- - Indeed it is not. Alderman HUTCHINSON said he must confess he was rather surprised the Cemetery Committee had not brought before the members of the Council statistics about the matter which bad been obtained from other towns. Counciller F R ROBERTS : They did so. .</p>
        <p>Alderman HuTcniNsoN : But they are not here.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: They are in the report.</p>
        <p>Councillor SMETTIti-RST : Just a few.</p>
        <p>Alderman HUTCHINSON, continuing, said the morning fees had been reduced to 10s, an i that was an alteration tbat was not necessary. He did not think that Alderman Shaw Green would care to go to the Cemetery and spend half-a-day there in performance of a duty which the clergy now performed for 103. It should De borne in mind that the clergy of the Established Church had lost a great deal through the Cemetery Acts. The burial grounds associated with Acts of Parliament bad been closed. Of course. the Warrington Cemetery had been in existence for a considerable number of years, but he contended that to reduce the fee to 103 for every burial in the morning was not fair. He did not believe that any funeral took place in the morning where the usual fee was considered too much to pay to the clergyman. Alderman Shaw Green had observed in his speech that there were few funerals in the morning, and if that was the case why make an alteration ? (Hear, hear). He contended that the fees in every department were less than they were in most places in Lancashire. Councillor F. R. RORERTS : Very much higher. Alderman HUTCHINSON : I question that. Councillor F. R. ROBERTS: Much higher. Alderman HUTCHINSON : The general fees ?</p>
        <p>Councillor F. R. ROBERTS: Yes. Alderman BIITCIIINSON : I have been led to believe that our fees were 1 )wer than in any other place in Lancashire. I contend that 203 is little enough for a morning. and I hope the chairman of the committe will see his way to increase the fee to that amount.</p>
        <p>=Alderman BOLTON said he had been trying to get at the root of the whole matter. It seemed to him that the general feeling was that the Church of England party wanted the fees in the morning to be 203 ; the Noncon- formists on the committee thought the minister ought to be satisfied with 103. ("N0,n0.") The Act distinctly stated that all ministers cf religion should be entitled to the fee fixed by the new table. He thought the Noncon- formists ought to have 20s. If it was good for the Non- conformist minister it was good for the Church of Eng- land clergyman, and if it was good for the Church of England clergyman it was good for the Nonconformist minister. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) This was a storm in a teapot. He contended that the best course to adopt was to accept the amendment which had been submitted. The discussion was now wasting the time of the Council; it was a matter that could be settled. Both parties ought to have 20s and as much more as they could get. A postponemect having been suggested Alderman HEATON said if they did postpone it they would have to go through the same process again. Councillor Smirruunsr : Is the resolution out of order or not ?</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK : It i 3 distinctly out of order if the object of Councillor John Roberts is to maintain the status quo. Councillor PIEREGINT : The amendment was that the fee should be 203. for every burial in the morning. The MAYOR: We have to consider what the effect of the amendment ia likely to be. I den't suggest that Councillor John Roberts wishes to render null and void an Act of Parliament and the duties which the Act has laid on the committee, but if the effect of his amendment is to maintain the fee at present in existence—for other than services rendered—then I am afraid I shall have to move the amendment out of order. Parliament in its wisdom lid decided that the status quo was unjust, and had laid on the Cemetery Committees in the various constituencies the duty of reconsidering the question of the fees paid. The fees paid did not affect the present incumbent.</p>
        <p>Alderman HIITCIIINSON : Make it 203 for every funeral in the morning instead of 103 and it will end the matter.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR . lam afraid I cannot accept the sugges- tion because its effect would be to maintain the status quo, and Parliament has decided that that shall not ba maintained.</p>
        <p>Councillor John Roberts rose to Ppeak but was ruled out of order by the Mayor.</p>
        <p>In answer to Councillor Malkin, The MAYOR said that each burial authority would send up its proposed scale of fees to thz.4 Home Secretary, who would decide whether the fees were equitable or not. If the fees to be sent from Warrington were con- sidered by him not to be equitab's he would not allow them, but would alter them to what he considered to be equitable. It applied to all the kingdom. Councillor MALKIN : Then what is the good of dis- ct:ssing the matter to-night ? I contend that this is a storm in a teapot. The MAYOR: Every Committee is bound to submit its proceedings for the approval of the Council. The amendment is a very clever attempt to maintain the status quo which the Act of Parliament has said shall not be maintained. I don't like to take the extreme course, and I should, therefore, lLe the amendment to be with— drawn.</p>
        <p>_ COMICIIIOI' JOHN EVANS, in reply, said that the Town</p>
        <p>Clerk had written to various towns about the size of Wallington, and these were the scales of fees which were in torce.—Widnes : Morning, 5s ; afternoon. 2s 6d. Southport: Morning, 9.i ; afternoon, 3s. Bury : Morning, 7s 61; afternoon. 2s (revisod scale), Preston : Morning, Os ; afternoon, 2s 61. Birming- ham ; Morning, 5s ; afternoon; is. The amendment was then put to the meeting and declared by the Mayor to have been lost. Councillor A J JOLLEY said ho bad no desire to impart any warmtu into the discussion but it had occurred to him that the fees for the afternoon services of a minister had been fixed rather low, namely, at 33. He did not think the member of any other professicm would he ex- pected to give his time for such a very small remunera- tion, and he moved as an amendment that the fee increased to 4s. He would also like to include that t!) item for the burial of a pauper be increased. He did not bee why there should be an invidious distinction drawn between the corpse of a pauper and any other person. (Hear, hear.) He had been trying for some years, as the members of the Council knew, to fight against any stigma being cast upon their less fortunate brethren. He proposed 2s 6cl for the pauper burial. The TOWN CLERK said the fee for a pauper burial was imited by the Act of 1853. Councillor A J JOLLEY : I don't press that point. Councillor DAVIES seconded the amendment.</p>
        <p>Ccuncillor F R ROBERTS thought Mr Jolley's amend- ment even more objectionable than the previous one. He believed he was correct in saying that the clergy were satisfied with the afternoon fee. There were many interments in the afternoon, and if the whole of the afternoon was given to interments the remuneration was not to be despised. He objected to the amendment on the ground that it hit so hard the poorer classes of the community. It was only the well-to-do classes who were likely to inter in the morning, and the difference be tween lOs and 20s was not a very great thing to them. but every extra shilling they took out of the pockets of the working people was extortionate. He did not see why they should take out of the pockets of the working classes what must be a very large sum indeed. If an alteration were made the town would be in ait uproar for they would hit the working classes very hard Therefore considering that the persons most interested had not asked for it, he did not see why it should be altered.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEACOCK thought 3s was quite sufficient, as sometimes one miniteter would bury ten people in one a fternoon.</p>
        <p>The amendment was lost, end the minutes of the committee were then agreed to in their entirety.</p>
        <p>A Suggestion. The Town Hall, Park aria Baths Committee recom- mended that the thanks;of the Committee be given to Mr John Smith and Mr Robert Benson, of Ulverston, for their gift of water fowl to thn gardens, Alderman BOLTON moved the adoption of the minutes. Councillor SMETHURST seconled.</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT suggested that the name of the Committee should be altered to that of the Town Hall, " Parks" and Baths Committee.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON: It is quite long enough, (Laugh- ter). _</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : I think Councillor Bennett is strictly correct.</p>
        <p>The minutes were passed. Markets Committee.</p>
        <p>This Committee recommended that Ernest Richard Plins'-on be appointed assistant to the Market Inspector at the wages of 24s per week.</p>
        <p>~ Alderman HUTCHINSON moved the confirmation of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROGERS seconded, and .the minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>Gas Committee.</p>
        <p>The Gas Committee reap mmended that the offer of Messrs James B Watson and Sons for the purchase of the old locomotive at £260 be accepted.—Carried.</p>
        <p>Erection of a Gauge at Walton Lock.</p>
        <p>The BOROUGH SURVEYOR reported to the Rivers Com- mittee that with the permission of the Manchester Ship Canal Company he had erected a gauge at Walton Lock as directed by the Committee.—Approved. Water Works Extension. %The Water Committee recommended that a special sub-committee,consisting of the Chairman,Deputy-Chair- man, the Mayor, Aldermen Bolton, Shaw Green, and Monks, be appointed to carry out the details of the work authorised by the Committee in connection with the Waterworks Extension ; that Mr Thomas Fletcher be appointed Clerk of the Works at Appleton at a salary of £3 103 per week; that the tender of Mr Joshua Tay- lor for the setting of the new boiler at Winwick at £lOO be accepted.</p>
        <p>Alderman FRANCOMB in moving the confirmation Of the minutes said the committee was spending large sums of money on the extensions. Mr rhomas Fletcher, the Clerk of Works,had proved himself to b 3 a very faithful servant of the Corporation as Clerk of Works of the electric lighting station. Councillor JOIIN EveNs seconded, and the minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
