      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Installation of Electric Lighting.</head>
        <p>The Installation of Electric Lighting.</p>
        <p>The Electric Lighting Committtee recommended that Mr William H Grimsdale be appointed electrical engineer, at the salary of £250 per annum.</p>
        <p>- Alderman- :111oNks moved the confirmation of% the minutes, and remarked that Mr Grimsdale was well recommended, and there was A decided advantage in taking him because he was familiar with all the details of the work, having been in charge of the work from the commencement.</p>
        <p>Alderman FAIRCLOOGIi seconded. Alderman SHAW GREEN observed that on going through the principal streets he had been conscious of a con- siderable alteration of the footpaths, owing to the inser- tion of a large number of square iron covers over the electric light connections. Unless something was done he was afraid these covers w ou!d be very slippery in the winter time. His attention had been called to the change, and he noticed that in one case there were three or four in close proximity. He hoped something would be done to mitigate the danger. Councillor WILLIAM ROBERTS said he had been hoping that Alderman Nlonks would give them some good news as to the time when they might definitely expect the electric light. A large number of tradesmen had made provision for it, and they would be extremely pleased to hear that they could depend up3n it for Christmas. (Hear, hear.) Councillor A J JOLLEY said he understood from what the Chairman said at the last meeting that they were to have the Pglat before Christmas. He would like to throw out the suggestion that the ratspayers should be given an opportunity of inspecting the works. Alderman MONKS said it would not be possible for the public to inspect the works for a little time to come. It was the intention of the committee dto get one engine going at the earliest possible moment. Two others were being put down, and when this work had been completed, the ratepayers might be given an opportunity of going through the works. It was desir- able that they should be allowed to do so for many reasons. (Hear, hear.) In reply to Councillor Roberts, he could not state the exact day and minute when the light would be ready but the coinceittoe wore doing all they could to push on with the work and there was every reason to believe that ties light would be ready in time for Christmas. (Hear. hear.) With reference to the covers in the streets these were only a temporary provision and whoa the joints and works were Com- pleted ordinary flags would bs substituted for the covers• (Hear, hear.) The minutes were then passed. The Tramways Scheme.</p>
        <p>The Tramways Committee reported that they had submitted to them by the British Electric Traction Com- pany, Limited, proposals for leasing the Warrington tramways, the consideration of which was postponed. A proposal having been made by the promoters of the Warrington and Northwich Light R ailway that the Cor- poration should join in the application for the Order, it was resolved, that in the opinion of this Committee it is inadvisable for the Corporation to join in the proposed application for the Light Railway Order, and that the proposal be not entertained ; that in order to preserve the entire control of the tram- ways within the borough, no running powers over them be granted to any company. Oa the Bth November the committee again considered at length the terms for tak- ing the traffic from the Warrington and Northwich Light Railway over the Warrington tramways. It was resolved that as advised the difficulties which would attend the snaking of a permanent agreement to take over the traffic of the Northwich Light Railway Com- pany are so serious as to compel this committee to with- draw the proposals suggested at the meeting held last Friday. A letter having been received from the Bosrd of Trade requesting that that Department should be furnished with plans showing the intended method of laying rails over the crossing at Wilderspool Causeway of the London and North-western Railway Company, it was resolved that Major Cardew be requested to prepare the necessary plans.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR in moving the confirmation of the minutes said the negotiations between the promoters of the Ynthwieli Light Railway and the Corporation were not absolutely at an end. Tiv)Corpo:atiou were expeot- ing to receivd certain proposals from them which would rQceive the seri )113 consideration of the committee. Alderman MONKS. seconded and the minutes were passed.</p>
        <p>Important Resolutions Adopted. The Contract Question. _ .</p>
        <p>The General Purposes Commit:tee, at their last meet- ing, agreed to the following resolucioo : " That the Town Hall, Park, and Baths Committee be requested to consider and report upon the advisability of providing a public municipal bowling green ; tt:lLt no Committee of the Corporation be allowed to entertain at the xpense any deputation from another authority witi,out t •,ar.-!tion of the Council, or of tho General Purpa,t,C Lrmiteexpressed in a special resolutiun ; and that it be autii,t:uction to the committees of the Corporation and to the chief official of each department not to make or accept any contract with any member of the Council or his partner." The M&amp;YOR moved, and the DEPUTY.MAYOn seconded the passing of the micutes, and the Council rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Tea and Meeting at Warrington Liberal Club.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance of ladies at the Liberal Club, Wazrington, on Monday evening on the occasion of the 6th annual tea merting in connection with the Women's Liberal Association. Tea was provided in the Committee Room and a public meeting was afterwards held in the reading room. Mrs Zib3. Armitage, the pr- sident ot the Issociation, occupied the chair, and t here were also present the Mayoress (Mrs filberts) bon treasurer of the association, and Miss Roberts, Mrs Wil- liam Grimsbaw (non secretary), Mr and Mrs Allan Bright (Liverpool), Miss Bessie Dutton, Mesdames F W Monks, Richard Roberts, William Robercs. Clay, Yon,ge, Jackson Holmes, Cook, Crampton, Rathbone, Wyllie, Knowles, Hawthorn, and Hill ; Miss Houghton, Miss Wood (Manchester), Councillor William Roberts, Mr Mitchell, Mr T Hawthorn, Mr G Jackson Holmes and Mr O'Brien.</p>
        <p>The PRESIDENT explained that Ma Walker and Mrs Garnett were unable to be present. The first item in the programme was a pianoforte solo by Mrs Jackson Holmes which was played with her accustomed skill and taste.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>ADDRESS BY THE PRESIDENT.</head>
        <p>ADDRESS BY THE PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>Mrs. ARMITAGE&amp; said it was her duty as president, as well as her pleasure, to welcome her friends that even- ing. It was very cheering to the committee to see such a large attendance. Sometimes they had had rather a small meeting, and had felt they were not getting on as well as they should have liked, but surely in the future they would be able to do some better work. (Hear, hear. It was very much to be regretted that at the recent Parliamentary election in Warrington they were not able to do any work. She was away from home, as her husband had been ill, and the Mayoress, of course, could not take an active part on the Liberal side owing to her position, and altogether it seemed as if they did not help Mr Crosfield at all. She hoped that if there was a cnance in the future they would be able to make themselves known. (Applause). Personally she felt that she ought not to occupy her present position, as she was going from home for three months. It would be very much to the good of the association if they could find a president who would be more at hand and able to help the association in a more energetic manner than shs had been able to do. (" No, no.") She was willing to give her assistance in a quieter way, but at the present time it seemed to her that she was only a figure head. She would be away until nearly April.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILLIAM ROBERTS : Time enough for the next elec@ion. (Laughter). Mrs ARMITAGE : If you appoint anyone else as presi- dent it will not interfere with my work so far as I am able to:help you. I feel strongly that at the last elec- tion we did not do all we ought to bave done, simply, I suppose, because we could not do it. We ought to have rendered more help to Mr Arthur Crosfiald. _ _ THE SECRETARY'S ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mrs WlLLfeaf Gruarsuaw in her annual report for 1899-1900 said :—" I have much pleasure in submittine my annual report of the Warrington Women's Liberal Association for the year ending July. 1900, which is, unfortunately somewhat meagre. lam afraid we have not been quite so active this year. The attention of our association, as of the whole nation, has been cen- tred in the all-engrossing interest of the terrible issues of peace and war, to the detriment I fear, of everything else. But we have Rot been altogether idle, as you will see. We held a very successful tea meeting early in October of last year. About 70 of our members were present. Miss Shilston came to speak to us, and lam sure we all enjoyed her very able address. A. month later we held a public meeting in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, when Miss Balgarnie spoke to us on 'Women and labour.' After Christmas your committee were so busy with the work in connection with our soldiers and also the cause of the famine in India that it was considered better to cancel our Liberal ball. We have held five committee meetings. In May, Mrs C Jackson Holmes attended a meeting in Liverpool on the peace question. In June, our friends Mrs Clay and Mrs Cook attended the annual meetings of the Women's Liberal Federal Council held in London. Your Secretary received an invitation in Jaly to attend a meeting in Manchester under the auspices of the Lanca- shire and Cheshire Unica of Women's Liberal Associa- tions, for the union of that Society with the Liverpool Federal Council. In September Mrs Cook also attended a meeting in Liverpool on the same subject. lam very glad to state that the desired amalgamation has been brought about, which I am sure will be of great benefit to tbe Lilaeral cause.for 'Union is strength.' I am sorry to say there are vacancies in our roll call, by death and re- movals. I would earnestly ask you to help to fill up those gaps, and to work well for the sucsess of our asiociation in the coming year. I may state that the Liberal ball is fixed for Thursday, January 17th." (Applause).</p>
        <p>THE BALANCE SHEET. Mrs HENRY ROBERTS presented the annual statement of accounts. The year commenced with cash in hand amounting to £5 83 sid and a balance of £l4 163 id at the bank. The subscriptions, proceeds from picnic, bank interest, So, brought up the income to £4O us 63. Tcr expenses were £2l us 6d leaving a balance in hand of £l9 Os id. The accounts had been audited by Mr George Clay. The PRESIDENT moved the adoption of the report and statement of accounts.</p>
        <p>The motion was seconded by Mrs. CL&amp;Y, and carried.</p>
        <p>THE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. JonN YONCIE submitted the following resolution for the appointment of officers for the ensuing, year : President, Mrs Armitage ; vice-presidents, Mrs J Cros- field, Mrs R Garnett, Mrs Holmes, Mrs William Pier- point, Miss Dutton ; hon treasurer, Mrs Henry Roberts, hon secretary, Mrs Grimshaw ; committee, Mesdames Clay, .Cook, C J Holmes, Walker, J U Hill, Mounfield, Rathbone an 1 Wyllie. Councillor WILLIAM ROBERTS seconded, and remarked that the presence of so many ladies indicated that they had not ceased to take any interest in the welfare of the Liberal party in Warrington. lie hoped that when the ladies reached home that night they would give their husbands a jolly good trouncing for not attending the meeting with them and supporting them in such a good cause. (Laughter and applause.) Mr. Allan Bright, who was with them that evening, contested Exeter at the last election, and he was sorry that he was not in the House of Commons that night. Referring to Mrs. Armitage he said she was always at her post when she was able to be there, and he was sure they all regretted that their es'nemed friend Mr. Armitage suffered from ill-health. They hoped he would return to his old home restored and strengthened. (Applause.) The resolution was carried. "SOME ASPECTS OF TILE RECENT ELKTON."</p>
        <p>_ . This was the subject of an address by Mrs ALLAN BRIGHT. Sim observed that she was n t surprise i the Women's Liberal Association of Warrington did so little, because the election came upon them all as a great sur- prise. In her opinion there had never been a Parlia- mentary election in recent political hi^tory of a more astonishing character. She knew that rumours were rife about it before it came off, but it was forced upon the country. Mr Asquith spoke of it as being " hustled on" in a time of! aim )st tragic tension, Tao whole coun- try was in fact, flooded with turmoil, excitement and superhuman exertions, and what was the end? Simply practical nil so far as results were concerned. If the Tories had wanted to annihilate the Liberals they should have had the election a few weeks sooner ; or if the Literals had any hope of a reaction the elec- tion should have been put off a few months later. The The Liberals had lost Sir Wilfred Lawson, Mr Philip Stanhope and Mr Maddison but they all hoped these gentlemen would soon seek and obtain re-election.(Hear, hear). There had, been very few changes in the Con- servative ranks, and if she were asked to briefly sum- marize the results of the election—the main points—she would do so in these three words—forced, foolish, futile. (Applause). There was no greater crisis at the time of the election than there had been for some time pre- viously. The register was old and stale, and there was nothing to prevent the Government following the normal course of events and retiring next June with an appeal to the country. (Hear, hear). However, in her opinion, there was one reason for this extraordinary dissolution, and that was that the Government were afraid of the sober judgment of the country and they were glad to go out of office and be swept in again at the head of the Jingo Efever. (Hear, hear and applause) The attempt of the Conservative party to exploit the patriotism of the country for their own ends was alluded</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
