        <p>....,- —_.....__.. J. G. GRAVES, Dept. 161 Division</p>
        <p>to. The issue was confined solely t 3 the war in order that the Conservatives might not be questioned on their failure to pass old age pensions, the drink traffic, the condition of shop assistants, and the housing problem. Referring to the attitude of the Conservative party to the beer trade she said beer had this peculiar quality about it, that it was easier to float down with beer than up, and that was exactly what the Government did. They were in office five years, and yet they did net in- troduce a single measure of social reform, save perhaps the single measures which included the agricultural labourer in the Workmen's Csmoensation Act. They seemed to have since been ashamed of it, as they never made any reference to it at the election. What was the result of the recent election ?—a eain of two seats to the Unionist Government, and, considering the enormous price the nation had paid for theal,she hoped the friends of the Government would sufficiently appreciate the gentlemen who won those two seats for them. (Hear, hear and laughter.) It was a ludicrous gain It was almost a going back, bin it never— theless prevented it being a triumph for the Liberal party. (Applause.) The Government were in for months or for five years,but if they were in for five you's they would be five lean years, years of want, destitution, dear food, dear coal, everything go- ing up because war, whether they thrilled at the sound of the trumpet—for the Anglo-Saxons were fighting men —or whether they shrank from its horrors, meant a terrible price to pay. It woo a price the Government should meet, because the war had been brought about by an absolute want of diplomacy. (Applause.) The recent election had had the effect of uniting the Liberal party and had enabled them in the common dislike of the annoyance of their opponents to work shoulder to shoulder. The Liberal party was stronger now than it was a year ago, and she tiaw no sign of its unity being broken. They must endeavour to strengthen their party in numbers for she believed that the Liberal party was bound to triumph again. When that triumph came it would be owing to the Women's Liberal Association and other associations which had hoped and worked all through the dark niments of defeat. (Applause). During the evening two violin solos were efficiently given by Miss Wood, of Manchester. Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes accompanied on the piano.</p>
        <p>Where we all break down is just here. We 'rely fail to grasp the importance of what we trifles. " Tuppence " a day, what is it? it is ss. per month, or, to put it more ingly, those two coppers a day will pay for )f the finest watches in the world. u only send ss. to start with. J. G. Graves .rd his World-renowned Success . . . HE "EXPRESS" ENCLISH LEV'</p>
