      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>VOTES OF TiI&amp;NIES.</head>
        <p>VOTES OF TiI&amp;NIES.</p>
        <p>Miss BESSIE DUTTON submitted a comprehensive vote of thanks to Mrs. Allan Bright for her eloquent address, to Mrs, Armitage for presiding, and to the instrumental- ists for their able services at the piano and violin. In the course of an interesting address, she remarked that they would have another political struggle some day, and the prediction of Mrs Bright would be fulfilleo—she thanked her for her optimistic prediction, which had cheered them—that the Liberals would come into favour again and have sway. There would be another election some day, and they must, as a Women's Liberal Associa- tion, try to do a great deal better than they did on the last occasion, for they had an excellent candidate in Mr. Crosfield. (Applause, and a Voice: "We shall manage it next time.") Councillor WILLIAM ROBERTS in seconding the motion remarked that the Liberal party were not discredited by the result of the General Election in Warrington, nor were they ashamed of having been la?aten. There was something more disgraceful than defeat, and it would have been very much more to their discredit as a Liberal party had they allowed the election to go by without a contest. (Hear, hear.) They made their protost, they did their best, and they were not ashamed. The time would come—he was as certain of it as that he was in that room—when history would prove that those who had been opposed to the policy in South Africa would be found to have been in the right. Time was on their side, and events which were occurring daily proved that it had been a terrible mistake, a mistake for which they would have to pay enormously not only in money but in the terrible sacrifice of life. The time would yet come in this benighted town of Warrington when they would be able to rejaice in a glorious victory. (Applause.) The motion W4B carried.</p>
        <p>Mr. ALLA.N BRIGHT, in acknowledging the vote of thanks, said he really thought be ought not to be called upon to thank them for the vote of thanks passed to his wife, because she could speak far better and he alivays believed in ladies making tnemselves heard both in public and at home. (Hear, hear.) Refering to the closing words in his wife's address of " Peace, retrench- ment and Reform " he said they as Liberals ought to keep those words steadfastly before them and try to apply those principles which the words contained to the political life of the naticn. Whether this was right or wrong and he held and should continue to hold that it was wrong,lamentable, and deplorable that our brave so'diers should be bleeding to death on the veldt, and that at that moment they should be forced as a nation toibe fighting women and children. lie knew there was provocation, but whether the war was a necessity or not the fact had to be faced that they were at that moment engaged in a conflict the like of which had never before occurred in Euglish history. He held it was not e necessity if our Government; would pocket their false pride and face the situation as it was to-day, a better means could be found by which this terrible struggle could be brought to an end in an honest and honourable manner. If the Government had one spark of imagination or knew anything of the history of our country or anything of the history of our Colonies of America they would keow that the policy we were now pursuing in South Africa was just the policy to alienate the Dutch from us and might lead in the long run to our losing our possessions in that quarter of the world. That was noz a result we desirei. We wished to retain what we had honestly got fairly and generously , and with the good feeling and goodwill of the inhabi- tants over whom we exercised dominion and sway. (Ap- plause.) What of retrenchment ? How could they have retrenchment when two millions were being spent each week on war? The public money was teing poured out. We were told that ten millions would be ksufficieht, but already seventy-five millions had been spent and Parliament was meeting in order to make another grant. How do we know that ws the end ? Some said at the commeuccineat of the wxr that the Government was going into an enterprise of which they did not know the end, and they were ridiculed, but it would be found in the long run that those who had held up the banner of peace and said that the war would be disastrous and that the consequences of it were more than we could readily tell, were in the right. He noticed a considerable change of opinion in the war party. They were lseginning to be uneasy. They did not like to adroit that they had been wrong. but there was at this moment a strong feeling growing that they had been led into a sort of political and national quagmire. (Hear, hear.) How could they have reform when the eyes of the nation were fixed upon foreign trouble. Be remembered Mr. Gladstone saying we ar devoted to the empire, but opposed to imperialism' , That motto hte gave to every Liberal. We believed in 1 ' our great and glorious empire and great sell-governing communities of Australia and Canada, but we did not believe in a policy of bluste: and bluff up and down the I world. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
