        <p>Warrington ,:Association Football Club.— Dinner and Smoker. With a view of explaining their financial posi- tion to the playing members comprising the above club the committee invited them to Li dinner and smoker at the Patten Arms Hotel, on Satur- evening. Together with members and friends there would be an attendance of nearly 50, and a capital repast served in Mrs. Kiernan's best style was done full justice to. The after proceedings were pre- sided over by Mr. Elliott, chairman of the com- mittee. In the course of his remarks Mr. Elliott explained that owing to the poor support accorded the Association code this season,the committee had deemed it adviFable to meet the players and explain their financial status. Although they were not in a position to do whar, they would like to the players, the committee were still hopful that the tide of ill-luck would be stemmed, and that with better results better support would be obtained, and the club established on a sound financial basis. The players were thanked for their gratuitous services, and Mr Murray (captain of the club) responded, saying that he and his team were still hopeful, feeling confident that they would occupy a better position in the statis- tical table of the Combination. A capital programme was submitted, and the vocalisation of Mr Ruffles in several songs showed him to be passessed of a rich contralto voice. Mr Jones was also successful in several baritone songs, and Mr Einson was humour personified in his three essays. Messrs Whittaker, Murray, Broster, W!iite, Smith and Whitham were applauded for their vocal efforts and Mr Furness in several recitations proved himself in the front rank of our local entertainers. Mr Whit- taker displayed rare skill in a couple of concertina solos and the accompaniments were skilfully played by Mr Bert Smith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>STOCKINGS! --- A Worker's Complaint.</head>
        <p>STOCKINGS! --- A Worker's Complaint.</p>
        <p>The Griswold stocking knitter, a marvelous piece of machinery, is worked very largely, if not altogether, by female labour. Leicester, says the " Leicester Daily Post," is the centre of this industry, but considerable amount ofivvork is also done in the numerous village around. At one of these, about five miles trona Leices- ter, a reporter had an interesting chat with Miss Emma L Rest, living in Maiet-street,Wlnetstone, will works at a stocking knitter. She told him that for some years she had been engaged in this interesting but sedentary labour. Proceeding.she explained : "I have had good health most of my life, but about five years ago I began to suffer greatly from ansemia, and was at lait en ill that 1 had to leave work. A doctor attended me, but he said that Haw stomach was too weak to bear any more medi- cine. I used ta crawl downstairs, but nearly every time I tainted, and had tø be carried back to bed." " Yes," said Mrs. Rest, " my daugnter was very ill indeed, so ill that I didn't think she CV-ifi going to get better. She was deathly pale, and she jus:, lay there and took what we gave her, never saying anything, scarcely evec moving."</p>
        <p>In answei to further questions, Miss Rest, now a bealtloy-lookiag, tall girl of 22, agreed that there was s difference in her present appearance. By way of explanation, Miss Rest continued, "We had often read in the" Leicester Daily Mercury" and the " Christian Budget" about Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, and the cures they effected, and had seen accounts of cases like mine. So mother thought she would try whether th4v would co any good. Although I was so ill, I found great benefit from them before I had even finished one box, and so I continued to take tilem for several weeks, getting better and stronger as I went on. In all I took seven boxes, and was then quite well and able to go to work again."</p>
        <p>Asked as to whether there was any doubt as to what caused the cure, Miss Rest said there was no doubt what. ever. When she started taking Dr. Williams' pink pills the stopped the dortor's medicine altogether. She added. that the neighbours could scarcely believe their eyes. when they saw her out again. The remarkable improvement in Miss Bait's looks is of course due to the entire restoration of her lost health. When, as in anremia, the face grows pale, eallow,and thin, impoverished and deficient blood is the direct cause; and good looks can only be restored by restoring the blood. There is new, rich, aud pure blood in every dose of Dr. Williams' pink pills : and they are at the same time the best of all nerve tonics. They are obtainable everywhere, and reliable tradesmen will not offer substitutea, as the latter are quite u=eless, and people who offer them should be distrusted. Dr. Williams' medicine company, Holborn-viaduct, London, will send them post free at two and ninepence a box, if the reader should have any difficulty, or if substitutes are pushed. The genuine pill: have cured rheumatism, sciatica, indigestion, bile, bronchitis, consumption in its early stages, paralysis, Sc. Vitus' dance, and all ailments .that trouble women and girls. intemic and bilking people should avoid purgatives, which impoverish the blood and weaken the entire system.</p>
