        <p>Mn CHAPLIN'S self-deny Caring for Agriculture. ing resolution to remain in the House of Oommons "for the present at all events," in order to look after the :interests of agriculture is as interesting an event as is his explanation of the circumstances under which he ceased to be President of the ILocal Government Board. He:will thrust away from him the proffered peerage because of "the gravity lof the agricultural outlook for his constituents." There will be some curiosity as to his attitude upon the pure beer-question and on the relief of agricultural land from, half its local rates when that matter comes up again, as it must do, in the present Parliament owing to the Act which sanctionedLthe relief having been passed only for four years. The present Ministry ill so far as it is a continuation of the Ministry which, came into- office in 1895, has declined to take any steps to ensure the purity of beer brewed in this country, despite the strong recommendations made to it on the subject. It.would seem to have preferred the sup- port of the brewers to the support of the farmers. The results are most unsatisfactory as far as the farmers and the general public are concerned. The Treasury has power to prohibit the employment of any deleterious compounds in the manufacture of beer. Sir CUTHBERT QUILTER, M.P., is our authority for saying that" this power has never been exercised save in the case of saccharin," and then not in the public interests, but in sole regala to the revenue. Had the Treasury and the Revenue officials been up-to•clate in their researche 8 and their solicitude for the public health, they would have prevented the use of moist sugar, glucose, and other chemical materials in the man" facture of beer, and we should not have had thou'• sands of cases of arsenical poisoning, and a scare which has given brewers the worst WO they have had for many years. The publitc; will now insiat upon having pure beer, ee the representatives of the farmers will hands with the pure beer advocates out of reef for the growers of barley and hops. We wanb a new official description of beer, and "made $01:13 malt and hops" ought now to be affixed to sveq barrel the brewers turn out, and to be exhibited as a guarantee in every public bar. The chemicals used in beer are substitutes, and they are empinYed also to enable beer to be matured more quicklici But they are not good for the consumer, en. Englishmen might follow the example of Centre., nental countries where the purity of beer I effectually guarded by stringent regulation! Chemistry has of late been employed largelY 13. what may be called the sophistication of prucluc Beer does not stand alone in this respect.ts' Fore milk is imported, which is kept from going bad bY chemicals not allowed to be used the countries whence the milk is Of ported. Foreign butters come to us sophist' ated in the same way. Consumers ere dosed with various food preservatives' everi day of their lives, until, ailments are set d deteriorate health, cause the loss of infant life,an baffle the physicians. The beer drinker is perhap milk end a more powerful electoral force than the butter consumers, and hence he may be relied gl? which of pure E uoooodn. tomvearkyebaodby h caresrawveghtinftohreiteret f</p>
        <p>61Ir</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <p>to join in the crusade, because it will tend to keep down unfair competition and to prevent chemical sophistication from pcisoning us with a variety of acids that avert fermentation, solidify albumen, and cannot fail to be injurious to health. Pere beer, pure milk, and pure butter, would be of equal advantage to agriculture and the general public. Tha effort being made by an association to in- crease the supply of eggs and poultry has our Warmest support. The care of agriculture is not a party question, and yet the Conservatives have always profess 3d to be enthusiastically and per- mane otly interested in its well-being. The party has also professed a desire not to rob the poor man of his baer. We hope it will prevent the poor man being poisoned by arsenical and other com- pounds.</p>
        <p>IF the saying whic "All is Lost." heads our article be cor-</p>
        <p>rect, ex-President KRU- GER, in using it as a reason for not going to Brus- sels, has correctly interpreted the general attitude of Europe towards the Boer cause. His own ad- visers areto blame in not having warned him that he could not possibly hope7to change the views and intentions of the Great Powers of Europe. It Would have been a most unfortunate spectacle for the world if diplomacy were liable to be effected by a personal visit or by spontaneous as well as pur- chased support from popular factions. The whole question of the Boer War has been examined long ago, and in the best possible light. Every con- sideration was given to the facts of the case, the relations between Great Britain and the Trans- vaal, and the attitude and effect of the unwise Participation caf the Orange Free State. Nothing has occurred to change that attitude. Great Britain has been scrupulously fair to the rights of Portugal, the only European Power directly inter- ested in the war, even at considerable loss from a military and a material point of view. The Boers have never ceased until now to misuse their rights of entry by way of Delagoa Bay to import arms, ammunition, military stores, and mercenaries. But England has not taken advantage of the position to interfere with the rights of Portugal, and when mistakes were made</p>
        <p>in searching ye's-ids suspected of containing arms she cheerfully indemnified their owners for the losses incurred. There has been nothing in our conduct to invite the intervention of the Powers. If Mr. KRUGER thought otherwise, he is by this time undeceived. He has desired to induce the Powers to depart from their neutrality, and to address to this country an appeal, if not a demand for arbitration. His friendly reception in France has not been followed by a welcome to Berlin, and the KAISER has declined to receive him for the present. That is, until he has abandoned his mission of intervention he is an unacceptable Person. Not only has he received this rebuff, but he has been informed, in a semi-official ,way, that Germany has no interest in encouraging him to persevere in the war. We take this to be a most important point gained. Mr. KRUGER is warned that in the view of Germany "it is now no longer in accordance with the interests of his countrY to encourage afresh that bloody and cruel desolation which is involved in the protraction of the guerilla war, without any prospect whatever of the further maintenance of the independence of the land." In other words, the German Govern- ment plainly intimates to Mr. KRUGER and the Boers that the best thing they can do is to stop the war, and make the best terms they can with Great Britain. There is no other reasonable ' alternative. Independence cannot be recovered. The Boers cannot recover it, and Europe will not stir a hand to help them. Acute Frenchmen may have told the Boer chief that if he could move Germany to act he might be sure of French co- operation. It was an excuse to get rid of him without any pledge, and he now finds that" all is lost." We shall be glad :if he consents to act in the spirit of his own reluctant philosophy. He may order the Boers to continue the war, but: it will not be with any hope of European assistance, and it must be with censure for a desolating and cruel proceeding. He may suggest that terms should be sought for, or that the Boers should lay down their arms and submit to the inevitable. M. Pr/HERS fared no better in 1870 when he invited Europe to save France How can he expect to enlist Europe in favour of the Boers against England? He himself made war against England, Contrary to the advice of: Holland and Germany, E[is own obstinacy and ambition have brought the Boers to their present forlorn and desperate posi- tion. All is indeed lost, and it is a good thing for International harmony and peace that a beaten foe 8110u1d realise the sad truth.</p>
        <p>If the statement be correct that only a single member of the C.l.V.'s has joined the Regulars, and that one hundred and fifty are eagerly seek- ing situations, it will force the conviction that the enthusiasm for khaki, both in the observed and the observer, is short lived. Could any compliment to privates compensate them for hard living in the field, the splendid reception given by the vast crowds a few weeks since should have been suffi- cient for the C.l.V.'s. On the other hand, it must be said that if the citizen soldiers desired to reverse their title and become soldier citizens, that wave of fervour should have meant that seven score of employers should be ready to give them decent situations—paying them ordinary wages. The sentiment, however, appears to have effervesced very rapidly, and seeing ;that for some years past We have had a difficulty in obtaining recruits for the army, and that it will be necessary to increase the standing army to meet the situation in South Africa, we may expect a substantial increase in the rate of pay. Possibly by making the length of Service abroad shorter—by a system of quick exchanges—the coyness to enter the army may be Partly met ; but it is quite certain that the pay taust be improved, if higher efficiency and larger numbers are to be obtained.</p>
        <p>Lord Wolseley has been descanting on the value of shorthand. He declares that he has used it for many years, and found it very nseful, partly because it saved time, and also because the notes he made remained private, and were not decipher- -able by his assistants or servants. What he said ,certainly has an application for all, whatever their 'degree, who employ themselves at a desk; but, on the other hand, it is generally equally true that a large number of people waste time in learning Shorthand, inasmuch as they will have no special reason to employ it in their calling and recreations. To say that the ability to write abbreviated notes Tu. ay be of value to anyone is beside the point, for life is short, and we must in,lce our choice of the elements of knowledge we will acquire. In sea- coast places, for example, nothing could be more absurd than for lads whose ambition it is to speni ,a life on the waves to give attention to steno- graphy, while their minds might be much more profitably employed in reading booxs of travel, to Prepare them for their voyages, and in studying rnavigation. Strange to relate, however, a short- hand class ,was arranged not long ago in a small c. oast town as the principal course of technical ia.struction for the winter. Again, in country villages it is guite,clear that gardening and natural</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <p>history might be taken up with far greater advant- age. The idea that shorthand will bring a good wage should be dismissed. It is a desirable accom- plishment for many people, and it na:ty turn the scale in the selection of a candidate for a number of callings. It is, indeed, essential for several ; but its benefits are exaggerated.</p>
        <p>The local branch of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children suffered a double misfortune in connection with the public meeting held in Warrington. In the first place the alteration of the date of the Mayor's "At Home" prevented the Society from holding the meeting in the Town Hall as at first arranged, and then on Thursday when the people had assembled in Parr Hall,alsecond disappointment had to be announced. The Rev Benjamin Waugh, the founder of the society was to have been present at 4.30, but about that time he was being . whisked through Warrington Station in an express train which he had entered by mistake at Crewe. Preston was the , first stopping place, and in order to give him time to return to Warrington, the meet- ing was adjourned until an hour later Those who stayed to listen to Mr Waugh's address were compensated by hearing a graphic and interesting account of the Society's work. By facts and figures which ran into the thousands, he was able to bring to the minds of his hearers some idea of the extensive scope of this beneficent society in protecting the child-life of the country from vicious and negligent parents. The Mayor pointed out that the cases which came before the Warring- ton magistrates were not so much the resalt of starvation as the filthy conditions under which the children lived owing to the drunken habits of the parents. This is the experience of most towns and it is a blot upon our national escutcheon. It is a melancholy fact that at the close of the Nineteenth Century, with all our boasted civilisa- tion and Christianity, a society for the prevention of cruelty to children should be considered neces- sary. That it is necessary has been amply demon- strated in the brief history of the Society, and it is the duty of all true friends of children to see that I the Society is not crippled for want of funds. - I</p>
        <p>A memorial has been presented to the Watch Committee of the Warrington Corporation by the carriers and carting agents in the town requesting them to alter the new Lights on Vehicles by-law so that it shall apply only to vehicles "proceeding faster than a walking pace." The Town Clerk has been instructed to obtain information as to the • practice in other towns, and this indicates, in our opinion, a favourable consideration of the request. It may or may not be acceded to, but whatever may be the practico elsewhere we are strongly opposed to any such modification of the law here. An unlighted vehicle proceeding at a walking pace is only a very little less dangerous than one travelling at double the speed, and even were it stationary it would still be a serious source of danger to other vehicles. All vehicles and sun- dry which are out after dark ought to carry a light, and the point raised as to the speed at which they may be travelling is not worth five minutes' consideration. Surely the carriers and carting agents of the town are not jibbing at the trifling expense of lamps and a few pints of oil when they endeavour to obtain such an exemption. If they are, then their parsimony is only equalled by their disregard for public safety. We hope the Watch Committee will insist upon at least one vehicle one light, whether the vehicle is proceeding at a " snail's pace," "doing record time," or standing still.</p>
        <p>The Mayor of a 13,..r0ugh is called upon to play many parts but it is seldom that one is asked to discharge such a duty as that which fell to the lot of the Mayor of Warrington. It is said to be an old Lancashire custom for some leading person to lay the highest piece of material in a public building. Fortunately this custom is more honoured in the breach than in the observance. His Worship the Mayor has proved himself on many occasions a most capable chief magistrate and his achievement last Tuesday, week,when be ascended, a scadolding 60ft. high and" well and truly laid' a chimney-pot on the new constabulary buildings must be reckoned as a physical triumph. It is not every Mayor who could rise to such giddy heights and we are afraid that this " Old Lancashire custom "would have gone unobserved if a less nimble and more portly gentleman had been under- taking the Mayoral duties.</p>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter. Dr Joseph Parker is about to make an experiment in journalism, somewhat on the lines of Mr Sheldon and for one week will have absolute control of a London paper. He is to have a perfectly free hand, and to "show the British journalist and the British public how, in his opinion, a newspaper, should be conducted in order to effect the greatest good within the scope of its activities. 'Lie public are to have the hopes and wishes of many religious teachers and social reformers' and there is to be no Ignoring of that which is dark and sad. It is need- leis to expect very much change or improvement, little more than a change in the personality of the editor—Dr Parker, ot the City Temple pulpit instead of the present occupant of the editorial chair. There are many ideal newspapers already in PXiBteaC.-3, of what will be the lasting benefit of Dr Parker's rule has to be seen.</p>
        <p>lift is not easy to perceive what the Welsh quarry- men are to gain by refusing Lord Peurhyn's offer to re-open his quarries. But instead of falling in with and accepting the offer, and thus trying to permanently heal the differences in regard to the management, they are now clearing out by the hundred, and breaking up what were recently happy homes. It is evident that a spirit of much bitter- ness prevails and that the men have a false impres- sion of the attitude which Lprd Penrhyn and his advisers take up.. There is a distinct promise that if the men will, they may return to work, and on conditions welch should be satisfactory to them. No doubt there are faults on both sides, but one side will not acknowledge it.</p>
        <p>A" corner "hi Queen's pigs i 3 the latest form of agitation in the agricultural world—and so near to Christmas too! The Royal porkers have been bought up by a Slough tradesman, who has thus stolen a march on the rest of the pig dealing frater- nity.</p>
        <p>Many people who are not teetotalers will sym- pathise with the objects of the" Bobs "League, which has been started by some members of a London Social Club, for the purpose of giving effect to Lord Roberts's appeal to the public not to treat the home-coming soldiers and sailors to drink. The members of the League,branches of which are being formed in the provinces, are not asked to pledge themselves to abstinence, or to become teetotal lecturers. " Every member shall be taken to have passed his promise to Lord Roberts personally that he will not publicly and unnecessarily " treat " to intoxicating liquors any of the soldiers, vol- unteers, or sailors returning from South Africa." This is a kind of obligation which every one might enter into on his own account,with out joining any society or league; but in these matters numbers give strength, and it is hoped that the" Bobs" League will find many adherents in the provinces as well as in London. In order to strengthen the movement it is proposed that every club formed, or body of men desiring to assist the movement, shall appoint a secretary to receive the names of persons inclined to join the League, and the secretary should communicate the fact of the existence of the League to any other club,society,or body of men with whom he is acquainted, and in- vite them to form other branches. The object of the League is temporary,ani wiled its object is attained may cease to exist.</p>
        <p>Hooliganism, which is commonly supposed to have had its origin in the provinces, s 3 far as this country is concerned,is novo rampant in London,the Whitechapel district of which city has within the. past week produced several very sensational cases It is suggested that however severe the term of imprisonment', it does not deter the street rough from inflicting grievous bodily harm upon respect- able citizens, and that therefore the magistrates ought to have power to order the use of the "cat." The public are becoming quite convinced that something more drastic is needed, and certainly flogging, well and judiciously administered, would have a tendency to make the Hooligan and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>EXAMINER, FR I DAY, DECEMBER 7, 1900 ;RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FR I DAY, DECEMBER 7, 1900 ;RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>the " peaky blinder " consider more seriously their own skins, however little respect they may have for the feelings of other people. With trade not so good, and the weather less favourable than it has beeen, the number of the unemployed is gradually increasing. The Hooli- gan tribe are always the first to be out of work, as they are about the last to do any. So that the pinch of poverty seizes them, and makes them more desperate than before. It is really difficult to know whet to do with such creatures. Every nation has the problem before it, in one shape or another. The "larrikinm" of Austialia have given plenty of troubie as have the Bowery roughs of New York, and many a fearful crime has remained a mystery in the dark streets on the further .ide of the Seine. There is no cunning, however, with the Hooligan of our larger provincial towns. It is sheer brutality,which nothing fairly stamps out.</p>
        <p>An analysis of the various occupations of mem- bers of the new Parliament gives some interesting results. As usual, the most numerous class of mem- bers is found to be t:-.e lawyers, which comprises lid barristers and twenty-four solicitors. The next in order are the gentry and landowners, who num- ber sixty-five ; followed closely by sixty-three army and navy officers,who,however, include only four of the latter. Manufacturers and spinners,to the num- ber of fifty-two, have taken their seats, and been followed in numerical order by forty-four mer- chants and forty ministers and ex-ministers of Government. Newspaper proprietors and journal- ists make a respectable total of thirty-three,and out- number their august colleagues, peers' eons and brothers, by two only. There are fifteen farmers and agriculturiits, twenty-two bankers, and finan- ciers,and twenty-three brewers, distillers and wine merchants who form seats in the new Parliament. The professions which are least represented are those of estate agents, architects, and accountants, who have sent a modest couple, and how these two seats are divided in respect of the three designations it would be difficult to calculate.</p>
        <p>It may be said that the return just issued by the Labour Commissioner of the Board of Trade, deal- ing with trade disputes during 1899, is one of the most satisfactory documents of the kind ever issued by that Department. Indeed, it may he seriously questioned whether so excellent a record has ever been previously published. The number of persons involved in trade disputes last year was 180,217, a decrease of 73,690, as compared with the previous twelve months, while the aggregate duration of the disputes was yearly thirteen million days fewer then in 1898. The year mentioned was, on the whole,to use the words of the Labour Commissioner, one of settled good trade. Changes of wages were of an upward tendency, and differences were easily settled without recourse to extreme measures. In short, this may be described as an almost ideal record, and one that reflects credit upon employers and employed alike.</p>
        <p>One of the many difficulties which rifle Clubs ía various parts of the provinces have to encounter is that of providing a sufficient number of rifles: Many clubs have to be content with a few, which are used in turn by the members. This is very well as a makeshift, but if good shooting is to be pro- moted, and still more, if the members are to take part in the active work of defending the country, every man should have a rifle of his own. The Il- ford Rifle Association has under consideration a scheme which may be recommended to other Clubs. It is there proposed that a certain number of members shall act together, paying a minimum subscription of one shilling per week. So soon as sufficient funds are in hand to purchase a rifle the amount will be balloted for, and the process will go on until each member is in possession of a rifle. Naturally, those who draw the ballot continue their subscription until the rifle is paid for. As soon as any member has paid in the amount equivalent to the value of a rifle he will be entitled to claim the next ballot, and the scheme should work well if there are enough of members.</p>
        <p>BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Mr T imister presided at the usual weekly gather- ing of this Class, when the members spent, a pleas- ant evening studying thu contents of the" im- promptu Question Box." SUCCESS OF A RUNCORN EXHIBITOR.</p>
        <p>Mr II Cawly, of Shaw-street, Runcorn, has been mot successful at the recent Bird Snow in Cardiff, securing Ist, 2,id, 3rd, and 4-n places in Norwich einibits and 2nd in clear yellows. NEW WORKS FOR, WIDNES.</p>
        <p>We learn that negotiations have just been com- pleted by which a large iron and copper soseltin,; firm having works at Swansea and Ellesmere Port, have purchased a large plot of ground at Ditton. It is contemplated mat building operations will be proceeded with shortly</p>
        <p>MEETING OF SCHOOL TEACHERS AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A meeting of assistant teachers of Widnes, Run- corn and district was held at the Conservative As- sembly-rooms, on Saturday, Mr R Calland presided. An address was delivered by Mr J E Holden, Roch- dale, on the importance of assistant teachers being associated and federated to the National Federation of Assistant Teachers, which is connected with the National Union of Teachers. It was unanimously decided to form an association for Widnes,Runcorn and district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>This Council held its ustril meeting at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Tuesday. There were present :—Messrs J Knowles (chairman), S Woodward (vice-chairman), J Mclntyre, J Stcles, T Lewts. George Filling, P Weave, E Clarke, E Wallington, W Fletcher. T Wright, E Brethertou, J M Williams, S Boughton, T Darlington, C Roylance, J Edwards J B Chadwick, George F Ashton (clerk), W E Hough (assistant clerk), J Farrington (iEspector), J Ssinton and W Digg,le (surveyors), and Dr J Adams (medical officer). RUNCORN TO WARRINGTON' LIGHT RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>Messrs Bennett and Ward-Thomas, engineers, Man- chester, wrote as follows :—We regret to inform you that our applicatim for a light railway between Runcorn mid IVarritigton in last May was no successful and we were advised to renew our application with the alterations recommended by the Light Railway Commis- sioners, and it is our intention to do this next May. We should, therefore, be glad if you will kindly renew your resolution in our favour in order to keep matters in ore.er.</p>
        <p>Mr Ashton explained that the resolution passed at that time was one to the effect that the Council could see no reason to object to the scheme as a light railway. Mr Stokes; Then I propose it. Mr Antwiss:scconded, and the proposition was agreed upon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>WARRINGTON TO NORTEIWICII LIMIT RAILWAY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TO NORTEIWICII LIMIT RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>The Clerk stated that on Saturday last he received a letter from Messrs Brown, Brown and Murphy, solicitors, to the promoters of the Warrington to Northwieh Light Railway. Be bad notihad sufficient time to compare the scheme with the one depoted iswith them on a previous occasion. With the Council's permission he would lay it before them at the next meeting. I This was agreed to. THE THELWALL NUISANCE. Mr 11 A Naylor, wrote under date December 3rd.— "Dear. Sir.—your medical offieer Lai aeala given me I"the direct traverse"—a very convenient way out of a difficulty—but it is quite immaterial as far aa I am con- cerned, though in this particular instance I think he is a little confused in the days. Would your Council allow me to draw your attention to the following statementt which, according to the press he made at your 14s meeting.—"lt is rather curious that a smell which de- vastated one set of people should not affect other people at all." Now there are certain features in this Tannery smell like those in the recent beer poisoning cases. What would the public have thought if medical officers had simply dismissed the matter with auch remarks as your medical offiner has made? Instead of drinking poison we are compelled to inhale it, and if your Council can longer remain inactive after Dr Marshall's letter it is no longer any use for me to plead with you for the protection of the innocent little children whose parents live in daily dread hut they should be carried away like the others. Admiral Jones-Parry has very bravely done his duty for the locality he represents, but we have been placed at a serious disadvantage through this tannery having such an able gentleman as Mr Mclntyre to look after its special interests on your Council. There are two very large tanneries near these works which are carrie,i on in the usual wey—no one here has even had occasion to make a single complaint abeut them, and I ehou'd certainly never have com- plained about ref ems. Long and Soa's Tanneries. only that some new process of tannieg hes been introduced, the deadly effects of which the trees around are the silent witnes=es ; although it is rather curious that a smell which devastates one set of trees should not affect other trees at all."</p>
        <p>The Clerk was instructed to acknowledge the letter. HIGHWAY COMMITTEE -THE STEAM ROLLER.</p>
        <p>Mr. 8 Woodward submitted the report of the High- way Committee which suggestedllthat the steam roller be insured against accident in the Vulcan Insurance Com- pany for £l,OOO, at a premium of £2 10s per annum, and that roadmeu when taken away from home for the purposes of misting the roadmaking wherever the steam-roller was at work, be treated shnilar to the County Council roadmen and allowed 61. a night lodg- ing money. The Surveyors were also given a further control of the:men resident within two or three miles. Mr. Edwards seconded the report which was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>3100 RE HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>THE 3100 RE HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Mr P Gleave submitted an exhaustive report on the condition of the Moore Hospital, in which the Com- mittee appointed to go into the affair made several re- commendations for painting and r.nnveirig. It was also showa that the vans used in connection with the institute had never been properly constructed and it was urged that steps should be taken to remedy the evil. This was finally decided upon and Mr G!eave was instructed to obtain detailed specifications of the work rPquired, which the Council would consider at a future date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>SCARLET FEVER AT DARESBURY.</head>
        <p>SCARLET FEVER AT DARESBURY.</p>
        <p>Dr Adams in his report, stated that he had issued an order for the Daresbury Schools to be closed for three weeks, which would bring it to within one day of tha Christmas holidays. Tne usual order was for six weeks but he had adopted this course as the holidays would extend it. The cause was the prevalence of scarlet fever.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA has a world-wide reputation as a deli- cious, strengthening beverage, awl a valuable nutritive food the "Lancet" says it represents " the standard of highest purity." Always insist on having C4DRURY'S—soId only in packets and Tins—as other Cocoas are often substituted for the sake of extra proflt.—Advt. (2)</p>
        <p>PUBLIC! BENEFIT UNDER THE CLOCK FOR Dior BOOTS DI LAROASIALUR.—Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>CORRECTION.</head>
        <p>CORRECTION.</p>
        <p>In the report of the special meeting of Runcorn Council with regard to the Transporter Bridge, published last week, the following words were inadvertently attributed to Mr Wiswall : "There has been a great deal said about this ratepayers meeting. (Laughter). It Wit 3 a very great disa- pJintment to me. (Laughter). It was Mr Grice who made these remarks and not Mr Wiswall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>HOUSE BREAKING AT WIDNES</head>
        <p>HOUSE BREAKING AT WIDNES</p>
        <p>Joseph Knott, 13, and Stephen Peril, were charged before the Widnes Bench on Thursday with entering a lock-up shop, 13, Waterloo.road, belong ing to Naylor and Co., and stealing therefrom three boxes of cigarettes, a quantity of Christmas cards and is 91 in money.—John Hughes, manager, deposed that the shop was left securely looked up on Saturday night last, and to finding the window broken on the Annday aioraing, and the property d,3scribed missing.—Another boy named Meadin testified to seeing the prisoners break the window and enter the shop.—Knott received 6 strokes, and Parll 3 as punishment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>SAILOR'S SHOCKING FALL AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SAILOR'S SHOCKING FALL AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Herbert Wittey, of London, an apprentice on the steamship Bar Dowie, of Glaigow, met with a very serious accident on Wednesday morning. The ship was engaged in unloading and Wittey was walking between decks when he slipped and fell into the hold. This was a drop of about IG feet and he wal fortunate to escape with his life. He was severely injured about the head and body and Dr. Delaney was called iu and after an F.xlmination recom- mended his removal to Liverpool Infirmary, where he was conveyed after some hours' delay. Mr W Shaw, Seamens' Mission, and Mr W Banks, of High- street, kindly performed this duty. This cas, illu- strates the great need of a cottage hospital at Runcorn. VOLUNTEER PRIZE D/STRIBITTION.</p>
        <p>The prize distribution in connection with the Runcorn Volunteer Detachment,will this year make 'quite a red-letter da3. Mr Douglas Story, of the " Daily Mail "fame, will lecture cu the South African War, at the Drill Hall,Runcorn, on Wednes- day next, after which the distribution :of prizes will take place. The prices of admission are Is, and 6d ; and 2s 63 for riserved seats. As the volunteers have, hitherto, provide I us with many capital en- tertainments it is to be hoped a crowded audience will flock to the support of " our citizen soldiers " on this occasion. The lecture commences at 8 p.m.</p>
        <p>017TRAGE ON THE BRIDGWATER CANAL - PRISONERS FURTHER REMANDED.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court, on Saturday lact, the prisoners Henry and Fanny Spencer, husband and wife, boatpeople of Runcorn, who were remanded from the previous Weduesday,for unlaw- fully causing grievous bodily harm to Samuel Lamb, an aged boatman, also of Runcorn, were further remanded, and will come again before the Court on Friday next. —Dr McDougall testified to the rnan Lamb, still being in a very bad condition. His p-lvis bone was almost smashed to atoms, still he was making a little progress towards recovery.— The female was granted bail, but that ptivilege WB refused the male prisoner. RUNCOIIWB NEW SCHOOLMASTER.</p>
        <p>Referring, in the current number of the "Parish Magazine,' to the appointment of Mr. Arthur S Lindsay as headmaster of the National School, out of nearly 200 candidates, the Vicar says :—Mr. Lindsay was head assistant in our boy,' school for three years, when he was appointed headmaster of St. Mark's School, Southampton, where he ha laboured with great success for six years and a half. Mr. Lindsay is a Che,hire man, was educated at the Egerton School, Knutsford, and the Chester Train- ing College. He is an associate of the College of Preceptors, and an undergraduate of the University of London. Mr. Jordan, the present schoolmaQter, does not give up work until January 31st, and it is proposed to recognize his long services to the school in a fitting manner. _ SCENE IN CHURCH-STREET, RUNCORN. _</p>
        <p>About 7.30 on Saturday night Church-street was the scene of a somewhat exciting incident. The thoroughfare was thronged with people, and they were suddenly startled to see a horse and cart bE- longing to Sirs. Carney,a local diti dealer,come tear- ing!along at a furious rate,the drirer,a mere boy, ap- parently having lost entire control of the animal. Fortunately the driver was aided in his exertions by a number of shopkeepers,who, seeing the danger, rushed to the horse's head, and brought the animal to a standstill just as he was about to dash into a grocer's shop beloaging to Mr. Kennedy. Search re- vealed the fact that the back strap had, by some means become broken, and the horse becoming alarmed had dashed away with one of the shafts be- tween his legs, which had the result of frightening him still more. SAD DEATH OF A RUNCORN SAILOR.</p>
        <p>Infcrmation reached dr W Shaw, the Missioner of Runcorn Mersey Missions on Monday, of the sudden death of George Fryer. 45 years of age a sailor on board the schooner Lupentia, and whose home was at Lock-street, Runcorn. It appears he was paid off at Plymouth on Saturday an I shortly after being landed on the quay-side he collapsPrl and died. Dr P McWilliams made a post-mortem examination of the body and informed the coroner jury that death was due to heart disease. A verdict in accordance with this testimony was returned. The deceased had in his possession a sailor's bag and £3lB 4.1 in money. Upon him was found the following letter from his little daughter, which has now a highly pathetic interest. " Dear Fa`her.— Mamma received your letter and was very glad to hear from you. We were sorry you were in a hurry. We have had a letter from Ned Ford and he is coming home at Christmas, so that 1 hope you wi!l be here. No more this time from your loving daughter Selina Fryer. I was at a birthday party on Thursiay and will learn off some poetry for Christmas, So good-bye.' Then followed a number ot " kisses." THE RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night, Mr. E vraal DicHuczh, of the Land Valuation Offices, Liverpool, was the lecturer at the above club, and to a large audience he expounded " The Labour Question," showing " What it involved, and how alone it could be settled." His main contention was that if land values were taxed, the result would be to free the buildings and improvements on laud, and sufficient income would be derived to free can from both Imperial and local taxation. Nothing could be pro- duced without land, and nothing could be produced without labour, or muscular or nervous exertion on the part of man; but the product of land and labour was wealth. Hence the wealth of a community re- sulted from its capability to procure the most from land and labour. That being established it was clear that the taxation of laud values, would render laud easier in the market, and more €ft,rvicenble to labour, a consummation which could not but result in the benefiting of the whole common weal.- -At the conclusion of the lecture, a number of questions were dealt with by the lecturer, and altogether a profitable evening was sent.—The popularity of the lecturer was testified to by be way a vote of thanks to him was received and carried,—Next week Mr. W Millington ; subject " Who passed the reform Bill, 1867." - YOUNG BURGLARS AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Before Alderman J Nield and Major Lewis,at Wid- nes essions, on ThurEday morning. William Twigg, John Ogden, Thomas Jone, and Ralph Urwin, boys between 10 and 12 years of age,were charged with breaking into a fiat belonging to Enos Baswick, and tealing therefrom a watch and chin and other property. It appeared that the flat was lying se- curely locked up on the St Helens and Ssnkey Ctinsi at Widnes. Twigg broke the lock off and the boys went into the flat, lit the lamp and made a a fire, and finding a quantity of provisions cooked bacon an ether things, having on the whole quite a ‘",.-4n f.qst." Subsequently they crossed the bridge 14,,,1 i ted Plincorn where Twigg sold the watchchain for a 1 i twig, who had been many timed previously before the 13,nich was sent to a reformatory until 16 years of age Ogden. who had been c*nvicted once before was ordered to receive six strokes and the other two prisoners were bound over.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RUNCORN HARRIERS STILL SUCCESSFUL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN HARRIERS STILL SUCCESSFUL.</p>
        <p>The first visit of East Liverpool Harriers to Run- corn on Saturday created a large amount of interest in the town, an a good number of spectators con- gregated at the finishing point; and all along the route chosen for the mile race. The weather was very unfavourable, but a fairnumber of runners turned out half an hour after the advertised time, , owing to the late arrival of the viators. It will be remembered that in th 3 opening of the cross coun- try season Runcorn met East Liverpool for their in- ter run, and won by the narrow margin of three points at Liverpool, thus putting a great deal of in- terest in this run. The visitors decided to run a mile break, this being a prominent feature in their running, having a good number of mile runners in their ranks. After a short run out the mile break was lined up for at Halton Brow, where J Goodall with his usual dash. made it a hot pace for the start, closely followed by S Hopkins and J Morrison of the visitors. It was a closely contested race from start to finish and provided a really good ending. S Hopkins made a popular win for the home team, ,beating his opponent J Morrison, by ten yards. The following was the position of teams :—Runcorn: 1, S Hopkins 3 J Goodall, 4 W Lewis, 5 W Ra 3 ,6 W Stubbs, a total of 19 points East Liverpool : 2 J Morrison, S E Hudson, 10 Roberts, 12 Quail, 13 Atherton, a total of 45 points, being a win for Run- corn by the margin of 26 points.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED WIDNES AND RUNCORN TRANSPORTER BRIDGE. Prospectus of the Company. The prospectus of the Widnes and Runcorn Bridge Company will be found in our advertising columns. Thea share capital is £lOO,OOO divided into 10,000 shares of £lO each, and the directors invite application for 6,500 of these shares. Interest at the rate of 3 per cent. per annum, will be paid half- yearly during the construction of the bridge on the amount, from time to time paid up on the shares. A; our readers are aware it is the intention of the Widnes Corporation and the Runcorn Urban District Council to have a substantial interest in the company The necessity for improved means of communication between Widnes and Runcorn has long been felt and the proposed suspension brrdge is designed to meet the want. The car, in which covered accommoda- tion will be provided for passengers, will be about 55 feet wide, and will be very conveniently avail- able for passengers, carriages, carts, omnibuses, tramcars, and wheel traffic of every descrip ion. The proposed bridge will have a span of about 1000 feet, one pier being constructed on the foreshore at the termination of Mersey road, Widnes' and the o'her at the commencement of Waterloo rd,R uncorn The directors are of opinion that the bridge will command a large and varied traffic, and will be used not only to supply purely local needs, but also as a means of communication between Liverpool, St. Helens, and other Lancashire towns on the one hand, and the rich agricultural districts of Cheshire on the other hand. The list of applications will open at 10 a.m., on Monday next, and close on or before Wednesday next at 3 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>___.•-. RUNCORN NO7 ES</head>
        <p>___.•-. RUNCORN NO7 ES</p>
        <p>[By a Correspondent]. The scheme tot an electric Light Railway from Warrington to Runcorn, is according to promise, about to be renewed; at least, at the meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council last Tuesday notice was received from the promuters of the Bill of their intention of applying to the commissioners and requesting the District Council to renew their resolution "to offer no opposition to the scheme." This was done with the far seeing proviso protecting the roads of the Council.</p>
        <p>Regarding the Warrington to North wich Light R lilway it was announced in a Liverpool contem- porary the other day, that Messrs. Brunner, Mond, and Co. have given notice of an application to the Railway Commissioners for powers to con- struct a light railway between Warrington and Northwich. I further learn that Messrs, Brown, Brown and Murphy, solicitors to the pro- moters of the Warrington and Northwich Light Railways, are ahoat, to apply to the Light Railway Commissioners for an order to incorporate a Com- pany to make and maintain light railways from Warrington to Northwich and elsewhere in the county. I give both items for what they are worth, but if my Liverpool contemporary can vouch for the one, I have the highest authority for the other.</p>
        <p>Many of my Runcorn working-men friends com- plain to me about Lhe "lights ofißuncorn,"inastnuch as they have to tind their way about in the morn- ings with considerable difficulty. They say that occasionally "Stygian darkness is eclipsed "and those who do not wear clogs and give an indication that they are about, run into one another with the disastrous effect ot upsetting breakfast or dinner baskets and cans, and sometimes worse results. I know that "moonlight night economy" prevails in Runcorn, but can anyone but smile at the moon- light ot Monday night last. Without saying any more, I wish to add that the District C3uncil are respectfully asked to consider the question.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Urban District Council and the Runcorn Rural District Council, have both re- ceived intimation from the County Council, that their applications respecting the incorporation of the road from Runcorn Railway Station to Weston as a main road have not yet been finally disposed of It is not right to anticipate too much, but the re- mark of Mr W Handley that "there is still hope' aptly describes the position. Whilst upon the subject, it might be in place to remind the com- munity of the dire necessity of a good road to Weston Point. It is certainly an old question, and possibly by soma people has been relegated to the "impossible achievement" department but its need is none the less.</p>
        <p>The honour conferred on Runcorn Volunteers by the action of the Urban District Council is fitting, and should please every townsman, and personally I echo the hope expressed by Mr Imison that in the near future—when they return from South Africa— the town will dot forget its duty. Upon their going away, it will be rememb3red that Mr Samuel Taylor, with his accustomed generosity performed the honour for us. But to allow such a thing again would be falling far short of our duty as towns- people. True, we accept all Mr Taylor's gifts and bounties as a matter of course, und his way of giv- ing is such that we cannot feel offence or indeed anything but a pleasure. But, surely the historic welcome of Runcorn's “Volunteer Soldiers" must on tnis occasion however, be a popular welcome from the Runcorn community as a whole.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>k ST. HELENS WIFE'S GRIEVANCES.</p>
        <p>Thomas Cal:aghan, of Boundary-road, was charged at St. Helens Police Court, on Monday morning, with be- ing drunk and disorderly in the early hours of Sunday mornin7: His wife appeared in the witness-box and said she would like a separation order on account of prisoner's continual abuse. Mr. B. Brewis, deputy- clerk, asked if he had ever atruck her. " Na," replied Mn Callaghan, in an injured tone, " but there is many a way riff killing a cat as well as by choking it." Con- tinuing, sh said that her husband had often threatened to strike her, and was al ways "cursing and romanc- ing." He did not give her his full wages. Prisoner said he took to drink bbcause of an affection of the heart and because his wife pawned his clothing. He was fined ss. and costs and advised to sign the pledge. This be promised to do. TUE ART OF CADGING.</p>
        <p>Who James B:bby went to the house of Mrs Maud Shaw, at 83, Bold-road, St Helens. on Saturday and said her husband had sent him for foad and drink for his (Bibby's) starving children, bis tale was so evidently false that he was not believed, and Mrs Shaw sent for Constable Peters. The upshot was that Bibby was charged at the Police Court with " endeavourinz to obtain charitable contributions by false pret2nces." Chief-con4tab!e Wood said prisoner had boon doing this kind of thing for two years. He was now sent to prison for 28 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>youß ADVERTISENI ENT in thy" Examiner places it be ore those whom you want t e oh, goes dlrc3t to the Public, keeps your name before them, serves to make you btttor ktuAN among them, at a Very small cost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above Cmincil was held ou fbursday morning last. There were pre- sent . Messrs. W Handiey, J.P., (presiding), E Imison, E Gatley, S Walton, J H Kirkham, 11 0 Grice, T Dean, It H Pesnett, D Bisbrown, JII Sal- keld, J Wilson, with E Marshall, (secretary), F A Lake (law clerk), 3 Wilding (surveyor), and Dr. F McDougall (medical officer.)</p>
        <p>"HONOUR THE BRAVE."</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he had a duty to perform be- fore commencing the usual business of the Council It seemed to him that it was a very happy circum- stance that prompted Mr. Imison to move the reso- lution that they should recognise in some tangible way the loyalty of the citizen army of this country. The resolution,which was seconded by Mr. J Wilson provided that the names of the 46 volunteers who offered their services, should be entered upon the minute book of the Council, and that the names of those six who were accepted should be drafted in red ink. Not only had that been done, but there was a further sugges- tion made that a small tablet be inscribed with the names of the six, and hung in the Council Chamber. small committee was formed, and the tablet had been completed, and was hanging in the Council Chamber. (Cheers.) He would display it to them. Suiting the action to the word Mr Handley did so, and by pulling a cord displayed a shield of brass, in- scribed as follows :—"Scuth African War. 1809- 1900. On this tablet the names of the nat!n are re corded who volunteered and were accepted for active service. 2nd Vol. Batt. Cheshire Regiment Runcorn Detachment :—Sergeant F J Jeacock, Lance-Corporal. F W Speakman, Private D Chad- wick, Private F Platt, Private A Timmins,Private L Mack. Honour the Brave. "God save the Queen." and at the top and bottom were engraved the Prince of Wales feathers, which had distinguished the County Palatine of Chester for many genera- tions.</p>
        <p>Mr E Imison concurred in all that the Chairman had said, and added that so far none of the young gentlemen whose names were upon that tablet had as yet lost either life or limb, and he hoped that in time to come that some effort would be made by the townspeople to give them a very suitable wel- come back again,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>MORTALITY RETURNS.</head>
        <p>MORTALITY RETURNS.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer (Dr McDougall) reported that during November, 31 deaths were recorded ; of these two were inquest cases. The 31 deaths showed a death rate of 19.5 of the estimated popu- lation and exclusive of the inquest cases of 18 3, 49 births were registered. In the corresponding month last year there were 54 births and 19 deaths registered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>NEW STREET.</head>
        <p>A NEW STREET.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr R II Posnett, seconded by Mr D BiArown, the Highway Committee recom- mendations were approved of. They implied that a part of Waterloo-road, from Station-road to where it joined Percival-lane, be known as "Percival- lane."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>HOSPITAL IMPROVEMENTS</head>
        <p>THE HOSPITAL IMPROVEMENTS</p>
        <p>Mr T. Dean in moving the Health Committee's recommendation that the roofs of the wooden structures at the Hospital be repaired, that two wards and nurses room be formed by means of partitions in the north wooden ward, and that cast iron gutters and down spouts be put on the said buildings, said the estimated cost of work was £7O, and was in accordance with recommendations made by the Medical Officer of Health, Mr. Grice seconded and the recommendation was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>AN INCREASED SALARY.</head>
        <p>AN INCREASED SALARY.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. J. Wilson, it was decided to increase the wages of the Technical Institute care- taker from 21s to als a week.</p>
        <p>THE TRANSPORTER BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The financial statement was approved of on the motion of Mr. E. Imison,seconded by Mr H. Salkeld. Amongst other things it was decided to apply to the Local Government Board for sanction to borrow £lO,OOO for investment in the shares of the Widnes and Runcorn Bridge Company, and the repayment thereof within a period of not less than fifty years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>APPOINTMENT OF DELEGATES.</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF DELEGATES.</p>
        <p>Tho Chairman, Mr S Taylor, and the law clerk, were appointed delegates to attend a conference of Cheshire Urban District Councils,to b held at Ches- ter, for the purpose of considering the grants of the County Council towards Main Road making,</p>
        <p>the troublous timi-s in China, it was with feelings of thankfulness to God th -it they could refl-et that all the missionaries that the Wesleyan body had sent out to China had their livsia spared. (Applause.) Property had been destroyed, au el in the North it was lament- able to think that even some Christians bad been persecuted unto death. Other districs, however, it was thankworthy to note, bad entirely escaped. During the last ten years the work of the mission with which he was most intimately connected, had increased its membership by about 500, and though it took 30 years to make the first 500 members, in the last ten years that figure had been doubled. The opening of the Yantsze Valley to missionaries would afford their society greater scope for their work, and he hoped that the opportunity would be taken when offered. (Hear, bear). The work of the Weileyan Mission was extending on all hands. Old circuits were being divided and new ones extended on both sides. (Hear, hear). Foot-binding had been successfully discountenanced in many instances. (Hear, hear). Young girls, and even an old lady of 70 years bad unbound their feet. (Hear. ilea* There had been an increase also in financial support on the part of converts, and there was, too, a far more earnest interest taken in the services of the Church. (Hear, hear). Having given a graphic description of the werk of the mission field, the rev, gentlemen closed an earnest and eloquent address with an appeal to his hearers to make the mission the 'special object of their work.</p>
        <p>Mr Simister proposed a vote of thanks to the Rev J K Hill for his sermons on Sunday and his speech that oveia- lug,</p>
        <p>clifr F J Norman seconded, Mr Posnett supported, and the motion was carried unanimously. A similar compliment was paid to the Chairman for presiding. Mr Taylor announcPd that the collection that evening amounted to £l7 Hs 81, bringing the total amount raised to £l5O 10s 51.</p>
        <p>The meeting terminated with the Benediction, pro- nounced by the Rev Alfred Wells.</p>
        <p>The Rev J K Hill and Miss Nellie Coventry both appeared on the platform in Chinese costume.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>RUNCORN WELEYANS AND FOREIGN MISSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN WELEYANS AND FOREIGN MISSIONS.</p>
        <p>Special Services and Public Meeting.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Foreign Missions arouse much interest at and deserve considerable financial support from St Paul's Wesleyan Church and Brunswick Sunday School, and the annual services in t Paul's on Sunday were remark ibly well attended. The Rev J K Hill, of the China Mission occupied the pulpit both morning and evening and he preached two thoughtful and convincing dioceurses. Be based his morning's message on St John, 19th chapter, 28th verse, and his evening's sermon on Genesis, 9th chapter 3rd verse. Specially selected hymns were sung and the choir admirably rendered the anthems' Send out Thy light "and " How lovely are the Messengers". The collections showed a slight decrease on last year's services. On Sunday afternoon there was a very successful meeting held at the Brunswick Schools, and it is interesting to record that so thorough has been the work of the Oung people connected with the school that the school collections had increased on the amount of the previous year. The annual meeting was held at St. Paul's Church on Monday evening. Mr R Garnett, of Warrington, pre- sided, and he was supported on the platform by the Revs Alfred Wells, H Morrison, J B Forster, J K Bill from China (deputation). Messrs F J Norman, A R Norman R U Pcsinett, S Taylor T Simister, E Taylor, T Boot?, J. Hayes, J M Frith and others. There was only a moderate attendance,a fact which might be attributed to the unfavourable weather which prevailed. The Rev H Morrison reported upon the general out- look of the society's work. Having referred to the general effect that depressing conditions such as the war In South Africs and no trouble in China.rnust naturally have upon the mission work, Mr Morrison gave the followine interesting fieures :—There were in the mission field 365 missionaries, 300 catechisers and teachers, 47,000 church members. and 92,000 Sunday school scholars. In the past year they bad received £133,690 to carry on the work. This showed what he called an encouraging decrease of £340 on the previous year's account. He thought it was encouraging, in face of what had been done by the people in the past year, especially for the War Find, which naturally had the primary claim of all funds in England. Then, too, there was the Indian Famine Fund, and the 20:h Century Fund. (Applause.) One item especially interesting was the amount that the Young people had raised. They raised last year £21,000 or about-fifth of the whole. (applause.) Liverpool Dis- trict contributed about £7,000; and Runcorn Circuit last year raised £243 14s 4d. (Applause.) In addition to that the Women's Auxiliary raised £lO 135.. The young people's work in Runcorn had been peithaes unique, for out of the £243 the young people had raised more than half. (Hesr, hear.) Tne collections in the several places of worship in the Runcorn Circuit last year were as follows :—St. Paul's £l5O 7s 9d ; Halton- road, £26 Os 7d ; Camden, £lB Os 21 ; Halton. £3l is ; Preston Brook, £4 17s; Weston, £8 5s 9d; Weston Point, £4l3s 101.</p>
        <p>Mr E Taylor (local secretary) supplemented the last speaker's remarks and read the amounts collected by the several lady collectara and Sunday classes, and stated that up to that time the collectiona had ameunted to a sum only £2 2s short of last year's figures. He also paid a high compliment to Mr T Simister'a Select Bible Clas, which has contributed about 3s a week to the funds. (Applause.) The Chairman having expressed the p'evure he felt at being associated on the same platform with the Hey J HUI, his old school-fellow, said he was sure that their men abroad valued highly the moral support and, of course, the financial support given by the laymen at home. It seemed to him that a very successful feature of tle Cuurch's.enterprise was the fact that this foreign missionary work was so far shouldered and supnorted by the laymen at home, who were not able to go abroad The natural sequence of a vital, throbbing, energetic Church, pulsing in every fibre, must naturally he that ir, would throw out its branches and bring forth fruit. A living Church must have acme outlet for its energy and the branches which became as firm and as strong as the parent were branches similar to the foreign mis- sioria‘.v work which th 7 were there to rupport, and just as the tree itself drew virtue bark again through its branches s they received encouragement from hearing of the successful work carried on by men whom they were able to send away ta work on foreign soil. Miaeionary work was carried on not only at the cost of money, but what was of greater cost, viz.., the sacrifice of home, friendship, domestic ties, and every- thing that a man holds most dear, and if men were pre- pared ta go forth and forsake home arrl horn, ties to preach the Gospel abroad did they need to ask whether the work was successful and measure their support ac- , cordingly These missions deserved their liberal sup- port. (Applause.) The Rev. J. B. Foster gave an interesting account of his six years' missionary work in the West Indiee, in the Danish island of St. Thomas. The Rev. J. K. Hill (late missionary in China, and nerhew of David Hill) was well received! Throughout</p>
