      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <p>• - - . - -- - • • • . _ _ .</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>ate, that the unusual spectacle was most interesting to ,watch. Meanwhile, both vanguards were wan- dering about the face of the ground in an aimless but energetic manner. Seldom has finer kicking being seen on Cavendish Park, and Run- corn are to be warmly complimented on their efforts against a strong wind. Once or twice only did the kick- ing cease, and on one of these rare occasions Dickie made another successful dash for the line, and scored his second try. This was followed by a dropped goal from Bowker. and a penalty goal to Farmer almost immediately alter. The final score was Barrow eight pauts, Runcorn four points. NOTES.</p>
        <p>Barrow appear to be in grand form just now,and ha7e in their victory over Runcorn annexed their third con- secutive win in the competition. Moreover in this par- ticular match they did not allow their formidable oppon- ents to cross their line once, although there were two goals registered against them, and it must be conceded they were the better team on the day's play. It was another great day for Tom Bowker. He was undoubt- edly the best back of the held,closely followed by Jolley. Now that Bowker has gut a capable colleague to act in unison with him in the centre, much may be expected rom the Barrow backs. Dickie is now almost as good as Bowker, and his two tries were the result of dashing runs. The wing men, Williams and Bowden, were up to form. The half backs played well again, Wharton being the best. Proctor made a slip or two, but other- wise was in grand form. Forward Runcorn were slightly better, but only in showing better combination. Jolley played a great game ; his kicking and fielding especially, being of the highest order. Wilkinson, bar one bad mis- take, acquitted himself with credit, and D Jones was the best of the three quarters. Oldham v. Millom.</p>
        <p>The visitors were seldom really dangerous at Oldham, the home side having much the better of the play, although very little scoring was done. Oldham obtained two tries before the interval, and One afterwards, and thus won by nine points co nil. Williams scored all three tries.</p>
        <p>Broughton Rangers v. Wigan. These fifteens met at Broughton, the meeting being productive of a very hard game. W. James scored for the Rangers prior to the change of ends, and towards the close Wilson increased the score, the Rangers win- ning by two tries to nil.</p>
        <p>rHE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 7, 1900.</p>
        <p>Su 9 5 92</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Bradford v. Hunslet.</p>
        <p>Considering the heavy ground and the unfavourable weather, this match at Bradford was pretty fast. The result was a win for the borne side by a goal and two tries to a goal. Laidlaw, Cooper, the brothers Gold- thorpe, Hodgson and Eigers were conspicuous. Leeds Parish Church v. Halifax.</p>
        <p>Three goals and a try to a goil and two tries in favour of Halifax was the outcome of this match at Leeds. At the close of the opening half the winners led by a goal—the only point then registered. Huddersfield v. Castleford.</p>
        <p>Playing at home the Huddersfield team did pretty mach as they pleased, and the scoring went entirely in their favour. At half time they led by sixteen points, rnd they won in the end by twenty to nil. Holbeck v. Wakefield. It was not until the second thirty-five minutes of this game, at Holbeck, was well advanced that Wakefield scored a try, which was improved upon. This gave them the victory by five points to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Hull v. Bramley.</head>
        <p>Hull v. Bramley.</p>
        <p>Up to half-time the play was very even, at Hull, the home fifteen only leading by a plint when the teams crossed over. Subsequently, Hull played up wonder- fully well, and ran out winners by one goal and four tries to a goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Brighouse Rangers v. Leeds.</head>
        <p>Brighouse Rangers v. Leeds.</p>
        <p>Another defeat fell to Leeds in this match, at Brig- house, the local team being successful by a goal, from a mark (kicked by Battye) and two tries (by Saville and Walker) to a try (Varley).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Batley v. Hull Kingston Rangers.</head>
        <p>Batley v. Hull Kingston Rangers.</p>
        <p>Batley, who were playing at home, had the best of this engagement, and scored two tries, but the efforts to convert failed in each instance. Batiey thus won by six points to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>TEAMS COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>TEAMS COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Another Victory for Warrington.</p>
        <p>An easy victory for Warrington was the result of Sat- urday's engagement at Widnes. During the first half tne homo team held the upper hand and Hughes and Taylor almoit got over. Then Hamblin from a rush, scored under the posts, Roberts failing at goal. On changing ends, Harris opened the score for the visitors, the goal kick failing. warrington renewed the attack, and Allen got over twice, Murray kicking a penalty goal. Shallcross succeeded in notching another try for Widnes and Harris again scoral for Warrington. Final, War- rington, IA points ; Widnes, six points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>OTHER MATCHES.</head>
        <p>OTHER MATCHES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RECREATION V. VVARRINOTON RANGERS.--At Runcorn on Saturday. Early on Wright scored for the Recreation, Done kicking a goal. Tiie Ringers then attacked persistently. but) could not break through. The Recreation now pressed. Dunbavin scoring a smart try. At half-time the Recreation led by eight points. Parr scored for the Recreation, and Done improved. Sutton dropped a goal for the Recreation. Final i—Runcorn R=ereation, four goals five tries; Warrington Rangers,</p>
        <p>FLIRTON7v. FLETCHER RIISSELL.S.—It Flixton on Saturday. Fletcher's pressed, and Ashurst scored a. penalty goal. Paxton th.n rushed the ball to the visi- tors' goal but the splendid defence of Fletcher's pre- vented them scoring. Half time :—Fletcher's, two points; Flucton, nothing. On resuming Warrington pressed, and Clare scored a clever try,but Ashurst failed at goal. Close on time Boscow drooped a splendid goal, Result .—Fletcher Russell's, seven points ; Flixton nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
