      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>4.6, Ondine Road, East</head>
        <p>4.6, Ondine Road, East</p>
        <p>D ulwi ch, S.E., May 28th, 1900.</p>
        <p>Gentlemen,—Having received so much benefit from the taking of Scott's Emulsion of Cod-Liver Oil, I feel / must write and let you know it. It is some time now since I lint started taking it. Being subject to colds of all kinds, principally on my chest, I was advised by my doctor to take Scott's Emulsion. I did so and have been quite free from any ailment whatever. The cough I had kept me awake at night for hours, and I would get up in the morning quite urrefreshed and worn out, but Scott's Emulsion has quite cured me, and I now sleep well. My stomach is weak through indigestion, but I do not find Scott's Emulsion at all difficult to take; in fact, it helps digestion, and my appetite is much better. I am glad to be able to write and tell you this, and hope others, who reading this try Scott's Emulsion, will receive the same benefit as has yours very sincerely, (Signed) E. S. ALLEN. efits the entire digestive system, soothes the nerves, promotes the making of healthy flesh, and makes growth vigorous and natural. No better remedy for children has ever been originated, and for adults who are subject to chronic ailments and are never well, Scott's Emulsion is the best remedy that can be taken. In the face of such unmistake- able evidence as we offer to prove the effectiveness of Scott's Emul- sion, we do not see the utility of experimenting with cheap and in- ferior nostrums. In no other form will cod-liver oil produce such bene- TRADE ISIARIC. fizial results as Scott's Emulsion, and it certainly pays to purchase only the genuine preparation, which may be distinguished by our trade mark on the wrapper encasing every bottle. You can obtain a sample of Scott's Emulsion by sending threepence to ewer postage to Scott and Bowne, Limited, 95, Great Saffron Hill, London, E- C., and mentioning the name of this paper. All chemists sell Scott's Emulsion.</p>
        <p>The point we should; emphasise in the foregoing letter is the fact that notwithstanding the gentle- man had a weak stomach, he took Scott's Emulsion readily, and faund that it aided digestion and im- proved his appetite. Such cases as this prove conclusively what a triumph Scott's x;mulsion is over other forms of cod-liver oil. What really prompted the experiments twenty five years ago which resulted in Scott's Emulsion was the natural repugnance which many people have to plain cod- liver oil, together with the fact, discovered by a German scientist, that before cod-liver oil can be absorbed into the blood it must be emulsified by the digestive process. The problem was to make cod- liver oil perfectly palatable, and to offer it in as natural a form as milk or cream, both of which are emulsions.</p>
        <p>If you desire a better test :if the ease with which Scott's EmulFion is digested, and in fact of the benefit that it affords to the entire digestive system, give it to a little baby that is Nothing, or to the growing child whose digestive system is weak and who is unable to derive the nourishing properties of food. You will here find that Scott's Emulsion ben.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Thud was held at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Tuesday- last, when there were present :—Mes,rs. J MlsPley,Williims (chairman), Dr Gastaldi, J Meadowcroft, T Reynolds, S Taylor, and A R Norman with the Rural District; Councillors, and Messrs George F Ashton (clerk), W E Hough (assistant clerk), and Joseph Cannah (master).</p>
        <p>This was agreed to.</p>
        <p>female tramp ward where only there is a fixed batb. Care seems to be taken to keep this bath disinfected, but it would be an improvement to be desired tba separate baths should be provided for the 'regular in' mates of the house. Tee diet will probably be improved under the new order. At present it rather need one,or perhaps:two additional solid meat dinners in lieu of aouP or scouse. Mr. A R Norman (chairman of the House Committee) stated that the baths had not been taken into considera- tion, owing to the number of things the Committee bad to deal with, and in regard to the dietary that too NOS under the consideration of the whole Board. ,The Chairman : Then the matter will be adjourned to the next meeting.</p>
        <p>, / Pt e.;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>TUE TREATMENT OF CARELES3 SONS.</head>
        <p>TUE TREATMENT OF CARELES3 SONS.</p>
        <p>Whilst discussing the out-relief cases Mr J Stokes took the opportunity of condemning the principle of showing any leniency to drunken and careless sons, svhose parents were compelled to seek shelter in the house ; and con- sidered it a great hardship that struggling hard-working ratepayer s, who could barely support their own families, should have to contribute to their support through the rates. It was nothing but a policy that encouraged drunkenness and laziness.—The sentiment was heartily concurred in; and, in the case under notice, relief was granted, and the collector will follow up the sons and investigate their case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>TREATMENT OF IMBECILES AND EPILEPTICS.</head>
        <p>TREATMENT OF IMBECILES AND EPILEPTICS.</p>
        <p>Mr Algernon Fletcher, clerk to the Northwich Union wrote as follows :—lmbeciles and Epileptics. In con- sidering this question and having regard to the com- munications received from the Unions in the County, the Chairman of this Board has drawn attention to the statement in the public press that one of the work- houses in Cheshire in a central position is not required for ordinary poor law purposes. It appears to this Board that this workhouse would be capable of easy adaptation for the reception of Imbeciles and Epileptics. If some arrangement could be entered into by the several Unions in Ctieshire for the use of an existing institution such as this forth° purpose indicated the difficulty of dealing with these cases might be solved without any extrava- gant outlay. An early conference of representatives of the several Boards of Guardians in the County and the County Council appears desirable. Mr George Leigh, Clerk to the Bucklow Union, wrote from Snutsford, under date November 17th. Haar Sir —Sane epileptics. The Guardians of tois Union have recenty had under consideration the matter of dealing with sane epileptic paupers, and they have COMO to the conclusion that it is most undesirable that such cases should be placed along with imbeciles as obtains in many workhouses. Tney think, moreover, that such cases might with great advantage be taken from work- houses altogether and placed in county institutions to be established for the purpose and to be managed by a board consisting Af representatives from the various boards or Guardians in the county. For the purpose of considering so important a question my Guardians sug- gest that a meeting of representatives of the Unions throughout the county should be held at an early date, and I shall bo glad to know whether your board favour the idea and will be represented at any meeting. The Clerk to the Guardians of the Runcorn Union. The Clerk stated that he had replied to these letters, and to one from the National Union that the Board were in favour of a conference.</p>
        <p>Later, on the motion of Mr A R Norman, seconded by Mr S Taylor, it was decided to appoint Messrs J M Williams (chairman), Joseph Stokes (vicn-chairman), and Georke F Ashton (clerk), to represent the Board should a conference be eventually held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>LUNACY IN TUE WORECHOUSE.</head>
        <p>LUNACY IN TUE WORECHOUSE.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board!transmitted for the con- sideration of the Guardians the following copy of a report they had received from the Commissioners in Lunacy of a visit to the Work- house on the Ist inst. by Mr Bagot one of the Visiting Commissioners. Copy of report: " I found to-day (November Ist) inscribed op the list of lunatics in this workhouse the names of five women but none of males. The women are all proper cases for the house in the work of which they are most useful. Four work in the washhouse, and one is a helper in the hospital where she is warded. I found them in a satis- factory state, and th-y appeared contented. The rooms occupied by them, as indeed is the house generally, were clean and inlgood order. They are bathed weekly in the</p>
      </div>
