      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>LIKE A LOT OF JOLLY IRISHMEN. BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>LIKE A LOT OF JOLLY IRISHMEN. BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>SHOCKING NEGLECT OF CHILDREN.—A MOTHER SENT TO PRISON. on Friday at the Warrirgton Police Court. before tho Mayor and other justices, Mary Newton, 70, Lvthgoes- lane, was charged with neglecting her seven children in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering and injury to health.—Mr A 13TraWRO prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C.—The evidecce was to the effect that • the woman lived apart from her husband, for the reason that he could do no good with ber. He was a bolter- down and through his sister-in-law, who was a shop-keeper, he allowed his wife a certain amount per week to provide food. The sister- in-law gave her proper food but if she allowed her too much she sold it and spent the money in drink. Prisoner had been under the observation of the Society for the past six months, hut despite the In- pector's warnings,there had been no improvement what- ever. The children were only radially clothed and were In a most filthy condition. They were also poorly nour- ished. The bed in which the family slept was one mass of filth, and in the opinion of Dr Bowden, the conditions ander which the children were living, would be detri- mental to their health. One neighbour stated that she bad seen the children going about the street in a naked state, and P.C. Rylatt had once vitited the house and found the woman very drunk and the children crying bitterly. Prisoner had previously been sent to gaol for three months for a similar offence.—The Bench inflicted the maximum term of imprisonment, six months with bard labour.</p>
        <p>MINOR CASES, Warrington Borough Court. FBlDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Coun Roberts and Smethurst, and Messrs. J. Rymer Hepherd. and Thornton. NITISANCE.—SamneI Grindley, 9, Powell-street, was' summoned to show cause why an order should not be made upon him to abate a nuisance arising from the keeping of poultry on his premises. —After bearing the evidence the Bench made an order for defendant to abate the nuisance within seve, days. Bieroune.x.—Before Mr. Birchall (in the chair), Councillor Roberts and Mr. B- T. Vairelough. A NEGLI,CrED CHILD. —A boy named James Newton was placed in the dock. afid the Clerk explained that on Friday the lad's mother was brought before the Court for neglecting to send him to school. A more serious charge of neglecting her children was preferred against the woman, and she was sent to gaol for six months. The boy did not appear at the Court on Friday, an be 'vas arrested for wandering and not being under proper control.— Mr. Jones. school attendance officer, said he had been instructed to ask that Newton be sent to the "0110." but Dr. Smith had examined the lad. and found he was not in a fit state tolgo.He was reeking with vermin, and had been thoroughly neglected.— An uncle of the boy came forward and volunteered to take charge of him, and see that he was sent to school, and on these conditions he magistrates discharged the prironer. MaxnaY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Colonel Ridg- way. Alderman Neaten, Councillor Smethurst, Messrs. Monks, Birchall. Wright. and Thornton. Mixoferrits.—James Morley, labourer. clleeson's lodging- house, Was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Bridge-3treet about 8.40 on Saturday night. A tine Of ss. including costs, was inflicted.—Alban Doyle, 50, Lord Nelson- street. charged with drunkenness at five minutes past nine on Saturday night. was, on the evidence of P.C. Phillips, fined ss. including costs.—Charles Devonport Brierley, 1, Plerpoint-street wa,s summoned for using obscene laruzuage In that street on November 28th—P.C. Corbett proved the case and prisoner who had nothing to say, was fined 5s and costs.—Alfred Stanway, Buttermarket-street. for allowing his chimney to be on fire at 6.45 on Wednesday evening was fined Is including costs.—A workingman named Cannon Blackburne, 27, Bostock-street. was summoned for keeping a dog without a license on November 27th, and on the evidence of P,C. Lewis was fined 7s 6d. TUICSDAY.—Before Mr. Birchall (in the chair), Alderman Hntchinson. and Dr. Fox. DRUNK IN CHATIGE.—Hugh Daley was summoned for having been drunk in charge of a horse and cart in Knots- ford-road at 12.40 on Monday morning.—P.S. Hollingworth gave evidence and prisoner was fined 108 including costs. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. Birchall (in the chair), and Alderman Monks. WAIrrED LODGINGS AND GOT THEM.—John O'Brien was charged with being an absentee from the let South Lancashire Regiment, stationed at Orford Barracks since the 30th of November. P.C. Monks said be was on duty in Butter- anarket-street, and the prisoner came to him and asked him -where he could obtain lodgings. Witness noticed he was in a dirty condition and brought him to the Police Station. Prisoner there admitted that be broke out of the barracks on Friday night. —Superintendent Monaghan : You found him odgings. (Laughter.) Prisoner was remanded to await an escort, and the constable was recommended for the usual reward- "</p>
        <p>"Why a rat would have starved on what I ate. Appetite I had none. Sometimes I got so swollen at the waist that I couldn't fasten my dress. "Now and then my head turned quite dizzy and stupid. The chairs, tables, and other articles of furni-</p>
        <p>ture seemed suddenly alive, and dancing a lively jig around the room, like a lot of jolly Irishmen at a fair." To see how strong and capable woman a Mrs Eliza- beth Rollings now is, you would never believe that she could have spoken of herself in such words as those. Yet immense changes happen, and, thank God, changes for the better as well as for the worse. Day follows night, and joy wipes the tears from the red eyes of Sorrow,and (again I say) thank the dear Lord for that. "For six months," says the lady, in a letter dated April 4th, 1900, and written at her home, 42, Woodbine- grove, Penge, "I had a fearful time with indigestion. My poor body was never free from aches and pains especially between the shoulders, in the small of the back and across the chest. Every morning I awoke withla sickening taste in my mouth. "At night sleep refused to visit my pillow, although I was always languid and weary enough to make it welcome. Often, however, I dare not lie down for fear of choking. " What with restless nights and little food, I got to be so feeble I could just manage to crawl about the house to see after the girls who work for me in my laundry. "Oh, it was a weary time—all day long waiting for the night, and at night tossing sleepless:on the bed wait- ng for the morning. "All the time I was attended by a doctor, but he re- fused to send me any mere medicine unless:l:took to my bed.</p>
        <p>"I coulint a-fford to do that if thers'Avas any-'way to keep out of bed, and so I said to myself, I will give up everything else and try what Mother Seigel's Syrup will do for me.</p>
        <p>"To my great delight I wag relieved after a few doses, and before I had used up the fourth bottle I was completely cured. "I still take it occasionally for any of the little'ail- ments that will come upon the best and healthiest of us, and without it—for it is a wonderful tonic—l don't think I should have strength to do my work. "One of my laundry girls was troubled with indiges- tion and pains round the heart, and I gave her Seigel's Syrup. Less than half a bottle cured her. lam always recommending it, and you may publish what I have said if you wish."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MoNDAY.—Pefore Messrs G. L. Davies and W Timmins. LicEcistiG BustNEss.—Transfers:—The following transfers weregranted. Sarah Elizabeth Latham to William Wildgoose Wilson's Hotel; Thomas Harrison to James Fleury Le Cott- teur. , Sloon Inn: William Coble' to John William Fitzsim- mons, Friendship Tavern; Alexander Carruthers to James Viroocber, Oddfellows Arms. Apolleatiqns for new Licences —Mrs Elizabeth Kearon applied for the licence of the Cleve— land Hotel; and Mrs Elizabeth Houghton applied for that of the Mersey Vaults, latterly held by her husband Charles.— The applications were granted. DRUNK AND DL3ORDEULY.— For being drunk and disorderly In Church-qtreet, oa the 25th November, John Flannigan, labourer, Water-,treet, Runcorn, was fined Sc. and 83. 6d, costs, or 7 days —The case was proved by P.C. Booth. laSertooL Cesr..9.—The following parents were proceeded against by the Runcorn School Board. and fined 6d. and 49. 6d. costs, In each case, for neglecting the education of their children :_William Martin, Gilbert-street; Mary Taylor, Oakland-street ; Peter Knight, Mill-street; Joseph Moore, Brickiln Houses ; George Worrall. elder. Boats, R,n- corn ; an attendance order was also granted against Oliver Ronghton, in respect of his son.—The several cases were proved by School Attendance Officers Bowyer and Howard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>MIRACULOUS CURE OF ASTHMA</head>
        <p>MIRACULOUS CURE OF ASTHMA</p>
        <p>The statement which—we publish below confirms the claim of Dr Scbiffmann that he has now discovered an absolute remedy for Asthma, a disease which has hereto- fore baffled the skill of the most noted physicians. lons WILLIAM LOWE, liloplz:inson's Yard, Chesterfield, Derbyshire says :—" For two years I was an incessant sufferer from Asthma, and the winter before last I was unable to work at all, but after using two boxes of your Asthma Core I was quite cured. Last winter I did not lose a day's work, nor did I suffer from a single attack. I bad tried marry remedies before but nothing did me any good, with the exception of • Schiffmann's Asthma Cure,"which has cnnpletely cured me." "Schiffmann's Asthma Cure" is sold by all Chemists at 2s 6d and 4s 6d per package, or can be obtained by remitting the amount to Dr R Schiffmann's British DepOt, 31, Snow Bill, London. E.C. _ CURE Or CONSUMPTION BY DR. LOCOCK'S PULMONIC WAPERS.—From Mr Richard Meredith. Kingston, Hereford- shire•—"l had a violent ;cough ; many persons gave their opinion that I should not be long in this world. I had the first advice to no purpose, when, after using Dr Locock's Wafers. lam now as well as ever. It is my opinion that these Wafers are a gift from God," Dr Locock's Wafers give Instant relief, and rapidly cure Asthma. Consumption, Coughs, Bronchitis, and all Disorders of the Breath and Lungs. They h lye a pleasant taste. Price Is lid and 2s 9d per box. Sold by all medicine vendors. Advt (4). WRITING ao. To become a kood Business Writer, take lessons from PROMISOR Satt7s. 47. GREENILL-STREET, WLIALIMPS. Open every day and evenkig.—Advt.</p>
