      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>WHERE ARE THE DEAD?</head>
        <p>WHERE ARE THE DEAD?</p>
        <p>Where are the dead for whom we mourn, The friends who left to ne'er return: Who shared with us in early years, Earth's chequered dream of joys and tears ? We pause and listen, but no sound Comes from yon starry heights profound— No voice reaponsive to our prayer, To tell us that the dead are there. Where are the dead ? The earth replies, With me their destined portion lies : Formed of my clay, to clay consigned, All else for ever left behind. Deprived of sight, deprived of breath, They sleep the dreamless sleep of death : Each in his narrow lot, alone, To every eye but Heaven's unknown. Where are the dead ? The winds that aweep. In mournful cadence o'er the deep Speak to our hearts, where'er we be, Of tales of sorrow on the sea : Where brave men dare its storms and gloom, And thousands meet a watery tomb, Where loving hands no tribute biing, But winds and %VIM% their dirges sing. Where are the dead ? All in their graves, In holy ground and 'neath the waves :</p>
        <p>Those graves their goal, the common lot, Where all things human are forgot. Beyond this point we cannot go, For all is mystery here below : And Heaven alone can predicate Man's future and eternal state. J. H. W. CLINDINNING.</p>
        <p>Tribute to the Mayor.</p>
        <p>'Me Liverpool and District Congregational Magazine" says: Mr. Roberts has for the second time been elected to the Mayoralty of Warrington. He and his father before him have been thorough going in their loyalty to Nonconformist convic- tions, and in the services which they have given to our church in that town. The present Mayor has been for years in the front of political work. He is a Liberal in politics, and sound in the temperance cause; but like the vast majority of public-spirited men, he has worked just as hard in the church. The reward of such consistency in public services alway follows. Partisanship may keep some people silent, but all men honour consistency. The people of Warrington have come to honour Mr. Roberts. For many years he has been alderman, and now for the second time he has been elected Mayor. No one asked a question as to what such a man was likely to do on Mayor's Sunday. He went to his own people amongst whom he had been accustomed to worship, and the Corporation wemt with him. In the evening he went to the Parish Church, and along with the members of the Corporation, Rev. John Yonge, who is Mayor's Chaplain during Mr. Roberts' term of office, accompanied him. This is matter of courtesy, and it affords opportunity for Christians of different ways of thinking to shake hands. We rejoice in it for that reason; and it is all the more manly when it is done without belittling your own side." There is Security in A feat drop. on onmothe.g toothbrush</p>
