      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Missionary Services at St. Barnabas'</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Missionary Services at St. Barnabas'</p>
        <p>Church•</p>
        <p>Services on behalf of the Universities' Mission to Central Africa, were held in St. Barnabas' Church, War- rington, on Sunday, the preacher being the Rev. H. W. Trott, M.A. (organising secretary of the Northern Pro- vince). There were good congregations at all the ser- vices especially in the evening, and the offertories were very satisfactory. At the morning service the rev. ger.tleman took for his text " Thy Kingdom come," and in the evening preached from the words "Come over to Macedonia and help us." In the afternoon he ad- dressed a children's service. The sermons were of a most interesting and instructive character, giving an in- sight into the methods of conducting the mission. Dur- ing the course of his remarks in the evening the preacher observed thatiDr Livingstone might he called the founder f the mission. In the year 1857 he returned to England after his travels in Central Africa and appealed to the great universities of Oxford and Cambridge in a number of very earnest speeches for the church to send out missionaries to Central Africa in order to try and lead the Africans up to a better state of things, to give them a knowledge of God and of His love, His goodness and power. The mis,ion was founded in 1859 and in 1861, the first Bishop, Bishop Mackenzie, was consecra- ted at Capetown. After spending some few years to the south of Lake Nyasa, the missionaries were so discour- aged by the terribly unhealthy climate,and by the fierce hostility of the native tribes, that after the death of Bishop Mackenzie the headquarters were transferred to Zanzibar. Here the great slave market of the world existed, and the first great glory achieved by the Mission was in 1873, when the slave market was bought and the cathedral of Christ Church built upon its site. After a number of institutions for the betterment of the natives had been founded at Zanzibar the missionaries struck out into the interior of the continent in two directions, towards the north- west and the southwest. The great aim from the beginning had been to establish an African church, with African teachers and clergymen. There were at the present time twelve African clergy and 150 lay readers and teachers, and it was hoped that when the African was better able to stand on his own feet and be trusted and depended upon, the English workers could retire and leave the church to be worked entirely by the Africans themselves. The work was one which deserved earnest support and liberal contributions from those at home in England. On Monday evening the Vicar (Rev J H G Hollingworth) delivered a lecture upon the work of the mission. It was illustrated by a large number of limelight views, the lantern being manipulated by Mr. Parkinson.</p>
      </div>
