      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Warrington's Third Win over</p>
        <p>Widnes.</p>
        <p>Widnes and Warrington met for the third time this season on Saturday, one match being in the South West Lancashire Cup Competition. As the previous matchas had resulted in victories for Warrington and this, the third, was at home, the locals were quite confident of winuing• In fact, they were almost too confident, and Widnes very nearly s2cured the verdict. Both teams turned out as selected, and the weather was not at all unsuitable, though the ground was rather soft. Mr. Kennedy, of Broughton, was the referee, and the players lined out as follows :</p>
        <p>Warrington Hallam, full back ; Fish, Isherwood, Dickenson and Hockenhall, three-quarters ; Burgess and Duckworth, half backs ; Taylor, Boardman, Mere- weather, Eden, Scholtze, Swift, Andrews, and Swift, junior. Widnes ; Kinsella, full back ; Tilley, Wickham, Lomax and Leadbetter, three-quarters; Douglas and Flalligan, half backs ; Aspey, Leyshon, Curtis, Riley, McDonald, Webster, Moss, and Handley, forwards. Warrington started and immediately made a dash for the Widnes line, Isherwood and Fish being nearly over. Kinsella touched down and gave his side relief, but War- rington were soon back again. Mereweather picked up and ran to the line where he passed to Eden, who mulled the ball. Warrington attacked hotly, and Fish got oval, but the whistle had gone for some informality. Duckworth twice spoiled Warrington's chances by kicking dead, but a 4 round of passing by Burgess, Isherwood Dickenson, and Hockeshall enabled the latter to score at the corner. Fish made a good attempt at goal. Widnes kicked out from centre and Hockenhall mulled an easy catch. The widnes forwards were oa him in a minute and before he could recover, Riley dashed over with the ball, making the sides level. Kinsella took the goal kick and landed a fine goal. This unexpected lead did Widnes a lot of good and they began to get the better of the homesters, especially in the packs. Hallam had to touch down before a rush, and then Isherwood and Mereweather took play to the other end, the latter tackl- ing Kinsella grandly. Fish tried another shot at goal and had the hard luck to hit one of the uprights, Widnes responding energetically. Wickham picked up in the loose and very smartly dropped a goal for Widnes. Warrington now rallied and Isherwood made a brilliant dash down touch, passing to Fish wha dodged over at the corner amid cho rs. 111-luck still dogged him, however, for the touch-judge ruled that he had stepped over the border. Warrington strove hard to reduce the lead and just before half-time, Fish landed a goal from a difficult angle, Widnes having been penalised for offside play. Half-time :—Widnes, severs points; Warrington, five points. Aspey re-commenced and Widnes were the first to assume the aggressive, Hallam clearing nicely. Fish kicked nicely from touch and the home forwards dashed down the field in a body. Widnes were pt,nalized again and Fish tried to lower the goal, but failed by an inch. Bad passing by the home backs lost Warrington several chances, while the persistent off-side play of the Widnes halves prevented many movements, which might have resulted in a score. Warrington played desperately and twice when near the line Dicker- son failed to give Hockenhall the ball when the latter had a certain try before him. The Widnes men gave nothing,' away and looked like keeping their line intact. Fish again kicked splendidly into the goal mouth, and a terrific rush by the Warrington forwards resulted in Eden touching down before the ball went dead. Widnes disputed the try but the referee allowed it, and amid tremendous cheering from the relieved spectators, Fish placed a goal. Widnes tried five three-quarters after this, but play ruled even, Fish once missing an easy pass from Isherwood. The finish came with the score reading : Warrington, 2 goals, 2 tries (10 points) ; Widnes, 2 goals, 1 try (7 points). Runcorn Surprised at Barrow. Far the first time in the history of football Runcorn put in an appearance at Cavendish Park,and they will have cause to remember it, too. Contrary to expectations, the redoubtable men in green met with a rude reverse, and the knowledge of this will furnish another big sur- prise in football centres not in the vicinity of North Lancashire. But Barrow have been doing so well lately in advancing step by step from a very lowly position that many in the district fancied the ground team would just be strong enough to win,especially as Runcorn were without Houghton, Faulkner and Reece. Hence the big gate to witness the encounter. When the gains com- menced a strong wind was blowing from end to end,and this Barrow faced in the first half. The early stages were finely contested. The " Greens " front rank per- sistently made headway in several rushos,in which com- bination was a prominent feature, and the home backs had a pretty lively time in keeping Farmer and his com- rades on the right side of the line. Wilkinson then missed the ball clean in attempting a flying kick, and had just sufficient time to recover himself and the ball and prevent Lloyd and Treweek taking the leather over the line. D Jones was next guilty of an error of judgment, and allowed Barrow to make a big incursion into the " Green's " territory. This movement led up to a try for Barrow, for Bowker got possesion and kicked across to Dickie, who broke away from the embrace of a couple of visitors and scored. Runcorn seemed to fall off a little ; their backs fielding somewhat indifferently. Runcorn next made a big effort to rub I.'off the deficit, but the movement was only partially saccessful, for after Jolley bad placed a goal from a mark he created a clear open- ing, which was grievously declined by Gayter. The teams turned out with Barrow leading by one point. The second half was peculiarly played, and apparently the only explanation is that Runcorn were de:ermined to stop the home team's passing just as Barrow were deter- mined to prevent their opponents from scoring if pos- sible, and the game simply resolved itself into a big kicking duel between Bowker and thereon one sidesand Jolley and others on the other side. It is not often spectators revel in this kind of football, but the kicking was so clean,lengthv.and loftv,andi the fielding so accur</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>E NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. [By " Ovalist."]</head>
        <p>E NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. [By " Ovalist."]</p>
        <p>Over confidence nearly lost Warrington the match with Widnes on Saturday,and the lesson that it does not do to hold a team too cheap seems very hard to learn. Warrington have made the same mistake before, and ought to knew by now that because they have beaten a team twice they can not go on doing so ad infinitum. Widnes were by no means resigned to a licking and came over fully determined to make Warrington go all the way for the coveted two points. The fact that they were leading for three fourths of the game and only lost by a three-point:margin in the end shows that they very nearly exceedEd their own expectations.</p>
        <p>--- Warrington started in very promising style, and the dash they showed at the commencement boded ill for tthe Widnesians. A very severe attack was main- abed on the Widnes line, and in less than ten minutes flockenhall put the finishing touch on a smart passing bout. Warrington might have followed this up with a big score but for one event which was not calculated upon. rho nest minute the scorer made a terrible mull near his own line, and presented Widnos with a soft try, which Kinsella kicked a goal from. This altered matters considerably, and Widnes having once obtained the lead showed a strong inclination to stick to it. Warrington found their efforts repeatedly frustrated and when a smart drop-kick increased the margin to four points things looked decidedly serious. Desperate efforts were made and before the interval Fish managed to wipe off half the deficit with a penalty goal after he had had hard luck with a try.</p>
        <p>The position of affairs at the interval was interesting for Warrington were still in a minority and full of deter- mination to win. On the other side there was as much determination and only a little less confidence. The man Widnes feared was, of course, Fish and they played an old trick in order to check his scoring powers, and with the referee being none too smart it worked well. The idea was this. If the ball appeared to be coming out on the Warrington side of the pack then one of the Widnes halves immedi- ately ran off-side and risked the penalty. Occasionally there was a free-kick, but in any case it prevented the ball travelling to the wings very often: Partly in con- sequence of this Fish did not score a try, though ha missed an opportunity near the closQ. Ho was,however, responsible for Warrington's ultimate victory. One of bis admirably-judged punts landed the ball under the goal and the home forwards broke through all oppo i- tion, Eden scoring the winning point,and Fish adding a goal. Widnes still played up pluckily but were never near scoring and had to retire defeated.</p>
        <p>As much credit—if not more—is attached to the per- formance of the visitors as that of the homesters. They have improved a lot since their last visit, and the raw material in the back division is rapidly getting knocked into shape. In addition they have plenty of pluck, and really deserved to win on Saturday,tor Warrington's last try had an element of luck ahout it. In Kinsella Widnes possess a full-back good enough for any club, and the once great Baty was never of more service to his side than is his successor. The other backs are not clever, but they are not muffs and can get through the game with few mistakes. As a scoring power they are decidedly weak, however and the forwards had to supply the deficiency. The Widnes eight have always been a vigorous lot, and on Saturday they beat Warrington easily in elo b work but were not in it for speed. Getting the ball is the most important thing, and in this direction they will take some beating. Warrington's weakest place is in the pack, where lack of weight tells every match that is played.</p>
        <p>Hallam played another fine game and got through a lot of work in first-class style, while Isherwood and Fish were always active. The former seems to be getting his dash and confidence back again. Dickenson and Hockenhall were both off-colour. Most fault was to be found with the halves, who did not pass often enough They went very near to losing the game through their bad judgment. Warrington's advantage lay behind and the halves ought to have given the threequarters every opportunity. Had they played as they did against Rochdale Hornets a big score would have been the result. They must pass out, and I hope the committee will insist on them doing so; The forwards deserve praise for their untiring exertions, and have the satisfac- tion of knowing that they pulled the match out of the fire. The League table up to date is as follows:</p>
        <p>For Agst. P. W. L. D. C+. T. P. a T. P. P Swinton 12...12... 0... 0...13 38-140... 9 4 30...24 Oldham ll.. 9. 2. 0...12 34-126... 7 7 35...18 Runcorn 12.. 8. 4. 0...19 21-101... 9 16— 66...16 Broughton leng'sl?... 7... 5... 0...14 17— 79... 8 13— 55...14 Warrington 12. 6.. 5. 1..14 21— 91... 9 14— 60...13 Salford 12.. 6. 6. 0...19 28-122...19 18— 92...121 Rochdale Hornetsll... 5... 4... 2...11 11— 55...15 20— Leigh l2. 5.. 7. 0..13 15— 71...12 23... 93...10 Stockport 11 4. 6. 1.. 5 10— 43... 6 9 39... 9 *St. Helens 12. 4.. 6. 2. 9 10— 48...11 21... 85... 8 Barrow ll. 4.. 7. 0..10 14— 62...14 18— 82... 8 filillom ll. 3. 8. 0 8 8— 40...13 18— 80... 6 Widnes 12. 3.. 9. 0..10 7 41...12 27-105... 6 Wigan ll. 1.. 8. 2. 7 9 41...20 35-145... 4 *SC. Helens have had two points doductei for a breach of the rules.</p>
