      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 7,1.90 P</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 7,1.90 P</p>
        <p>A NOBLE CAUSE. The Rev. Benjamin Waugh and the</p>
        <p>Work of the N.S.P.C.C.</p>
        <p>Lady Helpers Required.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <p>It was to be regretted that the ineetine held in the Parr HAll on Thursday last week in connection with the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Cnildren was but sparsely attended. The time appointed for the commencement of the proceedings was half- ') tst four, but shortly before that time, a telegram was ineeived containing an intinaatioo that the Rev Benjamin Waugh, of London, the founder and Director of the Society who was announced to !*deliver an address, hae by some mistake been taken on to Preston, and would therefore not reach Warrington until 5.20. The meeting was adjourned until 5.15, and as a consequence, the attendance was not so large as it otherwise might have been. The chair was occupied by Mr William Long, J.P., who was accompanied on the platform by the Rev B Waugh, the Mayor (Alderman Roberts), Mr T Birchall, J.P., and Councillor William Roberts. In introducing Mr. Waugh, the Chairman said that that gentleman had done more in the interests of infant humanity than anyone else with whom he was ac- quainted. (Applause.) The Rev. BENJAMIN WAI7GII then delivered an ex- tremely interesting address, appertaining to the national and local work of the Society. He said they were all assembled as friends of suffering children and the great question was, how could they promote the interests of the children P In 184 there was no society for the prevention of cruelty to children in the United Kingdom. There were many institutions for the removal of suffering children into happy, Christian homes, but that was not the object of their Society. Its object was to remove the evils 9, hich existed in the homes, and not to leave res- ponsibility but to enforce it. The speaker then proceeded to refer to the three methods by which the Society carried on this work—first by investigation, secondly by warning, and thirdly by prosecution. When he commenced tne work, every one said there was no such thing as- sufferine of the children in Eoglish homes. People might spoil their children, or send them into the streets at night to sell and hawk, but cruelty was impossible. In 1889 they ob- tained a statute which enabled them to test the incredi- ble and he thought they would admit with him that those who were then incredible were now precisely the reverse. For they had not only dealt with 500,000 suffer- ing children in England, Wales, Scotland and Ireland but they had inflicted through Her Majesty's justices and judges, who administered under the statute, 2,800 years of imprisonment upon fathers and mothers for cruelty to children. They had sent to gaol 27,000 fathers and mothers for breaking the law, and they might have sent 250,000 but they warned them. Warrington amongst the pioneers of the towns of the United Kingdom undertook some share of the work and in 1892 was allotted a I tree area of the country which it was becoming impossible to work. They had one Inspector, and they knew Inspector Hackett was one of the most courageous, industrious, hearty and judicious Inspectors the society had ever had. (Hear, hear.) But they were doing to-day more than it was possible for them to do thoroughly and they were injur- ing the chances of good health of a young, whole- some and energetic offic-r. They were leaving, in con- sequence, a large number of children undiscovered, 2 whose sufferings were 11013 e the less and who could only round off their sufferings in the grave, to which they were tending daily and in which alone" the wicked would cease from troubling, and their little limbs would be at rest." There was a proposal before the committee to divide the big area into at least two districts, and they intended to have an inspector appointed at Run- corn, Widnes and Freicham, to relieve Warrington from the reaponsibilities of serving the children in those districts. What he wanted them in Warrington to do was to feel that this work was a duty, and also one of their greatest plea- sures. He wished as many as possible to volunteer to do ' the work that wa%necessary for the full discharge of these responsibilities. They required women, with women's hearts and indauntibility of spirit, and the women's winsome persuasive aid, to circulate their literature in every house in the district, and to call upon the people a few days afterwards. The literature would be soliciting a subscription. The toe:oe who had made the Society what it was throughout the United King- dom were the lady collectors of whom they had on their registers 10,800 and who brought in £47,000. He asked them, what was the glory in fighting ag.alnst the Boer in South Africa, compared with the glory of the fight against the brute in England ? The Govern- ment voted £70,000,000 for the prosecution of the war, and the newspapers told them that they would have to vote further'millions;to supplement it.He hed no objection to the country raising funds enough to accomplish the purpose of its aovernment, but he asked them, was there any purpose so worthy of a Government as the preser- vation of the young life that was to become in a few years the citizen3.—Proceeding to speak at length upon the method of working by the Society, the rev, gentleman said their object was not only to stop the suqerings of the children, but to stop the source of the sufferings be improving the character of the parent. He attributed most of the neglect to selesh- neas on the part of the parents. Men liked their beer, and their tobacco and when they were sent to prison of course they had neither of these luxuries. Tl4e speaker proceeded to narrate instances in which men had re- formed after being sent to prison, because they had no tobacco there. On that point they touched men to a de- gree that would astonish them. He had almost come to regard Sir Walter Raleigh as the feunder of the Society, or at least the lever or fulcrum by which they lifted up the people with whom they were dealing. (Laughter.) In conclusion, he said they had discovered the evil and the remedy and all they desired were more workers in order that they might be able to apply the remedy with greater fulness throughout the land and with greater ease to themselves. (Applause.) In moving a We of thanks to Mr Waugh for his ad- dress, the Mayor said there was plenty of work for the Society to do in Warrington and the Society had so far done that work very satisfactorily. He was of opinion that the cases which came before the Warrington magis- trates were not so much the result of starvation, as the abominable filth in which the children were allowed to live, largely owing to the drunken habits of the parents. He hoped the people of the town would not be behind in pushing forward the interests of the Seciety,when its 1 claims were so strong upon them. (Applause.) .11Ir T BIRCIIALL seconded and the motion was carried with acclamation.</p>
        <p>The R.v B WAtiGH having responded, the services of Mr Long in the chair, were ackawrledged on the pro- pwition rof Mr BIRCRALL sec-mdeci by the MAYOR. Tbe proceedings then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>POISONED BEER</head>
        <p>THE POISONED BEER</p>
        <p>SENSATION.</p>
        <p>Cases at Warrington.</p>
        <p>- - The epidemic which has been attributed to beer poisoning has been less marked in Warrington than in man y of the neighbouring towns. Up to the present the cases reported have been very few considering the large number of inhabitants. But from inquiries made yesterday, it is absolutely certain that Warrington has not entirely escaped from the epidemic. Dr. Battle, the senior house surgeon at the War- rington Infirmary, informs us that he has one case of arsenical beer poisoning in the Infirmary and two persons are being treated in their own homes. The case which is being treated at the Infirmary is a fairly-severe one, biz* the other two are of a milder type. In addition several cases of alcoholic neuritis have come under his observation.</p>
        <p>Dr. Braide, the medical officer at the Workhruae, informed a reporter of the " Examiner" last evening that none of the inmates at the Workhouse Infirmary were suffering from arsenical poisoning. There were, however, about six inmates suffering from alcobolic neuritis, but the number was very little in excess of the average number under treatment.</p>
        <p>We understand an analytical test of beers sold in Warrington is being made, and the report will be pre- sented in the course of a few days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>WARRINGTON CALEDONIAN ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CALEDONIAN ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>11 Friday being St Andrews Day it was only natural that local Scotsmen should wish to fittingly celebrate the occasion. In accordance with annual custom the Warrington Caledonian Association held their annual dinner in the Pattern Arms Hotel last evening. About 40 sat down and did full justice to the excellent viands, including of course, real Scotch haggis. Mr B Shearer (the president of the association) presided and amongst those present were Councillors Tinnion and Forshaw. Messrs Alex Neil, J A Ewart, Dr Ferguson, J Finlayson, W Fletcher, J Davenport, G F Whittle, J F Lee, Thomas May, J A McGregor j D McLean, Bowman, Cowperthwaite, W B New- man, F (aylor, J McDermott. B.nnter, J Watson, F Booth, Whitehouse, Scattergood and W J Robson the energetic hon. secretary). A letter of apology for non-lttendance was"re- ceived from the Mayor, who was prevented from attending by a cold. -</p>
        <p>_ At the conclusion of the repnst a toast list; Nves submitted. The loyal toasts—" The Queen" and "Duke and Duchess of Rothesay," were proposed by the Chairman and musically honoured—Coun- cillor Fornhaw proposed " Army, Navy, and Reserve Forces,", which was responded to by Dr. Ferguson.</p>
      </div>
