        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>MANY a sweetheart has a sour disposition. It takes some worms a terribly long time to turn.</p>
        <p>AFTER a man discovers how little he knows, he Segias to suspect that possibly others do not know 4 much as they pretend. TEE QUESTION.—Mrs. A.: "I bought a lot of at the bargain salla yesterday." Mrs. B. : "Did you get anything you wanted 2" A TREORY.—Josh "It's a wonder none of them fellers ever found the North Pole." Hiram: " Mebbe it ain't there." EVERY INDICATION.—First Actor: "Was the piece well done ?" Second Actor : "I should say so. The critics roasted it."</p>
        <p>ADMIT occasionally to your friend that he knows more than you do. It gives him a great im- pression of your discrimination and good sense. _ _ .</p>
        <p>- WHEN we discover the faults of ciur friends we are happy ; when we discover the limits of our friends without being hippy we are great. THE; WIFE—" Wilt a sweet tuilo there is on baby's face, John." The Hasband—" Yes ; he's probably dreaming that he's keeping me awake." "I SEE your son has turned out an artist, Mr. Gibbs. How did that happen ?" " I dunno, sor, his mother thinks he was left that way by the measles."</p>
        <p>"THE Professor was in a very bad humour all the time he was conducting the examination," said one undergraduate tc another. " Yes," replied the latter, "it was a cro!s examination." "Now, papa, dear, when Mr. Timmons calls on you, don't be hasty." " 'fluty ? 1 guess not• I've been thinking what I'd do to him for more than a month."</p>
        <p>To UN covEß.—"There's a burglaz in the house !" she gr,,si,ed. "I have never yet uncovered my head for any man," her husband rejoined, with an affectation of hauteur. MAN at the door: "I called to see the head of the family. Mr. Henpeck : "She's out. Any message?" JACK: "Who's your friend ? " Oscar: "Friend. That isn't a friend. That's my wife." WREN we are not satisfied, we call it ambition; when others are similarly affected, we call it dis- content.</p>
        <p>BEFORE THE ELOPEMENT.—IIe "I think it would be a good move to take your lather's bicycle." She : "Oh, George, i we do that, he will never forgive us 1" IN THE ALPS.—Driver (arriving at a cliff, turn- ing to the passengers in his conveyance) : "Here the road is only passable for asses, and I must request the party to step out and walk ahead." 'SPOILED. Lucy: " Clara's honeymoon was completely spoiled." Alice : " How ? " Lucy : "The papers containing the account of the wedding did not reach her."</p>
        <p>EASILY DISTINGUISHED.—Mamma: "Why, a delicacy means something very nice to eat?" Johnny: "Oh, I know. It means the things papa can't eat on account of his dyspepsia." HE BLAMED HIMSELF.—" Yes, a wheelwoman ran me down; but I suppose it was my own fault." " How ?" " Well, I ought to have known enough to let a woman have her own way." A NEW BRAND. Hostess (politely) : "And will you have a little light wine, Dorothy?" Dorothy (quietly) : "No, I thank you. Mamma doesn't allow me to drink spiritual liquors." PROBABLY.—A: "The jury considered the case for several hours and then didn't agree." B: "That shows the fully of masculine juries. A jury of women would have disagreed much sooner than that."</p>
        <p>TOWNE.—" It's really wonderful how nature always manages to strike a balance in all her de- partments." Browne—" For instance?" Towne —" Well, in winter snow comes down, while in summer ice goes up." Is THE BARNYARD.—First Hen—" What are those young bantams fighting about?" Second Hen—" Oh, they are disputing about the question. which is the mother of the chick—the hen that lays the egg or the incubator?" THE DECORATIVE CRAZE.—" Does your wife do much fancy work?" "Fancy work? She won't even let a porous plaster come into the house with- out crotcheting a red scollop round it and running yellow ribbon through the holes." His ERROR.—Mrs. Brown: "And the burglar pointed a pistol at you?" Mrs. Jones: "Yes ; and I was paralysed with fear until he said, 'Don't speak! That gave me an idea, and I just shouted for help and he ran away." ALREADY TAKEN IN.—" Got any hopes of being taken into the firm?" "The firm," said the sad- faced hireling, "has already taken me in. They have failed, owing me arrears of salary for three months. Hadn't you heard of it?" QCITE Ricarr, WILLIE.—Willie Smith was play- ing with the Jones boys. His mother called him. "Willie, don't you know those are bad boys for you to Flay with?" "Yes, mother," said Willie, "I know that, but then I am a good boy for them to play with." "IF I were to die, would you marry again?" asked Mrs. Bikers of her husband. "My dear," replied Mr. Bickers, "an able philosopher once said that no man should be called a hopeless fool until he had made a fool of himself twice in the same way." SAPSMITH : "Miss Brisk is rather—aw—dense ; don't you think so?" Sissington : "I don't know. What makes you say that?" Sapsmith : " Why, when I awaked her if I might be her valen- tine, she never saw the pretty sentiment of the question at all. No, bah Jawve She replied that she did not care for comic valentines."</p>
        <p>Au. WORTHY. —Stranger (in Arizona): "But when you lynch a man so hurriedly isn't there great danger of making a mistake?" Broncho Pete: "Not the least in the world, stranger; yet can't make no great mistake lynching anybody in dis here town."</p>
        <p>THE TRITE SIGNIFICANCE.—Kerrigan : "Do yez belavo in dhrames, Riley?" Riley: "Oi do." Kerrigan: " Phwat's it a soign av if a married man dhrames he's a bachelor?" Riley: "Its a soign thot he's goin' to mate wid a great disap- ppintment—when he wakes oop." SHORT-SIGHTED.—He: "It is strange how fre- quently inventors fail to realise the importance of their own work." She: "What is the particular instance?" He: "Why, here is a statement that the inventor of the hair-pin intended it to be used simply in dressing the hair 1" MRS. OLDBRIDE met Mrs. Newlywed, who _</p>
        <p>walking very fast. "You seem to be in a hurl said the former. " Yes ; my husband promist.s. to give me a cheque this afternoon to cover all that I owe, so I am going out to run up a few more bills before he comes home."</p>
        <p>SIZED HER u.—Superintendent---" I think that lady over there is not being properly waited upon." Floor-walker—" Oh, she doesn't want to buy any- thing. Everything that has been shown her she has delared perfectly lovely. She hasn't found fault in the least with anything." TRAGEDIAN . I won't perjure myself by going through that .Eichard 111. ghost scene again. Manager: Perjure yourself? What do you mean? Tragedian: Here I have to swear I've seen ghosts walking about me, when I know well enough the ghost hasn't walked since we left London. ONE evening a man was performing the old trick of producing eggs from a pocket-handkerchief, when he remarked to a little boy in front—" Say, my boy, your mother can't get eggs without hens, can she ?' "Of course she can," said the boy. Why, how is that ? " asked the conjuror. "She tteeps ducks." .</p>
        <p>"How do I know that you really love me ?" she asked. "What assurance have I that you would be willing to make sacrifices and endure hardahips for my sake?" He looked at her in reproachful astonishment, and exclaimed—" What more can you ask? Haven't I for six months refrained from laying violent hands on your little brother ? " DZOIDED.—" Look here," said he, "I want it settled once and for all who is to be the man of the house from now on I" "You are, of course," said she. "Do you suppose I wish to be anything so insignificant, vacillating, and contemptible as a man?"</p>
        <p>BEsonz AND AY/EL—During their engagement: "Little sweetheart that lovely auburn air of yonrs always looks beautiful, but since the wind has slightly mussed it, it looks positively; bewitching. You should always make lb a point to have it a trifle out of order." Two, years after marriage 'Say, Jane, for heaven sake go upstairs and that red hair of yours in order, it's enough to me mad. Why, it looks like the fringe on lounge."</p>
        <p>';'," • •• • ; ML' • GA, FERENT COLOURS A:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <p>Every Description of</p>
        <p>LETTERPRMS AND LITHOGRAPHIC PRINTING</p>
        <p>J. WALKER &amp; CO., , BEWSEY SMUT, WARRINGTON'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, DECEMBER 7 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, DECEMBER 7 1900.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. MARTIN FLANAGAN—Unsuitable for publication— possibly 6landerous.</p>
        <p>The Beer Scare.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln view of the prevailing anxiety of the public as to the purity and wholesomeness of the beers now on Eale,we have submitted samples of our ales for analyses, and found them perfectly satisfactory. In addition, all the materials in our Warrington and Burton Breweries have been subjected to examination, and all such materials in use in the production of cur beers are guaranteed free from arsenic or other deleterious ingre- dients.</p>
        <p>Yours etc., PETER WALKER Sz SON (, WARRINGTON AND BURTON) LIMITED. King-street Brewery, Warrington, Nov. 30, 1900. [ADVT.]</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHIS EKSERVEN LICENSING REFORM,,</p>
        <p>authority, upon whom his prospects depend. And it is not conceivable that a man should fitly and properly do, or be deemed to be doing, his duty, when he holds simultaneously two such incon- sistent positions as chairman of the Watch Committee, and chairman of a brewery company owning many tied houses in the same town. Yet several instances were revealed, in which this state of things existed with the most disastrous effect upon the efficiency of the police force, notably at Wigan, though Wigan was not an isolated case. Evidence was given of a similar character from many other places. It would in fact appear that some of the Watch Committee, instead of framing regulations, as the law enjoins, for the prevention of neglect and abuse, and for making the borough constables efficient in the dis- charge of their duty, go out of their way to produce the very opposite effect, at least so far as the administration of the licensing laws is concerned, and it is a N ary singular and noticeable fact, that while in other matters the chief constable is given a free hand and allowed to adopt his own methods to be judged by the results, in licensing matters he is often not allowed to act without constant refer- ence to the Watch Committee on points of detail. Such a state of things was once the ckse in Liver- pool, but now the wiser ptrlicy is adopted by the Watch Committee of merely settling the main lines ot aCtion, and letting the Chief Constable manage his own force in hi own way, and making him responsible for the results.</p>
        <p>In the words of the report of the Licensing Com- mission, "the evil aimed at amounts to a gross scandal." "The same disqualification should apply to members of the Watch Committee as now applies to members of the licensing au, hority, and further any person acting as solicitor or valuer for any brewery company or trade organisation should also be disqualified." Nowhere does the political influence of the liquor trade appear in a more sinister light than in this matter of the Watch Committees The recommenda- tion above qulted would do a great deal of good; but as a further safeguard, the chief constable should be given a more independent position, and as is now the law in the counties, should not be removable from his position without the sanction of the Home Secre• ary.</p>
        <p>CLOSING REGULATIONS. No part of the licensing law is more important than that which deals with closing regulations Before the Act of 1872 public-houses in the country did not close at all by law., and no part of the Act did so much good as that introducing uniform hours of closing. Yet licensed houses can still open for 191 hours daily in London, 17 hours daily in towns and populous places,and 16 hours in country districts, while on Sundays they can be open for 2 hours in the morning, and 4 hours ;5 in London) in the evening. One would think from looking at the figures that publicans and their employees must be overwhelmingly in favour of shortening the hours; Probably hey are, but not so the brewers, their masters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>Auditors' Report to the Rate-</head>
        <p>The Auditors' Report to the Rate-</p>
        <p>payers.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Having read the report of the meeting of the General Purposes Committee on Monday last in your contemporary of the 28th inst., I shall feel obliged if you will permit me to state that Warrington ratepayers would like to be assured by an outside indepc.ndent, impartial authority that the proceedings of the Corpora- tion during the last five years have been regular and aatisfactory. This assurance I think they ought to have, for nothing less will satisfy them. The Warrington Corporation has not been afraid of spending the ratn- payers' money, and any expense incurred for such objects ought to be willingly met. I have taken the trouble to enquire 4s to the Borough Auditors' last r€- port to the ratepayers and am very much astonished to find that it has not been published. How is this? How- ever difficult or disagreeable this subject may be, it will not do to gloss it over and to let it slide. No one ought to object to a searching inquiry. If there never had been anything improper it could do no harm, and if there have been irregularities, they should be made known and every Councillor should help to remove them.—Yours, etc., November 29tb, 1900.</p>
        <p>S. KENYON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>Corporation Contracts and Accounts.</head>
        <p>Corporation Contracts and Accounts.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee on Monday last, the above matter was refeired to and if the Mayor in his reference to the Warrington Slate Company, and Corporation Contracts, as regards the Company. himself, and family, means it as a joke I my- sell must protest that this is not the time nor is it the occasion for frivolity. If on the other hand, he was serious, then I feel sure the Mayor will do everything in his power to bring about a searching i, quiry into the matter. I hope that when the Coun- cil meets next Tuesday the matter will meet with the serious attention which it demands. I should like to know from those who are responsible why there is no auditors' report printed in the Corporation accounts of 1898 and 99. It has always been the custom up to that year for the auditors' report to be sent out to the ratepayers. I also find that this last few years the names of firms supplying goods or doing work for the Corporation are left out in the published accounts, and I would suggest that in the n3xt statement of accounts sent out to the ratepayers, we go back to the szood old plan of giving the names and amounts. It has teen suggested to me that the Corporation have no funds or power to pay for an inspection of the accounts of the Corporation for the last five years. Ido not sup- pose they will be asked to do this. I myself, and ethers who being sure that this matter affects the interest of the town to such serious extent, will subscrite to a fund for that purpose and I will give £lO on condition that an independent Committee of ratepayers shall appoint a first class firm of accountants and a small Committee of ratepayers, with the consent of the Town Council, to in, pect the acco unts,contracts and vouchers for the last fise years,</p>
        <p>Yours truly. JOHN PEMBERTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>RECORD BREAKERS.</head>
