      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>BY THE HON. SIDNEY PEEL</head>
        <p>BY THE HON. SIDNEY PEEL</p>
        <p>THE RECONSTITUTION OF THE LICENSING AUTHORITY, ETC.</p>
        <p>The question of the licensing authority is one that can be discussed without prejudice to the ques- tion of direct popular control Whatever system be finally adopted, except that of national prohibi- tion, there must be a licensing authority, and it is desirable to make that authority as effective as possible. Stress has been laid on the administrative func- tions of the licensing authority, and our prop )sals contemplate an extension of their present powers. We shall see, moreover, when we come to discuss hours of closing, that it will be desirable to confer wide discretionary powers von them It follows therefore that some popular element should be introduced.</p>
        <p>This can be done either by direct or indirect elec- tion. There is a good deal to be said for a board directly elected for this purpose, but on the other side it is undesirable to increase the present large number of popular elections,a number which has the effec- of diminishing public interest. On the otlpr hand,to have a board selected wholly by the county or town council might have the dangerous effect of causing their elections to turn wholly upon licens- ing questions to the detriment of other important local issues At the same time there are some good elements in the present lic-nsing authority, which it would be well to preserve,—for instance the ex- perience of many of them, and their position of in- dependence removed from the necessity of con- sidering the baser temptations of local politics A definite committee, chosen for a period of say three years, or even six, half selected by the justices of the division from their own body, half by the county council or town council, would seem to be the best me hod of combining the desired ele- ments.</p>
        <p>The appeal body is a more difficult point. Many people think that no appeal at all is necessary, and with a reformed licensing authority this would be still more the case. But it may well be that in case the committee is very equally divided, or in case new facts may have turned up, a rehearing may be desirable.</p>
        <p>Experience has shown that if appeal be allowed to an entirely external body, the effect is to largely remove the decision from the body which has studied the question more to one which has studied it less, and this in administrative matters can never be wholly satisfactory,especially when owing to the value of licensed houses nearly every refusal is likely to be appealed against. The best solution is that the appeal should be to a commi tee consti- tuted in the same manne- as the original authority, whether of the same number or of a rather larger one, sitting along with the original authority. Thus, the more unanimous the original authority are in their decision, the more likely they are to prevail in the end, as i 4 only right The objection that this would be to give men the right to sit in judgment on their own decision, would be a good one, if licensing matters were purely judicial business but this not being so, the objection falls to the ground. The plan proposed is now followed in boroughs, where new licenses granted by the licensing committee, are confirmed by the full bench including that committe and the granting of a new license is at least as important a matter as the refusal of a r6newal The proposed appellate body should act as a confirming authority as well as an appellate body, and thus a distinct simplification of the law would be obtained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>POLICE AND THE POLICE AUTHORITIES.</head>
        <p>THE POLICE AND THE POLICE AUTHORITIES.</p>
        <p>The dries of the police under the licensing laws consist in the prevention or prosecution of offences committed by the public, the supervision of licensed houses, and the de-ection ad prosecution of offences committed by licensed holders, and in reporting the conduct of licensed houses to the licensing authority. They have thus a very import- ant part to play On the whole it is remarkable that the ordinary policeman does as much as he does. Many difficulties stand in his way, the excessive number of houses to be supervised, their often faulty position and construction, in county districts the long distances to be covered. The temptations to which he is exposed are very great. The people among whom he lives and from whom he comes are perhaps not disposed to have high ideals, where drink is concerned; the publican has much to offer, free beer, subscriptions to sports, testimonials to retiring inspectors and so on. As the chairman of the licensing justices of the Tower division in London said, the temptations are "almost more than a man can bear."</p>
        <p>One of the worst offences which a publican can commit is permitting drunkenness:it is one of those which is most often committed, bit very seldom prosecuted. The extreme rarity of prosecutions for this offence, compared with the frequency of appre- hensions for drunkenness, most of which must have their origin in the public-house, indicates some de- fect either in the law or in the activity of the police, even when all allowance is made for the natural difficulty of detecting the offence and the ease with which a drinker may deceive a publican as to his condition. The law at present is certainly defective, and it should be incumbent on the publi^an, when a person is found drunk on his premises or seen leaving them in a drunken con- dition, to show that neither he nor his servants knew of the drunkenness, or that they did not with such a knowledge allow the drunken person to remain on his premises At present the onus of proof lies on the prosecutor. There is a good deal to be said for the proposal that a special force should be organised charged with the duty of inspecting licensed houses. But besides the inconvenience and financial troubles connected with the institution of such a force, there is a danger that the supervision of the ordinary police which cannot be dispensed with should be relaxed, and tomptations could be almost more easily held out to the special inspectors. There is, however, no reason why the licensing suthority should not have a certain number of officers of higher rank at'ached specially to their service,who should act under their orders,and report directly to : them on points on which they required informa- tion- These officers, whils so acting, would remain members of the police force, and would be changed from time to time, so as not to become too well- known.</p>
        <p>A great deal depends upon the Chief Constable if he is an active man, who understands the law and is keen to enforce it, his men will be keen and active also; but it, as is occasionally the case, he regar is his post as entailing little more responsi- bility than the command of a section of infantry, and is reluctant to trouble himself particularly about the licensing laws, whose enforcement is always troublesome and sometimes unpopular, his men cannot be expectPd to be active and take their duties seriously. A strong and intelligent chief constable, well acquainted with the law and realis- ing its importance, can do more good than perhaps any other single man. especially if he has a strong public opinion behind him. A strict supervision of licensed houses alone has had an extraordinary effect in diminishing drunkenness in several instances</p>
        <p>Unfortunately an active chief constable too often meets with many discouragemenrs, which he must be a strong men indeed to overcome. Tie is ham- pered by a difficulty in obtaining legal advice in prosecutions,and, when he does obtain a conviction, small' penalties are imposed by an apathetic bench, or if the license is by chance endorsed, the endorse- ment is very likely taken off on appeal to quarter sessions. Worst of all are the abuses connected with the administration of Watch Committees. A Watch Committee in a borough is a committee of one third of the Town Council, but has an existence independent of it except so far as its expenditure has to be confirmed. It has full power to appoint, dismiss, or suspend any constable, including the Chief Constable, at pleasure without appeal : and to forfeit, refuse, or diminish a pension (wi.h appeal to quarter sessions). The Chief Con- stable of a borough is thus entirely under the control of the Watch Committee; he cannor dismiss, appoint, or promote any of his officers without their approval. _ _</p>
        <p>. . The most serious complaint against the Watch Committee is that persons are frequently members of these bodies, who are more or less directly con- nected with the liquor traffic, and whose presence is a discouragement and a hindrance to the police in the performance of their duty. Though such persons may be actuated by the best motives, and desimus of impartially doing their duty and enforcing the laws, even to their own detriment, motives of this kind cannot be expected to be easily credited by the ordinary constable, who is more likely to relax his activity, when he reflects tha zeal may not be altogether palatable to those in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>SUNDAY CLOSING HOURS.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY CLOSING HOURS.</p>
        <p>In estimating the effects of a restriction of the hours of opening, police statistics of drunkenness may be usefully called to our assistance; for other purposes, as a criterion of the amount of drunken- ness, for comparing one place with others, or even with itself at different periods, they are almost use- less,because of the innumerable varying conditions, other than a varying amount of drunkenness,which affect them. But when we are comparing one place with itself at the same time, that is, one hour with the next, or one day with the next they are of value for comparative purposes, because from one day to another the disturbing elements, personnel of the police, regulations, &amp;c„ do not vary. In Ltverpool from 1886-95 statiscics were kept of the days of the week on which arrests were ma I ; the totals were as follows:</p>
        <p>Sunday 6,155 Wednpsday ... 10,280 Monday . 17.233 Thursday 9.101 Tuesday 12,916 Friday .... 10,360 _ Saturday ... 35,784.</p>
        <p>It will be seen that the week falls into two periods, one of greater drunkenness, Saturday to sionday inclusive and one of less, the remaining four days The drunken period is naturally the period of wages in the pocket and greater leisure NOP7 Sunday falls in the centre of the • drunken period; there is more leisure than on any other day and more wages in pocket than on Monday, the second most drunken day,and yet the arrests for drunkenness are less than on any other day. It is impossible to avoid the conclusion that this is due to the fact that public houses are open for only 6 hours instead of 17. Similar results are shewn by other place, especially Manchester, except that there the Sunday drunkenness is proportionately greater, but still not so great as on Saturday or Monday. The figures point in favour of further restrictions on Sunday, even to complete Sunday closing. It is further argued that a complete break in the week's drinking would be very beneficial, that, if necessary for house consumption a supply of bottled beer can be laid in, that it would be a wel- come rest to publicans and their servants, who need a holiday on the seventh day as much as other men and women, and that Sunday as a day of leisure affords peculiar facilities for unnecessary drinking. On the other hand it is said that beer will not keep fresh, that laying in a store on Satur- day night would lead to increased drinking then, and that Sunday drinking would break out worse than ever in the homes, in clubs and in illicit drinking dens. Now complete Sunday closing is the law in Scot- land, \Vales, and all Ireland except 5 large towns; it will be instructive to glance at its results there.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>nEsuLTs IN SCOTLAND, WALES, IRELAND.</head>
        <p>nEsuLTs IN SCOTLAND, WALES, IRELAND.</p>
        <p>In Scotland complete Sunday closing was insti- tuted in 1853. The same dreadful propbectes were made, but the results have been beyond all expecta- tion good, No section of the community is in favour of repeal, which the publicans themselves would bitterly resent. The effects on arrests is nothing short of wonderful. The Dunde-3 arrests for all offdnces on Sunday average 2, on other days 10; in Aberdeen during a period of 7 years the total arrests for drunkenness on Sundays numbered 12, as against a total of week-day arrests of 2327. In Ireland Sundly closing dates from 1878. Where it is properly enforced, the results are nearly as remarkable. Though there are many special circumstances tending to prevent its proper enforcement, there is no wish for its repeal; on the contrary there is a strong agitation for its exten- sion to the 5 towns.</p>
        <p>In Wales Sunday Closing dates from 1881. There is no dispute as to its success in rural Wales. In Cardiff a great struggle took place. The conditions in Cardiff are as bad fts can be imagined for the suc- cess of Sunday Closing. It is a seaport with a large floating population, accustomed to break all laws, not the licensing laws only, and it is situated close to the Monmouthshire border, w(lere Sunday Clo- ing is not in force, and where a strong opposition was organised to make it fail. For a long time there was a great deal of illicit drinking in clubs and shebeens, but now the forces of order have triumphed, and Sunday Closing may be pronounced a success even there.</p>
        <p>Probably Sunday closing could be applied in many parts of England as BucceB3full7 as in the sister kingdoms, but in others the state of public opinion makes it advisable to proceed by degrees. Complete Sunday Closing should be extended to Monmouthshire, which is geographically a part of Wales. Elsewhere the hours of opening should be at once cut down one hour in the morning and two in the evening,and the reformed licensing authority should be empowered to make orders annually re- newable,further reducing the hours of opening.even to complete closing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>Next Week:</head>
        <p>Next Week:</p>
