      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>GAS COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>GAS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this Committee held on November 19%, the Engineer's monthly report was read,' from which it appeared that the lamps in Ashton-road bad been fixed, and that the same were lighted on the 16th i est.—The report of the Engine Boiler and Employers' Liability Insurance Company. Ltd., on an examination of Nos. 1 and 2 boilers at the Gas Works was submitted, from which it appeared that the boilers were in good or der.—l7, was resolved that the tender of Messrs W Neil and Sons, of St. Helens, for the supply of sulphate plant for the sum of £6OB 10s be accepted on condition that the plant when delivered is paved as satisfactory by 'he Gas Works Engiaeer. and that all unsatisfactory. plat is to be returned and accepted by the contractors' the contractors to supply full particulars of tbe plant and to &amp;liver and fix the same within three months from the date of the order. That proceedings be taken against William Smith, jun., of Johnson-street, Tyldes- ley, for the rec)very of the cost of making good the damage caused by him to a street lamp.--At a subse- quent meeting by the Committ.e it was decided that the lamps in Bridge-street and Market-street be fitted with incandescent burners. The minutes were confirmed. WATER COMMITTEE—THE RECENT STOPPAGE.</p>
        <p>This Committee recommends I that the tender of Messrs John Slee and Company for the removal from the gas works and re-fixing at the water works of the 5 ton cart wtrighing machine, for the sum of £4 lOs be accepted, and that accounts amounting to the sum of £1,597 7s 61 be passed and rccpmmended to the Finance Committee for paymont. At a meeting of the com- mittee on the 19th ult, the report of the sub-committee, which stated that the chairman had been authorised to engage an expert to examine and report upon the pumping engines at the water works, and that the expert be paid a sum of £5 53 for so doing was read and approved. In moving the confirmation oi the minutes,Mr. Borron expressed the regret of the committee at the great in- convenience caused in the neighbourhood by the stopp- age of water during last week. The contractors' atten- tion was called to the matter and they at once remedied it and he believed and hoped that such an occurrence as the breakdown of two pumping engines would not again take place. Steps had been taken by the Com— mittee to prevent any possibility of a stoppage happen- ing again. • Mr. E L Bent seconded and the minutes were passed</p>
        <p>IGTIWAYS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee it was resolved that the tender of Mr F T Bennie, amounting to £2lO for the construction of a sewer along Vista-road, be accepted ; also that the tender of Mr F T Bennie, amounting to £1,042 2s Bd, for tho execution of works of kerbing, channelline, and making good of Athol-street, be ac- cepted. The Clerk submitted to the Committee memor- andum of agreement which ee had received from Messrs Watts and Carr, the Solilit ors to the Roman Catholic Bishop of Liverpool :led others with respect to the giv ing up of land fsr the in aking of tbe road from Crow- lane to Wargrave-road. It was resolved that the pro- posed new road between Crov,-!ane and Wargrave•road be called "Victoria-road." The correspondence with Mr A C Leslie and Messrs Houseman and Co. the Agent and Solicitors to Lord Newton, with respect to the encroach- ment on Newton Common, was read, also litter from the Common Lookers, and the present position of mat- ters explained to the Committee. It was decided that tile matter be referred to a cub-committee consisting of the Chairmen of the Committees and Councillor Smith. A. circular letter from the Registrar-General inviting the co-operation of the Council in the taking of the ap- proaching census, was read,and it was resolved that the Clerk inform the registrar if births and deaths for this District that the Council will be pleased to authorise a part of their staff to officiate in the caoacity of enu- merators. The Clerk read a letter which he had received from the Medical Superintendent of the Warrington Fever Hospital with respect to the admiesion of a patient suffering from scarlet fever who had since developed acute mania, and stating that the extra expenses which would be incurred would be charged to this Council. Oa the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. Gilmour, the minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>GENERAL PIMP ISES COMMITTEE-THE PROPOSED ALTERATIONS AT ME STATION.</p>
        <p>The Chairman moved the adoption of the minutes of this committee, at a meetirg of which, the Clerk re- ported that he had received a letter from Messrs F Leslie and Co., Solicitors to the South Lancashire Tram- ways Co., applying for permission to post street notices along the roads where it is proposed to liy tramways, and had granted the required permission on condition that the Company took all Tem lnsibility therefor, and his action in the matter was approved. Mr. Evans called attention to the proposed alterations at Elrlestown Station- He said that so long ago as March 14Th, 1898. correspondence having taken place witn the railway company with respect to want of accommodation at the station, the directors had decided to carry out alterations in a very short time. He thought it was quite tinv that somethin4 was being done in the matter.</p>
        <p>Mr Gilmour said that from an intimation he had re- ceived he thought the alterations wonld soon bl pro- ceeded with. It was very desirous that there should be a booking office at the entrance of the station. The Chairman: lam very glad that the matter is being discussed as it is of great public interest. I think it might be an instruction to the clerk to write a re- minder to the company. Mr Borron said he believed that some steps were being taken to carry out the alterations, and the Company were progressing with the plans. He sympathised with Mr Evans that there weie cawies for complaint, but they were about to he remedied, and he understood tia che work was in a very forward state. (Hear, hear). The matte: then dropped.</p>
        <p>TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION</p>
        <p>It was reported that the 611111 of £BO 101 had been received by the Technics.' Instruction Committee, being the first instalment of the grant for nchni.Al instruction purposes. At a mef tin of the committee it was rFsolverl that the council bi recommended to pass a chrque for that amount to the Viaduct Technical Institute. This was agreed to. The Council then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>POLICEMAN SAID "MOVE ON."</head>
        <p>THE POLICEMAN SAID "MOVE ON."</p>
        <p>" A policeman once saw me standing cross-'egged in the street, and told me to move °rebut when I ex-leinel to him, he helped me home." So said Me George Clark of Long Row, South Shields, a retired seamen. The in- cident referred to was on an occasion upon which he was suffering agony from piles, and the representative of the law thought be was purposely obstructing. " For thirteen years," says Mr Clark, "I suffered from this ail ment. Indeed, it was because of it I had to abandon a eee-iariog life. That was six years ago, and I went to take charge of a miners' ball and institute in connection with the Brancepeth Colliery. Still I was not a bit bet- ter. A year ago I came to South Shields, but unfortu- nately I was worse than ever. In fact. during the past twelve months I was only able to work ten days ! My condition was terrible. I have fallen on my knees in this very place, wishing T were de .d,and nobody can tell the cruel agonising pain I have suffered. One night the pain was so bad I had to get out of bed and sit in a pail full of water to ease me- self. About a month ego I was so ill I said to mv wife I was on the point of death. By a strange coincid- ence about that time a pamphlet was thrown into the slice) and I picked it up. The first thing I read was the case of a !stay who had been cured of piles by taking Charles Fnrde's Bile Beans for Biliousness. I deter- mined to give them a trial, and with great difficulty I dragged myself to the shop of Messrs Mays and Ce. in Market Place and here I procured a box. As soon as I got home I took some. About three hours later I felt relief. I said to my wife. ' The pain has gone ' ; and she replied Thank God for that! ' The pain went away like a shot, and I slept better tnat night than I had done for menthe. N aurally I went on tak- ing the Beans, and this (producing a box for the inspec- tion of the reporter who was interviewine him) is the sixth I have had. They absolteely cured my complaint, and seem to have made a new man of me." Charles Forde's Bile Beans for Biliousness. which cured Mr Clark, may be obtained from any chemist or frona the Bile Bean Manufactuling Co , 119. London Wall, E.G., by sanding prices, one and three halfpence or two and nine per box. The cure of Mr Clark is probably OOP of the most sensational which has ever occurred in the Shields.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-12-07_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>MAD FREAK OF A ST. HELENS COLLIER.</head>
        <p>MAD FREAK OF A ST. HELENS COLLIER.</p>
        <p>At S. Helens, on Saturday, before Councillors J B Leach and J Green, John Coyne, a young collier, of 3, Taylor-street, pleaded guilty to beiNg drunk acid dis- orderly and smashing two panes of gl ISO and doing dam- age to confectionery, &amp;a., to tbe eAtent of 254. Police- constable FiHingham said that at half-past cloven the ntevious evening prisoner was drunk and disorderly in Elamsbridge-road. He ordered him off, but Coyne ran from the middle of the road and smashed the window of Mrs. iWainwright with his fists. He cut one of his hands and wrists. Mrs. Wainwright, widow, said she did not know prisoner, and he had no cause to attack her premisos. Prisoner, who said he did not remember,was fined 33s altogether, with the alternative of twenty-one days' hard labour.</p>
