        <p>THE WAR. LATEST TELEGRAMS. Significant Rumour. Boer Surrender Believed Possible. It is believed in certain quarters favourable to the Boers, says the "Standard," that they ere anxious to treat upon reasonable terms for a com- plete surrender. Holland's Welcome to Mr. Kruger. THE HAMM, TIII:7IISDAY. At the opening'of the si.Vng of the Second Cham- ber of the States General, Mg. Gleichmao, the Pre- sident, said : Gentlemen, State-President Kruger will arrive to-day. I take the occasion to propose to the Chamber to authorise me to welcome, in its name, the State-President to our c)untry. and to offer him an expression of cordial sympathy. The Chamber adopted M. Gleichman's proposal with cheers and cries of "'Bravo." The Illness of Captain G. R. Crosfield. A telegram has been recently received from Cape town, conveying the welcome intelligence that Captain George B Crosfied (son of Mr. John Crosfield. of Walton Lea) is progressing favourably from the attack of enteric lever. He is connected with the 77th Company Imperial Yeomanry,</p>
